      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>FOR RLWTIMPtIERY McDOUCALLS SELF-RAIS</head>
        <p>FOR RLWTIMPtIERY McDOUCALLS SELF-RAIS</p>
        <p>£296,000. The only item the Government intended to interfere with was the first item. (Hear, bear.) In future that money would be used for secular purposes. He believed the disestablishrnent and disendownient of the Church in Wales would strengthen her moral fibre, and he quite believed her own members would find it possible- to support her better both spiritually and financial y under Disestablishment. (Applause). Mr B S TowsrtoE (1., Chatham) said most of them felt that political matters must be subordinated to questions of religion. (Hear, bear.) There was a great difficulty in front of the country and that could be best overcome if members of the Church of England and members of;tbe Free Church worked together. The measure which was being brought forward was likely to embitter existing relationships and that in itself was a great danger. Ile regarded the Welsh language as a tremendous possession for the British race and the Welsh literature was a wonderful heritage. It was a Welsh Bishop who translated the Bible into the Welsh language, and members of the Church had also trans- lated the hymnal and revived the tisteddfod. The greater number of the clergy in Wales spoke Welsh According to the claims put forward by Free Church- men, their numbers in Wales worked out in the propor- tion of 10 in 21, and they included those claimed in certain cistricts which were actually in excess of the population. If they took away the endowments they were handicapping and hamstringing the work of religion. He suspected the Liberal Party in that matter. The whole movement for Welsh disestablish- ment arose from the Oxford movement in the Church of England. Mr Gladstone, however, had never brought forward any vindictive measure such as had been outlined in the past few months. The Liberal Party did not touch that measure until they were in the deep sea of Home Rule and in difficulties I with Mr. Redmond. One ought not as a fair minded man to support su6 a measure. At one crisis of the world's history a majority was found to vote for Barabbas, end Barribbas was a robber. (Applause) Mr. S. A. H. MACKEY (C., N. Fermanagh), said if they disestablisheo the Church in Wales they were going to reduce Wales to a pagan nation. (Cries of "Oh.") They were ending the nstional acknowledge- of religion. The endowments had been left by pious churchmen in the past and they had been left on the understanding that they were to be wed for the purposes of the Church. If they took away those endowments and used the money for other purposes. they were not fulfilling the trust that bad been placed with them. (Applause). Councillor HANN-THORNE (The Chancellor of the Exchequer) said the endowments since 1662 were retained under the Welsh Disestablishment (A V 01( E : " All wrong.") TbA CHANCELLOR : They are retained by the church. A V 01( E : What Bill are we discussing ? The CHANCELLOR : Mr. Asquith's Bill and in that Bill it was proposed to retain the endowments since 1662. After the Church of England took the Welsh Church under its wing it had retained the tithes that came in for that Church. (Shame !) If the Church would fight as strongly for Wales as it fought for the shekels it would he far better for this country. (Hear, hear.) After 40 years of Disestablishment the Irish Church was growing and increasing its prosperity. It would he better for Wales and better for this country when the present system was done away with. (Applause). The House was then adjourned until Tuesday next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>WELSH DISESTABLISHMENT AND DISENDOWMENT.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance of members at the meet- ing of the Warrington Debating Society on Tuesday evening when an interesting discussion ensued on the following resolution :—That this House welcomes the statement of Mr Asquith "That a Bill for the Disestablishment and Disendowment of the Church of England in Wales shall be given such a position in the programme of legislation for 1912, as will enable it to overide the Veto of the House of Lords during the present Parliament." This House frilly recognises that the withdrawal of the patronage and control of the State from the Episcopal Church is absolutely necessary for the social harmony of the people and further expresses the opinion that in the interests of justice, religious equality should be granted to Wales. This House further affirms that after an equitable satis- faction of existing interests the national property now devoted to the use of the Church shall be devoted to national purposes. The PRIME MINISTER (Mr. E. Wynne Jones), in submitting the resolution, said that they had no quarrel with the Established Church of this country—(bear, hear)—and they hoped that the discussion on the Ruestion of the Disestablishment of the Church of England in Wales would be raised far above party strife and religious animosity. (Hear, hear). With him the question of the Disestablishment and Disendow- meet of the Church in Wales was no question of any Petty quarrel between one religious sect and another. With him it was a question far more important than even State control of the Established Church, or even State patronage ; it was a question of what was just and right. He believed that in the interest of religion itself and social welfare of the nation the question of the establishment of one church in preference to any other should be settled once for all. There should be a complete abandonment of any inter- ference on the part of the State in the question of religion. Irrespective of any religious belief, or of no religious belief at all, every citizen should have equal civil rights. The Welsh Church —the ancient British Church—existed before St. Augustine visited this country. The Welsh Chnrch had fought for years not only against Canterbury but against Rome itself. Its independence existed for centuries, as Mr. Asquith had said. From the time St. Augustine landed on this shore there was probably a Bishop of St. Davide. A series of events took place during which the people of Wales were conquered and annexed to the Kingdom of England and by that annexation the Church in Wales became incorporated with the Church in England and the English nation did with the Welsh Church what they attempted to do with the Welsh people. ;They denationalised the Welsh Church, and for centuries sifter that the English Establishment of the Church in Wales—the old Welsh Church transformed by English rulers—was used for political purposes. In fact she became the organ of the English Government in Wales. From the time of Henry 11. to fifty years ego, the great places in the Welsh Church were occupied by people who were absolutely out of sympathy with the Welsh people and Welsh sentiment. He supposed the conquering of the Welsh Church by the See of Canterbury took place in 1115 and they appointed to the Bishopric of St. Davids a Norman foreigner—Bernard.—(Shame.) Walsh Nonconformity was the direct result of the policy of Canterbury in regard to Wales. The friend of Canterbury was the enemy of Wales and vice versa. Welsh Nonconformity was the direct result of the revolt of some of the finest and best spirits in the Welsh Church. It took place not en any doctrinal differences but because they objected to a political organisation governing the Welsh Church. They objected to Wales being governed from a political standpoint instead of a spiritual one. Nonconformity was in touch and in sympathy with the great mass of people in Wales at the present time. They desired the success of the Established Church because that success was their success. If the Established Church failed, then they failed. The accommodation in Wales for the people at the present time. (A Voice : Take the endowments). The PnistE MINISTER : We do not want the endow- ments, the Church of England took those and they had no right to. (Hear, hear.) Ile said the Free Churches made provision for 74 per cent of the population and the Anglican Church for 22-7. On the basis population, the Established Church had DO right to claim to be the National Church at all. Three-quarters of the popula- tion belonged to the Free Churches and one quarter to the Anglican Church and the rematnder were bona-fide travellers. (Laughter). ll] the interests of democratic government and in the interests of religious harmony the demand for Disestablishment should be granted. Ile did not think anyone could deny that there was a solid demand for disestablishment and disendowment of the Church in Wales. A VOICE: Will you refer it to them The PREMIER : If ever a referendum has taken place, it has been in Wales. The three Conservative members who represent parts of Wales at the present time have the great majority of 326 between them. Without the plural voter there would not even be three members. He could not understand bow the Church of England—proud Church asishe was—considered the present position consistent with her self-respect. (Hear, hear.) The National Church in the real sense was the Free Church in Wales. For forty years the Welsh people had said that they did not want the Anglican Church as their household chaplain. In regard to endow- ments, he saw that Mr McKenna had been pointing out that only £19,000 of endowments had originated since 1002. The tithes in Wales were not simply the gifts of pious men or those with a particular love for the Church. They were the creation of law, and could be dealt with by law. (Hear, hear). In England something different could be said in regard to the question of tithes. Originally they were levied by the State and she had the right to deal with them as she liked. The endowments were in the first place given to the Church and were intended for the people as a whole and not for the special advantage of one sect. The Anglican Church at the present day treated the Church that once had this property, as heretic and ido'atrous. They condemned its creed, despised its practices, and yet they retained tke property. (Hear, hear). In conclusion he would say that they lived in a free Commonwealth, and yet under existing ecclesiastical laws they stood pledged to support asystem which their consciences condemned. They wanted freedom, rights and justice, and that meant as far as Wales was concerned, absolute religious equality.. (Applause). The HOME SECRETARY (Mr Mark Howarth) seconded Mr. PETER DAvENPORT(C. West Salford) moved a number of amendments negativing the resolution. He would have liked the Prime Minister to have gone on further and given some more explanation with regard to the disendowment of the Church. The leaders of the agitation did not think the Disestablishment portion of the Bill worth fighting for unless accompanied by die- endowment. The Prime Minister said if one section of the Christian Church failed it affected the whole. The object of the resolution was to weaken the Church in Wales by taking away its endowments to give them to something for which they were not intended, and to take them away from an object of religion and devote them to secular purposes. That would do much to cripple the Church, and it was certain that without endowments the poor parishes could not afford to keep the clergy whose services were available at present for every parishioner. (A voice: " I dont know.") He did not see bow the Established Church interfered with the civil rights of any individual in Wales. The Nonconformist bodies were not united, except on that point and they united only because they thought they could cripple the Church. The Church of England was admittedly the strongest religious body in Wales, and as such he main- tained bad a right to be the Established Churcb,whether there were advantages or disadvantages from having Establishment. The nation should have some open expression of its religious life, and it was only by means of the Established Church that expression was given effect. (A VOICE : eNo, no.") They would do incalable harm by taking away that means of expression. It would make it appear as if they were putting religion at a discount, and people would not be so inclined to follow religion when they knew the State was doing all it could to nnderrnine it. (A VOICE : "It has not cione in Ireland.") He strongly disapproved of any attempt to disestablish the Welsh Church, because it was a stepping-stone to the Disestablishment of the Church in Bog land.To disendow the Church in Wales was nothing less than robbery and to devote the money that had been given to the Church to secular purposes was nothing less than sacrilege. (Applause). Mr MARK lAA:WORTH (Home Secretary) was glad to believe it was the intention of the Government to intro- duce a measure for the Disestablishment of the Church in Wales during the present year. The consideration of the effect of disestablishing the Church in Wales natur- ally divided itself into two branches—the ethical and the financial. The income of the Church in Wales came mainly from four sources :—(l) Endowments prior to 1660 ; (2) After 1660 ; (3) English contributon ; (4) Freewill offerings. In 1906, the year when the Royal, Commission went into the question, from the first source they estimated £180000; from the second. £20,000; third, £60,000; and free-will offerings</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>NATIONAL INSURANCE.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL INSURANCE.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER LIBERAL WARD MEETING THE SOUTH MANCHESTER RESULT.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Liberal Association held another public meeting in explanation of the National Insurance Act in the National Schools, Church-street, on Wednes- day evening. There was a fair attendance, Councillor C Jackson Holmes presiding. The CHAIRMAN said that in view of the statements , which the Conservative party were circulating through- out the country it had become absolutely necessary that they should have a clear and distinct explanation of the effects of the Act. He referred to a Tory placard, a copy of which was exhibited in the room, representing the British workman as contributing his 4d a week to the Exchequer while Mr Lloyd George was handing out £4OO to a member of Parliament—the inference being that contributions to the insurance fund would go to the salaries of M.P.'s. That state- ment was entirely incorrect, for no portion of the salaries of Commissioners or officials—let alone of M.P.'s—would be taken from the workman's contribu- tions. (Applause.) The whole of the amounts paid into the fund by the workmen, employers and the State would be devoted to insurance. (Applause). 1%1 r. J. JARMAN referred to the Liberal defeat in South Manchester. There had been a tremendous amount of controversy with regard to the position of ware- housemen and clerks and he understood from Mr. New, who was in the constituency on the election day, that a large majority of those classes of workers voted Tory in opposition to the Insurance Act. They pointed out that some clerks were paid wages during sickness and therefore they did not consider it necessary to be compulsorily insured. Many of them were members of the Association of Warehousemen, Travellers and Clerks which undertook to give fairly heavy benefits during sickness, considering the rate of contributions. The rules of the society, however, provided—or at any rate they did when he (Mr Jarman) was a member—that if a clerk was irs receipt of wages he had no claim for sick pay. He (Mr Jarman) was also refused payment of his medical bill by the society because the only doctor in the district where be resided was not on the society's books. It had been pointed out time after time that where it was the custom for employers to pay wages during sickness nothing in the Act would interfere with that custom. He supposed, however, that the clerks were afraid they would have to pay something and not get any particu- lar benefit for it. Hence the result in South Manchester. What somewhat irritated him (the speaker) was that on the very day that polling took place in Manchester the Association of Warehousemen, Travellers and Clerks issued a circular to their members stating that they had decided to become an approved society under the Act and giving a reduced scale of charges and an increased scale of benefits, owing to the fact that the employer and the State would contribute their share. They said they had not issued it earlier because they did not want to influence the election. If thly considered that was fair fighting, he (the speaker) did not. tApplause.) The Association of Clerks, like every friendly society, would be in a better position under the Insurance Act than ever before. It was not the first time the Government had been in its present position. The same vehement opposition was raised by those who had to pay land and super taxes under Mr. Lloyd George's Budget. A large number of consulting doctors resided in South Manchester and he was told that they were practically all opposed to the Insurance Act and that their cars should be used to convey voters to the poll. The amusing thing was that the doctors were corp.. plaining that they were not going to get a certain amount when it had not yet been actually settled what they should receive. When the doctors demanded about twice the rate that was generally paid for club work, their claim certainly required investigation. It would have been fairer to the workers if the friendly societies had let their memberi clearly understand that they were not going to be called upon to pay twice over, under the Act. He hoped the Act would be put to the proof at the earliest possible moment and then the people would realize its benefits , and the Government would be able to remove any defects which were shown to exist. (Applause). Mr. HAErtv NEw also dealt with the clerks' position under the Act and said they had no right to assume I that employers would no longer pay wages during I sickness. There were 15,500,000 people in this country who in the event of sickness were soon faced with poverty and practical starvation. The clerk who opposed the Act took up the position that rather than sacrifice some little pecuniary benefit—and he was only assuming that—he was content to see a continuance of the present condition of things which placed so many people at the mercy of charity or of the Poor Law Guardians. The Liberal party had to take a broader view than that. (Applause.) At present the country was in the throes of an industrial upheaval and deadlock because, owing to the rise in the cost of living, the demand for higher wages had become an urgent necessity. The miners were asking for a minimum wage and until they got it we could never expect the country to resume its normal peaceful conditions. People must have the necessities of life The Insurance Bill would, by helping to remove many evils which beset the workers in their daily lives, tend to make the country happier and more contented. Mr. New also laid great stress upon the beneficent proposals of the Act with respect to maternity benefits and the treatment of consumptives, and said the consequent gain to the national health would be enormous. He also pointed out that the Act would do a great deal to sweep away slum property. In another reference to the industrial dispute, Mr. New said that while be would rejoice at the immediate enactment of a minimum wage, be realized that while it would be comparatively easy to pass such an Act, it would be quite another matter to compel the masters and men to work under it. He believed that many of the Tories were rejoicing because they believed the Government was in serious straits with regard to the labour question. There was, however, no stronger man in the House of Commons than Mr Asquith—(applause)—and if any solution of the problem could be found, he was the man to bring it to a successful termination. (Applause.) Let them continue to raise the moral tone and physical conditions of the people, and they would get rid of the disaffected spirit which was at present prevalent throughout the land. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Sankey Bridges Adult School. Last Sunday morning, Mr. J. Hurst gave an address on "The development of the balloon." The lecturer described the different kinds of balloons invented during the past two centuries and the methods adopted to make them rise. in the air. The paper caused .an interesting discussion, many suggestive points being raised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>SUFFRAGETTES RAID ON THE SHOP WINDOWS.</head>
        <p>SUFFRAGETTES RAID ON THE SHOP WINDOWS.</p>
        <p>The West End of London last Friday night was the scene of an unexampled outrage Of the part of the militant suffragists. Bands of women marched along the principal thorough- fares smashing windows with hammers and atones hidden in muff,s and under cout9t 140</p>
        <p>attack was carefully organised and occurred with startling suddenness. The damage is estimated at several thousands of pounds. Altogether 124 arrests were made. Our pic- ture shows some Suffragettes sitting on their luggage outside Low-street Police-court,</p>
        <p>...::-.4,0'...:&gt;V1Y;;-'...•:*"... ' •" ' -..5.:•-',..:tie '5- '';... .....,,....... • ~...,.., .N.:.'-'7'.`•1.:„...:,:: ~. ..„, .4.-, It. . ..,,.....,-.., ... , . , . . ~...imiii., i:.• ,-' ..,. -' . $ s''' I-• . ,—. ~_. .....' 0.• f,;:f. - ' . ' . .-- .."4 m ....„„, f,..„..., .., ~...• . ... lc :.....„4„;•••••4... ... ~ 1 ..,.;.. , , : ' . 4 ' .. 0101, ,AO,- -,-. ...,,x.. ... ....,§41.....:•,,,..a.,6 • . . . ~,..,...,4,. , ........• • ;• .....0..,,...,..,4.. &gt;4Ol , ..,...,..,'••i•-, . ', ,:0...‘..,......vk , ~,... . .. ~..,:•,,.....-.,-,,,., '.i. - ',... ...,,,' - ,' --.- ' '.. • ir.'" `...;[';.'t.: ' . . . ' ~..,ii. . -...." '''. . . '' 1 A I ,e,.., . ' ~,..„•............::::•'.'...fp:•', , ~. : .;,;.'. . . • . , -..-;.?„ ...° „'.• 4- ?, v --. r . ...,',,,q.,..., .- - , .. . 2 . • - ..,,..;‘,....; , ~:..,.•,:..... . 4,...-, ...-4g...... , . . ...• - ~. . .. , . .. , 41..... ~ :,.....7 . -...4t,;... ,-;-.•-.:.m..1:,,., . ~... . - ...,......., .--. ~... :- : • ; : • • ••••• - , . .. . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>of the education authority for the past nine years, he believed they had rnade</p>
        <p>IMMENSE PROGRESS. While they did not satisfy the critic who asked for a competent book-keeper at 5s a week or an expert craftsman at the mature age of 13, they were turning out of the schools boys and girls with treater resourcefulness, adaptability and intelligence than their predecessors. At the same time they must admit that they had not 3et reached the ideal. It was only lately that education authorities and teachers bad been given a free hand in working out their ideas. They had been tied down by red tape to certain specified ways of teaching and by the most cast iron regulations. Those methods must be adopted in every type of school from the worst slum school to the school that was attended by the children of the skilled artisan, the clerk and the tradesman. A teacher was given sixty boys with different physiques, will powers, and brains, and he was told There is your raw material. At the end of a specified time you must produce sixty boys who have reached the standard of educational attainment, who can work exactly the same sums, read and write in exactly the same way,and who have an equal knowledge of history, geography, English, drawing and manual work." The poor fellow ' attempted the impossible but in many cases, by vehement labour, be was able to produce fifty lads who reached the requisite standard. The men ' who got hold of those fifty lads accepted them as the normal condition of affairs and did not call heaven to witness that they had secured boys who were intelligent and resourceful, while the men who got hold of the other ten boys who had been failures in the school cried from the housetops that the boys did not reach their expectations and immediately jumped to the conclusion that the whole of our education was a ghastly failure. One or the great critics of the day was Sir John Gorst. He had an unequalled opportunity of improving matters because for many years he was the responsible Minister for Education. During that time, however, he did absolutely nothing to modify the cast iron system which had been proved by experience to be such a failure. What was wanting in our education? Sir John Gorst said we were turning out from our schools to-day</p>
        <p>A NATION OF CLERKS and poor clerks at that. What he asked was that our education should be of a practical nature. One of the last movements in educational method was to make education practical and that movement owed nothing at all to the initiative of Sir John Gorst. It began with the teachers who had realized that the motor activities of the child might be developed and used in the training of his brains and intelligence. Those methods he (the speaker) believed to be good. On the other hand Mr. Ramsay Macdonald said that what was called practical education was the , most unpractical of all forms of education. In conclusion, Mr. Upson again put in a plea for criticism based on knowledge and said that unless education was going to be a disastrous failure we must RO resolutely along those lines of development and Improvement which experience had already taught us. (Applause). Miss MA DAMS dealt with her subject in a very com- prehensive, lucid and interesting manner. She said the most important question touching upon citizenship was the real education of the democracy—the people who were to govern our country in the future. We were proud of many things in England but the most patriotic of us must feel that certain things could well be improved. If we were going to improve them we must do so largely by gaining knowledge. We must begin a real system of ri4tional education. The educa- tional world had been fluttering owing to tbe appear- ance of an article by Sir John Gorer, telling us that, in short our system of national education was a failure. Her view was that in England we were only just beginning the glimmerings of a real system of national education at all. It was not the case that education had proved itself a failure. The fact was that we bad gone to work in a very haphazard way and were only just beginning to realise that if we were going to do a thing properly we Must have some clear ideal about it. Having traced the evolution of our educational methods from medieval times, Miss Madams passed strictures on the system of cramming and pointed out that until comparatively recent times the child was looked upon as a mschine for earning grants. During the lsst few years, largely owing to the efforts of men like Professor Sadler and others and to the fact that the workers were waking up to the import- ance of the question and had made up their minds that they were no longer going to be content with the second best for their children, we had improved enormously. She was a teacher and naturally she saw the defects as well as the improvements, but, speaking perfectly honestly, she had seen during the last nine or ten years an enormous change coming over our system of elementary education. When she began teaching the inspector was looked upon as an enemy—he simply came to find fault. They still suffered a great deal from cranks but on the whole the attitude of the Board I of Education in that respect had altered extremely and the inspector now came into the school as a friend We had also begun to train our teachers. Above all. the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>PERNICIOUS SYSTEMS</head>
        <p>PERNICIOUS SYSTEMS</p>
        <p>of payment by results and annual examina- tions bad been abolished and instead we had a system of inspection by people who came to look not so much at what a child could produce in a mechanical fashion,but at the general tone of the school, and to see whether children were being trained to use their faculties properly. Happily we had at last got into our educational code a definite statement that the object of education was the formation of character. Before we</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <p>- _____'</p>
        <p>SPIRITED CRITICISMS.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he wished he could take Miss Madams to the Council Chamber on Tuesday and let her give an address to the Town Council. If the Co-opera- tive Society could only arrange that, it would be one the finest achievements they had ever wrought. (Laughter and applause). Mr. J. JARMAN said he was of the opinion that our educational system was not what it used to be a good many years ago—it was not turning out as good a class of boy to begin work. It might be the idea not to turn out ready made clerks, but that was not what all employers were asking for. From his personal observa- tion, he believed that boys and girls of 13 and 11 were leaving school and beginning work with far less elementary education, as we knew it a few years ago. They were able neither to read, write nor sum. Of course the concern to which he referred might be getting the odd ten out of the sixty—(laughter)—but he was speaking from actual experience. They got boys who could not write a decent hand or read ordinarily well. They might be able to do algebra but they cer- certainly could not add an ordinary arithmetical sum. That was where, it seemed to him, education was lack. tag. His society had found it necessary to run elemen- tary classes for nearly six months for lads who were supposed to have left school partly educated. That did not sound well for our educational system. They did not expect to be provided with clerks but they did expect to get boys who were able to carry on every-day work, and they were not getting them. Perhaps the methods in Warrington were at fault or the employers expected too much. Mr. B. FARRAR pointed out that one great difficulty was the many different castes of mind with which the teachers had to deal. He considered that home lessons were often a hardship upon children after they had put ins day's work at school. Mr. G. T. RUSHTON expressed dissatisfaction with a system whereby it was possible for children to be moved up several classes in the course of a year and then spend several years " marking time " in standard ex-7. Mr. E. ENGLAND asked whether it was necessary under the educational code that children attending school should do home lessons at all ? In many cases it was a real hardship, besides being injurious to the physical welfare of the child. The increasing outlay on education had to be faced, for it would be repaid in days to come. The CHAIRMAN said the senior inspector for the dis- trict, in consultation with the Director of Education ' and tho head teachers, had suggested that there were I many children in the elementary schools who might legitimately be expected to do home lessons—especially those in good homes. It was, however, entirely optional to the parent as to whether home lessons were done or not. Neither the inspector, the Education Committee, nor the teachers could make the work obligatory. Miss MADAMS asked whether the boys referred to by Mr Jarman showed ignorance of elementary subjects when they had just left school or after they had been out fora while?</p>
        <p>Mr. JARMAN : When they have just left. Miss MADAMS Then I don't quite know what is the matter. Of course, I believe we are sometimes trying to do a great deal too much in a limited time, and</p>
        <p>CROWDING TOO MANY SMATTERINGS OF THINGS into the curriculum. (Hear, bear.) Miss Madams further suggested that if it were true that children could not work sums as they used to do, they were trained to do very quickly other things which they could not do in the old days. She did not agree with home work at all. It often amounted to cruelty in poor homes. The CTIAIRMAN said he did not know what Mr. Jarman based his statement upon. The greatest danger of all assertions of that kind was that people were apt to found an argument upon a few isolated instances and apply it generally to the whole body of children leaving school. After a teaching experience of over thirty years in Southampton, London, Cheshire and Warrington, he unhesitatingly affirmed that the boys and girls turned out of our schools to-day were better than those who had been turned out at any time since 1870. Why was it that in some instances Mr. Jarman got hold of boys who were incompetent and unable to pass a satisfactory test of their work? In the first place because of out</p>
        <p>WRETCHED SOCIAL SYSTEM. Some of the brightest brains were to-day being devoted to unskilled labour. It was nonsense to talk about the equality of opportunity nnder our society as it was organized to-day. Many of the most promising scholars were condemned to lives of hopeless drudgery, instead of having their finer faculties trained for their own advancement and the service of their fellows. They all condemned in the strongest terms the moving up of children on non-educational grounds simply and solely to make the school a grant- earning machine to the IngLest extent. At the same time they need not be so emphatic in their condemna- tion of the mere moving up of children. They could not say that all scholars must necessarily remain for the same length of time in one class. It was possible so to arrange the time table that the scholars In the highest class would have fresh work to do. At present, con- cluded Mr. Upson, those engaged in education were rejoicing in their new liberty and they 'were not aocustonied to ft. Many teachers had been so trained in the old groove that they had not begun to use the liberty which the Board of Education permitted.</p>
        <p>St. Paul's C.L.B. The monthly church parade of this company was held on Sunday morning. There was a parade state of 60. and the officers in charge were Captain Hensball and Lieutenants Bunch and Henshall. The Rev J H Bankes was the preacher.</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>NO FLOUR</head>
        <p>NO FLOUR</p>
        <p>IS EDUCATION IMPROVING?</p>
        <p>LOCAL CRITICS OF MODERN METHODS.</p>
        <p>BOYS WHO DO NOT KNOW THE "THREE R'S."</p>
        <p>Some candid criticisms of modern educational methods were forthcoming at a meeting held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Saturday evening to discuss the question of " Education and the Citizen." The subject was introduced by 'Miss Madams, of London, and in the course of the subse- quent debate it was asserted that the product of the 1 elementary school in these days does not compare favourably in useful attainments with the boys and girls who left the top standard in years gone by. Mr. J. Upson presided over a small attendance. The CHAIRMAN said they in Warrington were faced with a rising educatiin rate and that always produced 1 a host of critics. No one objected to criticism r s long as it was aimed at disclosing weaknesses and suggesting remedies,but they had a right;to ask that the critic should have some knowledge of the subject he criticised, and some other motive other than that of merely reducing the cost of education. Our educational methods were admittedly not yet perfect. Like everything else, education wk s a thing of revolu.l tionary growth, and that groa th had be€n proceeding for a considerable number of years. They acre told that the product of our elementary, technical and secondary schools was very unsatis- factory, that our educational methods did not make boys and girls, on leaving school, competent to take their place in life's battle. Speaking boa' consider- able experience as a headmaster and as a member</p>
        <p>got a system of education on national lines we would have to make it a great deal wider in its scope. We had been too apt to let educa- tion cease at the age of thirteen. A really good system would provide the best possible training for every child, every adolescent and every adult, and we should see, not a ladder—she hated the idea of an educational ladder—but a big broad highway leading , from the elementary school right up to the university for everybody who wanted it. Miss Adams put in a strong , plea for better training in the laws of health, medical treatment as well as inspection of school children, , instruction on the practical side of house-work, and training in the rights and duties of citizenship. They must also teach young people to make profitable use of their leisure, and then they would be able to do away with the stupid idea that when they lessened the hours of labour, people would otly spend a little more time in the public-house. Even the picture palaces, bad as they mere in many respects, were opening the eyes of children to new circumstances of life and to the wonders of nature. We must have the he,t, teachers we could possibly get; it must not bee case of "anybody will do for a teacher." The real teacher was born, not made. The school age most be raised, and after the age of fourteen or fifteen there must be a system of compulsory continuation schools—not necessarily evening classes. The technical training for the occupations of life must be gken at a later stage than at preQent, when it was possible to say for what avoct tion the youth was best fitted. Having pointed out the weaknesses of the scholarship system, Miss Madams advocated a system of secondary education which would embrace every child end enable it to specialize. She regardi ditas a hopeful sign that. 2,100 workpeople were at present attending tutorial classes and attribute,' their increased interest in erucalon largely to the stro3g desire to learn more about social conditions uod way things seemed to be so much out of joint. The old idea of education for the sake of individual benefit was slowly giving way to the newer idea of education for 'be sake of education itself and that, in turn, was rapinly being superseded by the still reater idea of education as a means of fitting us Err service. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
