        <p>TESTING TIIE EGGS. On the seventh day of incubation the eggs should be tested for fertility, and all addled and clear egits should be removed from the incubator. You can purchase an appliance for this purpose, or you unil, make one with very little difficulty. Take a piece 01 cardboard and make an aperture in the centre to an egg. Thus you have got an egg tester whieb will well set ve the purpose. By placing the egg in the aperture before a strong light (in s dark room of course) you are able to test the fertility of the ccntants. 'infertile eggs apPO,l perfectly clear, and these can be kept, for when belle' hard they make good food for the chicks. Almost the whole contents of an addled egg appear as one dar mass. If, however, there is any doubt, they can 11 tested later on. These should be specially market' before putting back into the drawer. On the twelfd) day the eggs should be tested again.</p>
        <p>THE TWENTY FIRST. On the twenty first day the chicks will commence to make their appearance. When they emerge frocn the shell and after they are fairly dry, remove them te the drying box and remove also the broken shells' The chicks should remain in the drying box br least twenty-four hours before having their first noes', of hard boiled egg and bread crumbs. Of course you have customers for newly hitched chicks then the aooner they are dispatched the better ; seeing that 1"e food is required for the first twenty-four hours, the. have a /good chance of reaching their destination before getting hungry. However, this question cso be dealt with when we talk of the day-old chicks' trade.</p>
