      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>EiZi-X-rt ++ + ERMECZEIMEICEigg I PRACTICAL I 1 POULTRY FARMING. I fti Etgf.oM4l-4511-EMEMMEIE-MoECalaniKal</head>
        <p>EiZi-X-rt ++ + ERMECZEIMEICEigg I PRACTICAL I 1 POULTRY FARMING. I fti Etgf.oM4l-4511-EMEMMEIE-MoECalaniKal</p>
        <p>[BY REDCAP].</p>
        <p>TAINTED EGGS.</p>
        <p>Some people think that because the contents of an egg are enveloped in shell, that it is impossible for them to become tainted. This is not so. In point of fact one of my readers had rather an unpleasant experience at breakfast one mot fling not long ago. He like many another sensible man thought that his ' rasher ' of bacon would be improved if accompanied with a nice fresh duck egg. The egg in question was certainly a new laid one but was by no means Ii esh— as we understand the word. Upon examination he found that the colour of the yolk was almost black, and upon investigation he found that the ducks had access only to sewerage water. Plenty of water, but in a filthy condition ! He at oace concluded—and rightly so—that here was the cause of the discoloured egg. Foals or ducks that live amongst dirty envirrns, and who have access to dirty water only, are bound to give very unsatisfactory results in the flavour of the eggs, not only so but dangerous results too. Still further, there are other points to watch, the egg shell being porous, care should be taken to store them in a place free from poisonous gases. If my readers would care to prove this argument, let them place a few eggs in a vessel together with an onion cut in halves, then boil one and notice the flavour for themselves. And although fish is a very good food for poultry, if given in too great quantities it will undoubtedly affect the flavour of the eggs. Plenty of fresh water for your fowls to drink is the best safeguard against tainted eggs. Many of our farmers are most careless in this direction. i have often seen fowls in a farm yard quenching their thirst—through sheer force of neceselity— at the water lying round a manure heap, and sometimes where the water I has lodged between the stones in the yard after washing the dairy utensils. Surely it is an easy matter to replenish the water trough say twice a day. A horse or cow would absolutely refuse to drink dirty water, then why should the fowls be forced to do so ? The best flavoured eggs are always got where cleanli- ness and proper attention to feeding is given. When purchasing eggs, these questions should be asked. It would have a marked effect en the better management of poultry. Farmers would begin to think, and I am sure also to act as well, for I am perfectly certain that a little boycotting of the egg trade in this matter would not do harm, but good.</p>
        <p>INDIAN GAME. From appearances the Indian Game Cock may be termed the' bull-dog "of the poultry world. If you care to call him handsome, then he is just as useful as he is handsome. If you care to call him ugly, then he is just as useful as he is ugly. So he wins both ways, and rightly so, for there is no breed of poultry which iu more exten- sively used—than the Indian game—in crossing with other breeds for table purposes. With the exception perhaps, of Dorkings and old English game, whatever breed you cross with the Indian game, you improve their qualities as table birds. They carry a minimum of feather and an abundance of good quality flesh, especially on the breast. The Indian game is not only useful for culinary purposes, but often enough</p>
        <p>From the " Feathered Warld." the laying properties of the female progeny— from the cross-mating—is improved. I remember once reading of a breeder of poultry who had had excellent results from a first cross between Indian game and Dark Brabmas. He wrote, that the pullets from these matings had proved to be first-class winter laying specimens. And I know from personal experience, that the Indian game—Buff Orpington cross ?s one of the best winter-laying crossbreds in the field. You will remember how prominently quoted the Indian Game was, in my list of first crosses for table. They are, indeed, a most useful fowl, and if you desire a change of blood in your stock-with a view to improving their table qualities—give the Indian Game Cock a trial. AR'l If [CAL MI:RATION. The management of an incubator is not difficult pro- viding you purchase a good machine. Always remember that the best is (in the long run) the cheapest. The first point to consider is the place to run your machine. This should be in a room free from vary- ing temperatures and free from draughts. The floor of the room also should be solid, an earth flour or cement is the best. Too much vibration affects the egg germs. Haying thus selected the room, read carefully all the instructions sent out with the incubator. See that the capsule is in W orkin order. This can be done by holding it in front of the fire. If after doing so it commences is bulge out, you can re-t i assured that t s alright. The thermometer should also be tested by using a clinical thermometer ; see that they both register the same temperature. Having satisfied yourself on these two points, you may com- mence to fill the tank with hot water, fill the lamp with oil, and after the machine has been running for 112 hours, adjust the regulating chimney rod so as to keep the temperature at 102 to 103 degrees. The machine should be run at this temperature for ar3ut two days, then you may place the eggs in the drawer, and keep the temperature as near to 101 degrees as possible throughout the whole time of incubation.</p>
        <p>DAILY Arri:NrioN. After the eggs have been in the machine for twenty- four hours they should be turned night and morning, until the twentieth day. Before doing so, however, they should be marked with a X on one aide; this enables you to observe with Ease that each egg is turned. For the first week the eggs should b airea for five minutes, the second ueek ten minutes, and the remaining period about fifteen minutes. Moisture should also be added but not until the twelfth day of incubation. This should be done by simply damping the canvas in the egg tray, and keep it damp for the rest of the period but do not overdo it. On the twentieth day, take a piece of red flannel of sufficient size to cover the eggs, soak it in warm water and then wring out the water. Then place the damp flannel over the eggs, close the drawer for fully ten minutes and then remove the flannel.]</p>
        <p>THE L 1.31 P. Don't forget to fill and trim lamp each day, always keeping the flame at the height, or as near as possible.</p>
