        <p>-- It takes Warrington and cup-tie football for creating surP,ises. Here are the Wirepullers safely in the !'hird round, and with excellent prespects for appear- Ing in the semi-final. A. week ago their chances 'were not considered worth a " continental," but the remarkable draw effected at Hull altered the whole situation, and the re-play was well won on Wednesday. As I said in this column last Saturday, "It's dogged as does it," and Warrington's chatacteristic cup-tie 'doggedness pulled them through the second round when all hope had almost iled. The feat performed at Craven-street, Hull, on Saturday, was one of the ?,I°l4idatiest in Warrington's long history as cup- Bravo Wirepullers ! -- b?(ltiling succeeds like succes., and the Fates have e`gl'a to smile again on Warrington. The draw for 4%third round gives them choice of ground with fo,.a"field Trinity as opponents, and you can take it Rranted that the match will be at Wilderspool on arcla 23rd. 't'he locals have waited two years for a '(.1:11Le ,draw, and if you offered them Wakefield hi --`ural they would not play elsewhere than on the areterie Patch of turf behind the church. The Trinity of tjaa4eady counted among the slain, but the prospects at match we will discuss later. Personally, I th•eak the Wakefield men will play better football than 413st0n Rovers, give Warrington as hard a fight, .of .Provide a better match from the spectators' point tlew than, the one on Wedneaday.</p>
