roold VI their superior pace behind, I am sure "atte won in handsome style. The ---- '`ttotte end Ives obtained just beforn half-time—and the ,ne result of that speedy following up for whi[nlin Nra'',d°noct brigade in the Warrington front rank i Play Zl_fanzle• Then the Wire pullers set themselves to illargia"ene "—a very risky policy with such a narrow 4,,atkay n",,,3 Points only. One single fluke or break- 05k3' 4 , 'lll, and the match might have fluke, or was only saved hy the merest squeak, and ' warrington would h,ave done far better to have toe an attacking game. rhey could have increased tteor !3t I am certain, and it cannot be too often Vtn,„lneed that the best defence is attack. Don't i'Vtt }sssany more sitting tight on a solitary try. "eart 101* the heart—particularly the spectator's W ---- oUli-r'onton adopted the same team formation as at itt(t the local crowd had a chance to see bow b bolit;" Worked. Jordan was a little hero in both is , for z;nsne, though he is the wrong kind of half back ingt ~.,tiliß the thr quarters going. The style of rtaY '4Oll ee initiated however, just suited the War- ,thotni:lll°.nlerfoney man. Renwick was in great form, b Is cleverness seems to have limited scare at Liekena:k' 89d I prefer him among the attackers. was the sound tactician he always is in • and young Daintith did many fine things. ;41.1;cl? Wati quite a failure at centre—though he tattler at null—and Waywell also shaped very tilanft„TallYt though none of the wings had much ' '0 shine. The forwards were once more the 135 fart of the side, and in the loose they will not I'4ole "Nod the best. They were too speedy and lit for null's more beefy brigade. ilnte --- and a ,3 one thing Warrington ought to :htakein an ot eettle finally before the next cup-tie, a 'our k,"!,e question of goal-kicking. There ale about tttc kioZ4 g°al-kickers in the team; but none of them ;'l,elrov., goals for want of practice. Either Renwick, ‘Qe re'n-et "r Woodburn should be practised and given fatt,i°lln44ed position of place-kicker. Thomas has oar, BPropensity for missing "sitters " under_ the Z.,3q•apa,,WituogB Wednesday and the cup-tie at I,\ igan ° arnerl a c t'n reliable neat-kicker can ntatl,n-ri-, and when found there should be none of nther,"nr,ohazard Rh oot og by first one and then the il'ettnee. 4116 team has gone on the wrong lines in this over since has gone We have goal- entrea,34,ill the team • let one and not four of them, be wi th this 'vitally important duty. The Lea table up-to-date is as follows : For. Against. Per- Wit,uerwid..,Pn• LD.aT. P. G. l'‘,; P. C'lll!.,q! , . 3 1...107164 700... 00 3; 11'1 Z4-el * uldh`s- ttt ** .ti 4 4 0... 70 87 401... 31 2 49 tla t• 041. . 17 ;8 0... 82 77 395... 30 39 147 75.0_0 "elern; ** 7 1... 67 97 425... 43 49 233 70.01 18 8 7 0... 50 82 358_ 05 42 196 6666 ... 1,, 9 1... 42 06 372... 52 48 248 6481* . "" 9 10 71 313... 48 65 291 6200 . 4.141nea . 2 14 O_ 5.. 40 44 212... 22 29 131 6086 37 47 216... 19 36 146 6041 ilz,:abur;;;:Z: 1" 2::: 48 75 321... 27 47 195 601)0 441i'RI3tOa.';',. 15 10 1... 51 76 336... 42 58 272 59.61 1... 42 53 243... 31 37 173 58'00 tt.4s. .... ••.. •-;! 15 12 2... 50 80 340... 35 57 241 55.17 .... ... 11; 0... 49 75 323... 39 63 267 5200. loll' 42 53 4357 5023 224663 55140 *011.2.6 11 13 2.... :tj r 16... 42 53 243 46.15 .. ... .. 912 I_ 37 '42 200... 50 60 280 43.18 "7„..' 8 12 .34 44 200 .. 39 69 285 40,17 14 3.• 37 47 ' 13 4. • 215 31 30 17; ... 4427 4549 227161 3470:5308 cora"--2 2 7 ti . 2l 37 5• 7 73 333 34'09 t6t;;slltrt, .2 516 21 32 13.... 4• 354 248 25.00 4.7'1.itr:2 42 211. 23: 29 98 14'2: 74127 529 10-23 17121 106_ 63 93 405 15.90 12 24 2... 19 24 110— 80 133 559 8.33 „Wa, -3 0.- 27 29 141... 71108 466 8.00 vißit -I.'ngton resume League football to-day with a taa ce of kughton, and they should have a fair tentViring point from the Rangers. The enwic : Mclntyre, full back ; Waywell, 54k and Woodburn, three-quarters ; ar,"Zl Jordan. half backs; Thomas, Shugars,