      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Winn 22 St. Helens A ... 10 NORTHERN UNION CUP. Second Round.</head>
        <p>Winn 22 St. Helens A ... 10 NORTHERN UNION CUP. Second Round.</p>
        <p>Warrington V. Hull KR.</p>
        <p>Wirepullers Create a Surprise at Hull. It was a determined, but not very confident band of Warringtonians which left for Hull on Saturday morn- ing.At the last minute Brookes and Bradshaw were both found to be unfit for the match, and Jordan and Waywell made the journey. The loss of Brookes was a serious matter, and luck seemed to be fighting against the Wirepullers. At Craven-street the Rovers entered I upon the contest with every confidence and a full team, but received a nasty set back. Warrington played as if their lives were at stake and finally equalised the scores after having far and away the best of the game. The weather was dry and the ground in capital order, buts strong wind blew from end to end. The crowd numbered 8,000, and the receipts were £197. Teams and officials:— _ _</p>
        <p>Warrington.—Jordan Waywell, Mclntyre, Dickenson and Woodburn; Daintith and Renwins ; Thomas Shugars, Cheaters, Skelhorne, Tranter and Fearnley. Itovers.—A Carmichael (captain) ; J C Brain, R Hughes, Thomas, and R S Ascott ; T Surman and I L Dean; A Mann, A Moore, W Sandham, W Hushing, .1 Blackmore, and P &amp;amen. Referee, W MoCutcheon (Oldham); touch-j udges, E J Kilvington (Leeds), and A Brown (Wakefield). Warrington won the toss and started with the wind at their backs, a piece of luck which had a marked etTet.t on the game. Early on there was a succession of 03(1118 by the home backs, who appeared unable to field the ball accurately. The breeze proved most perplexing, and upset early ideas of progression on the part of the home players. Jordan esseyed a drop at goal in the first minute, but the ball passed wide of the posts. Following this a penalty against the Rovers for illegal tackling by Dean saw Thomas take a penalty kick in a favourable position, but again Warrington were denied points. A free kick was awarded to the R)vers, and Carmichael's kick enabled the Rovers to reach their opponents' preserves, but Dickenson's return kick was so finely assisted by the wind that the Wirepullers were quickly on the attack again. The Warrington forwards scrimmaged keenly within a few yards of the home line, but another free kick relieved the home line very considerably. Shugars, Cheaters, aud Tranter headed a dribble back to the Rovers' 25, where Carmichael threw himself on the ball, saving at considerable personal risk. Brain was instrumental in removing hostilities, and he made good headway before being pulled down by Woodburn. Dean was repeatedly prevented from making progress behind the home scrummage. Daintith had quite the measure of the usually elusive Welshman, and the home spectators had the rare experience of witnessing all the efforts on the part of their favourites kept in check. Warrington were very smart behind, and a beautiful piece of combination by Daintith, Renwick, Mclntyre and Waywell covered three- quarters the length of the field, and left the home defence well beaten. At the critical moment Way well pabsed inside to Mclntyre, who raced over finely, only to hear the referee's whistle for a pass forward which was denied all round the ground. Even the Rovers' supporters admitted Warrington's hard luck in having such a great effort negatived. As usual, the other side 112ade headway after Warrington's temporary dishearten- ing. Sandham led up to the assault by rescuing his side when closely pressed, and Dean helped the move- ment along brilliantly from a subsequent scrimmage. The ball passed through the hands ot several of the home team before reaching Ascott. and the young Somerset three-quarter raced over with a clever try in the corner. Carmichael was unsuccessful in adding the goal points. Warrington quickly bore down to the home line again, and Dickenson proved en awkward customer to bring to grief in the neighbourhood of the home line. Phil Thomas succeeded in the effort after several others had failed, and the home forwards throwing more vigour into the scrnmmages gradually I reached the Warrington, half. half-time: Kingston Rovers, I try (3 points) ; Warrington, nil. On turning round to face the wind, Renwick and Jordan changed places—a move which was amply justified by the results. After an early effort by Surman, which was checked in the Warrington quarter, the Wirepullers made headway. The home forwards were scarcely able to get going with their old-time rushes, being kept surprisingly well in hand by their opponents. Brain repeatedly endeavoured to get away on the right, but anything he attempted rarely materialised. Ultimately the visitors applied severe pressure end Renwick and Daintith ran and passed so smartly that Way well when in possession succeeded in equalising with a clever try. Mclntyre took the goal kick amidst much excitement and made a magnificent shot. which was only deflected in the last yard by the wind. This position of affairs lent additional interest to the game, but leading points appeared like being gained by Sandham in a race for the ball near the War- rington line. Renwick, however, was a capable full back, and he saved courageously. Another forward rush by the visitors was further helped by a succession of blunders on the part of the Hull backs, until Carmichael came to the rescue. Waywell contributed another dashing run, which nearly brought about a score. At close quarters, however, the Rovers did not hesitate to tackle in the most approved style. Another scare was provided when Shugars and Cheaters raced along with an apparently clear opening, but Carmichael contrived to get up and save the situa- tion. Just before the end Carmichael tried to drop a goal, but Dickenson charged the shot down. Final : Kingston Rovers, 1 try (3 points) ; Warrington, 1 try (3 points).</p>
        <p>pOUND. THE GLAD EYE—and it was Mother's. No wonder she was pleased. She had just proved what a blessing Steedman's Powders are to Parents. Nothing else keeps children so fit.</p>
        <p>the crowd in joyous applause. The red-jerseyed Hull men were set to face the wind and sun, and hostilities commenced with a powerful punt to the home quarters. The Warrington forwards shoved their keenness by rushing the first scram, Skelhorn and Cbesters forcing play to the Hull end. Here the referee stepped in with a penalty, Carmichael putting in a lofty punt which Waywell failed to return, and the Robins got possession at the Warrington quarter. Hughes made a powerful dash through the Warrington backs, but was finely tackled by Daintith, and Jordan cleared with a strong punt to centre. The locals could make little real headway, as penalties were rather frequent, and Carmichael had n shot at goal from near the centre but the famous back found the range too long. Skelhorn again led the forwards in a speedy dribble to the Hull custodian, who was collared close in amid an outburst of cheering. Before Warrington could press home the advantage, however, they were again penalised, and the game swung back to the Warrington end. Neither side could make much headway from the scrums, the spotting was so keen and the tackling deadly. Eventually a fine punt by Daintitb found touch well in the Rovers' quarter, and the locals were not dislodged until they had taken the lead. Dairitith got Carmichael in difficulties with a speedy dribble' and the full back's return was well met by Jordan, who punted to the line. Fearnley and Tianter were bandy and downed Carmichael before he could clear. The ball came loose, and Tranter pick- ing up, handed off Hughes in a strong run which csrried him over the line near the posts. There was a hurricane of cheering to greet Warrington's success after 33 minutes play. Thomas took the easy goal-kick, but made a sad mesa of it by sending the ball onside the posts—two points thrown away. The Rovers resumed with desperate energy and Warrington were gradually driven back by smart touch- play from Dean and Surman. Carmichael had another try for goal hut failed to carry the long range, and then came an anxious moment for Warrington. A punt by Dean caught the Warrington defence napping, and Way well failing to clear Hughes dashed up and seized the ball five yards from the Warrington posts. He was promptly tackled and lucidly threw forward over the goal-line as Surrnan rushed up and touched down. But for the throw forward W rrini ton would probably have been in arrears. The home forwards quickly clesred from the next scrum, and a brilliant run by Woodburn was cheered to the echo. Fearniey followed up speedily and took the inside psss cleverly a yard from the Hull line, but was overwhelmed by Phil Thomas. Warrington were still hot on the attack when the whistle sounded for half-time, with the acore, Warrington 3 points ; 1 Hull nil.</p>
        <p>Though they held the lead, \Varrington could claim little advantage on play so far, and a desperate struggle awaited them in the second half. Renwick and Jorda n changed places to strengthen the defence, but the opening movements were in the locals favour. Jordan got the ball out to the backs, but. Mclntyre mulled a simple pass, and the Rovers' were quicky on the attack. Brain raced down touch and kicked across for Haskins and Sandham, who had a grand chance in front of goal. lhe latter fielded the ball—hesitated a second—and was downed by Renwick when the situation appeared lost for Warrington. There was a warm three minutes near the home line, and Surrnan tried a drop at goal, which wasl only a foot outside. Daintith again broke through in gallant style with the ball at his toes, but Carmichael punted back to the line' where Dickenson saved finely amid a crowd of Hull players. Excitement was at fever beat. but ‘Varrington graduslly forced the Hull men back with grand forward play, and Waywell ran the ball speedily into the Rovers quarter. Here a penalty to \Carrington improved matters. and Thomas found touch in the corner. The locals carried the scrum in brilliant style, and Jordan was nearly over, Dean finally punting cleverly to the half way flag. The Rovers persevered by short punting along the touch line, and got near enough for Carmichael to have another try for goal, but success was denied him. Renwick changed the scene with a fine run and punt which put Warrington on the attack. Treater fielded close in, and a score was certain if he bad passed to Woodburn, but the for- ward tried to double inside and was held—a bad error of judgment. Warrington now adopted safety tactics, and the Rovers seemed to lose their heads, with the result that the hall was rarely in the home half. Brain made a float dash just on time, hut was s rn. in t ouch by Renwick. and the whistle sounded with %Carrington winners of one of their keenest straggles for years by one try (3 points) to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>NORTHERN LEAGUE. WIDNES v. lIUNSLET.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN LEAGUE. WIDNES v. lIUNSLET.</p>
        <p>Plated at Widnes, before 4,000 spectators. The open- ing stages were in favour of Widnes, the three-quarters being prominent, but Farrar intercepted, and then lost possession. Hanslet now became aggressive, and some pretty passing was shown, but each time they became dangerous the Widnes forwards by good dribbling brought relief. From a scrummage on the Hunslet line Smith got possession, but Noon intercepted his pass, and easily ran over behind the posts, Jones improving. NV. Batten got away beautifully, and after beating a number of his opponents passed to Farrar, who, however, failed to take the hall. Half-time :—Widnes 1 goal, I try (5 points), Hunslet nil. Widnes attacked early in the second half, and smart work by Noon almost ended in a try, but J. Batten made dead in the nick of time, 3enkinson made a bold bid to get in at the corner, and from the subsequent scrummage about of passing by Smith, W. Batten, and Ferrer ended in the latter cross- ing at the corner. W. Batten failed at the place kick. Result : —Widnes 1 goal, 1 try (5 points), Hunslet 1 try (3 points). ST. 'HELENS v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>This arranged friendly fixture attracted a fair crowd to the Knowsley-road ground. Planagan,nicely served, Tan over at the corner for St. Helens, Shallcross failing. Greenwood dashed away, but when threatened with opposition he banded to Waddell, who scored, Turtill failing. Runcorn livened up and Butterworth, eluding Turtill and Flanagan, handed to Padhury, who crossed at the corner, Butterworth failing. Play con- tinued in Runcorn's favour, and Butterworth scored, and on the interval Lewis dropped a goal. In the second half Runcorn failed to score, but trios were scored for the home team by Wyatt, Flanagan, Mavitty (2), and Greenwood, Turtill placing throe go:ds. 1 Results :—St. Helens, 3 goals and 7 tries (27 points), Runcorn, 1 goal and 2 tries (8 points). -- WARRINGTON " A " v. RUNCORN WHITE STAR. Warrington " A " entertained the winners of the Lancashire Junior Cup in a friendly match on Saturday Soon after the start Clare got over for the horns team but failed to ground the ball, and afterwards the nippy juniors held their own. Rushing tactics ,by the home forwards took play to half-way, where Povey was left with only the full back to beat. He kicked over the head of the custodian,bot was thwarted in his efforts to regain the ball. Interval : No score. Runcorn took the field with only twelve men on resuming, one of their Dumber being injured. Warrington had a long period on the attack, and Arnold made great efforts to get through. Clare was often in possession, but the tackling of his vis-a-vis was altogether too much for them. From a scrimmage Grainger got over cleverly, but the position was too difficult for Jolley, and when McCabe scored in the same place the full-back again failed with the kick. Runcorn were by no means done with, and a spirited breakaway was saved by Jolley, and Arnold again took play to the visitors' end, vrhere Jolley missed with a penalty. There was plenty of excitement, but no further scoring. Result : Warrington "A " two tries (six points) ; Runcorn White Star, e.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER LEAGUE. RYLANDS v. MANDLEBURGS.</p>
        <p>This match was played at Gorsey-lane on Saturday before a good crowd. The home team attacked and Leah followed up, but mulled with the ball over the line,but Stretford was in attendance and touched down, Floyd goaling. Jones intercepted on his own line, but progress was soon stayed. Lear made a tine run and passed to Griffiths, who got over for a try. Leah was next prominent with a good sprint,but his final transfer was forward. Before the interval 13roadhurst,Stretford and Riley scored tries, the extra points being added in the first two instances by Floyd. nalf-time : Rylands, 21 points; Alandleburgs, 0. In the second portion play was a little more strenuous, notwithstanding the fact that the home side put on another twelve points. Griffiths (3) and Leah scored tries, the goal-kicks all failing. Final : Rylands, 33 points ; Maudleburghs, 0</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>ALTRINCEIANI DISTRICT LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION. RESCLTS TO DATE.</p>
        <p>Goals.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. P. A. PTS. Mobberley l9 .13 .. 4 2 . 50 ... 23 ...28 Latchford 19 .12 . 5 2... 47... 26 ...26 Bowdon Vale 17 .12 3 2 .. 44 ... 30 .. 26 Lymm l7 .11 . 3 . 3 .43 ... 28 ...25 Lostock Gralam —l5 _ll ... 2 ... 2 ... 83 ... 18 ...24 Broadheath 16 8 .. 5 3 .43 .29 ..19 Oughtrington Am 1S ... 6 ... 9 ... 3 ... 32 ... 29 —l5 St. Vincent's lB . 4 8 6 ... 19 ... 34 —.14 Oughtrington Parkl4 ... 5 ... 7 2 ... 28 ... 21 ...12 Atrincham.Lads 15 .......10 ... 0 ... 23 ... 40 —.le Sale Albion 19 3 12 3.. 28 47 9 Penketh l5 .. 2 ...12 ... 1 ... 13 ... 52 ... 5 linutstord St Crossls 0 ... 3 ... 21 ... 97 ... 3 ALTRINCHAM LADS V ST. VINCENT'S.</p>
        <p>Played at Stamford Park on Saturday before a pond attendance. Final : St Vincent's, two goals Mtrincham Lads, nil. SALE ALBION V KN'ETSTORD ST. CROSS.</p>
        <p>At Brooklands, Lb% final being : Sale Albion, six goals ; Knutsford St Crow'. nII.</p>
        <p>. BOWDON VALE V LYMM. At Vicarage-lane, Bowden, Winning the toss the Vale took advantage of the wind and at once pressed, Atherton clearing from Beckton's centres. Lymm retaliated, a good run by Owen and Potter ending in Potts conceding a corner. This was futile and play was carried to the other end. Good work by the home .irivards kept the visitors' defence fully employed. Cho game was vigorously contested all through, neither side asking for or giving quiver. At the interval the score was two goals to nil in favour of Bowdon Vale, Lymm took up the running in the second half, but could not press home their advantage, capital defensive work by Potts, Cash and Hadfield nullifying all attempts. The efforts of Lymm in the first half against the wind began to show effect,and in the end they were easily and well beaten with the s3ore ? Bowdon Vale, four goals ; Lymm, nil.</p>
        <p>I to' ['be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>SECOND DIVISION. LATCTIFORD V. LATCH FORD RECREATrON.</head>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION. LATCTIFORD V. LATCH FORD RECREATrON.</p>
        <p>This tie was played on the ground of the former before a fair attendance. Winning the toss, the Recrea- tion took advantage of the breeze and immediately pressed and were awarded a penalty in the first few minutes, which they failed to utilize, doing likewise with a similar award a few minutes later. Encouraged by this good fortune the home team did better, but could make no impression on the Rees' defence. The first goal fell to the Recreation somewhat luckily, one of the home backs ruiskicking and the wind carrying the ball through his own goal. At the interval the Recrea- tion led by two goals to nil. In the second half, with the aid of the wind, the home team did better and took a more prominent part in the game, hut met a splendid defence, Ditchfield (at centre- half) being the shining light, he being ably assisted by his backs. Although maintaining the upper hand, Latchford could not score until just before time, a well-contested game ending: Latchford Recreation, two goals; Latchford, one goal. HOWLF.Y V. SAICKEY.</p>
        <p>On the ground of the former. Pienty of interest was taken in the meeting of these two Warrington rivals. In their previous meetings (League and unior Corn- petition games) there has been very little between them. On this occasion Sankey had the best of matters and eventually ran out winners by four goals to one.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR MEDAL COMPETITION. FIRST ROUND. "GUARDIAN "JUNIORS V. WIDNES ST. AMBROSE. Played on the ground of the former on Saturday. Final : " Guardian," 4 goals ; Widnes, 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT TEACHERS' LEAGUE. "JAMES DODDS " CHALLENGE SHIELD. FINAL TIE. HEATIISIDE V. WYCLIFFE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT TEACHERS' LEAGUE. "JAMES DODDS " CHALLENGE SHIELD. FINAL TIE. HEATIISIDE V. WYCLIFFE.</p>
        <p>Great interest was taken in this match, which decided the championship. at Arpley, on Tuesday evening. Ileattiside have held the shield for two years and were anxious to keep it. Following on their success in the Swimming Shield Competition,the Wycliffe team were equally anxious to gain possession of the handsome shield. The game opened with even play but soon the VVycliffe half backs completely overwhelmed the oppos- ing forwards and, plying the forwards well with the ball,a score was certain to come. The Wycliffe captain opened the cow° after 20 minutes' play. This was the only score of the match, although throughout the Wycliffe team played the better game. Heathside were only dangerous at rare intervals ; the poor shooting of the Wycliffe forwards kept the score at 1.0. The Wycliffe team were best represented by the three half-backs and the centre and outside forwards. Heath. sides best men were the right-back and right-half. flarrox CLutt v STRETTON YOUNG MEN'S NiUTUAL Played at Stretton on Tuesday. Scores :</p>
        <p>ST R ETTON. J Rippas P Cliff° 4B F Blackshaw W G Dunoalf W W Leach 3 Houghton 75 IsaFtc Hewitt IS C Davies -,S -0 Willett 75</p>
        <p>HATT9N.</p>
        <p>3SB Mayjority for Hatton, 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>The First Eleven last week scored another good win at the expence of West Kirby by 7-2. The West Kirby halves in the first half were weak and consequently the Latchford attack got the upper hand and four goals were scored to which the visitors replied with one close on the interval. In the second half Latchford scored again straight from the bully-off and the visitors' centre scored their second goal with a fine effort, but two more goals were put on by the home team before the end. Dixon at back for Latchford played a polished game and quite touched his true form while in front the three halves worked untiringly and gave the West Kirby forwards little rope. Dale and Pearson did some fine forward work on the right wing the former getting across some glorious centres. The goals were scored by Pearson (3) TrAvis (3) Hackney (1). The Second Eleven match was cancelled by the opposing side owing to their being shorthanded. The First Eleven v Knutsford at Knutsford to-day Is : —H Wright goal ; P Alltinson,T Dixon backs ; F W Isherwood, J B Wilkinson, A Knight halves ; R A Dale, J E Pearson, II Travis,W Fletcher, C Hackney forwards. Bus from The Academy 1-30 p.m. The Second Eleven v Knutsford at Latchford is : It Walsh,W H Pickles, H Eccles, full-backs ; H T Gibbs, A E Bradshaw, T Banks halves : H R Colton, H Parker, E Banks, W H Minards, Pletcher, forwards. Bully-off 3-15 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>RUSKIN AND DRESS. LENGrHs. A LITTLE-KNOWN VENTURE OF THE GREAT</head>
        <p>RUSKIN AND DRESS. LENGrHs. A LITTLE-KNOWN VENTURE OF THE GREAT</p>
        <p>MASTER.</p>
        <p>It will be news to the majority of our readers that Ruskin founded a mill in the Isle of Man Zor the making of cloths for suitings and ladies' dresses. Dis- gusted by the trickery in textiles he started the St. George's Mill at Laxey, where fabrics, the best that could be produced, were manufactured, irrespective of profit. Any reader who wishes to procure the beautiful products of the mill, which includes homespuns, costume cloths, blankets, travelling rugs, etc., should send a postcard for patterns to the Ruskin Homespun, St. George's Mill, Laxey, Isle of Man, or to the distributors. Messrs. C. Holroyd and Son, 110, Station- street, Huddersfield.</p>
        <p>I WILL GIVE YOU 61. GPNUINE TESTIMONY. MISS LILY: ELSIE writes:</p>
        <p>" liTS.eicellerZt.eind most beautifully perfumed, I shall always use it." MISS ELLEN TERRY writes: " It is excellent." MISS IRENE VANBRUGH writes : "Quite excellent and most pleasant to use." MISS JULIA. NEILSON writes: "I like it very much." MISS FANNY BROUGH writes: " Most excellent. Delightfully refreshing." MISS GLADYS HUXLEY writes: • It is the BEST I have EVER used." NOTHING need be added to convince you that THE ELSINE SKIN FOOD COMPLEXION CREAM IS THE 8E51.,. It thoroughly Cleanses the Skin of ALL Blemishes, Softens, Beautifies and Preserns. Price 2s. post free, or cut out this advt., send with P.O. is. 6d. and get the sixpence as offered above. Sole Proprietor— ROBERTS, PHARMACIST, FOWY, CORENWALt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>NATIONAL CROSS-COUNTRY CHAMPIONSHIP.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL CROSS-COUNTRY CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON PROMINENT.</p>
        <p>A large number of Warringtonians made the journey to Ilaydock Park on Saturday last and had the pleasure of seeing the local Athletic Club make a good fight with the all conquering liallarnshire teams who perhaps represent the strongest combination known to the annals of the race and that the local lads should provo themselves the second best team in the country must be extremely gratifying to their friends and Warrington "sports" generally. The Midland champions, Tlarap- stone, confident of finding the individual winner and very hopeful of getting in touch with the holders for team honours.</p>
        <p>The Southern Champions, Highgate, have a splendid record in connection with the lace and fancied their chance considerably for the team honours. The West Lancashire Champions, Sutton, expected to confirm their earlier running and were very hopeful of respect- ing their win of 190 U when Charlie Straw led the field home and incidentally won the team race for the St Helens Club.</p>
        <p>_ - - The holders Hallamshire, however, experienced most trouble in shaking off the attentien of Warrington A.O and endorsed their performance in the "Northern." Individually the holder, F N Hit-thins, was the leading fancy, the strongest opposition being expected from F C Neaves, Southern Cnarnpion and winner in 1910, and the Northern Cnampion, E Glover. But the result of the Midland Championship, with G C Greenway, was verified and of the others only H Holnrook and C Vose, both of Warrington, ever gave the winner any cause for anxiety, and fully merited the recognition of the selection committee for the inter- national at Edinburgh on the 30th inst. 17 teams took part and the 173 competitors presented a pretty sight as they burst away from the starting pens about a quarter mile below the stands. Well arranged and equitable the start more satisfactory than usual, provided a dash at almost halt-mile pace for the first turn, and thenceforward the struggle for places was keen,the ever changing patches of colour being discern- ible for almost the whole of the ten mile course in four laps with about mile of ploughs in each. The first circuit, about two miles, 1,200 yards occupied 15min. 57 3-ssecs. The second and subsequent laps of about 2 miles, SOO yards each took reap ctively 1 irnin. 45 2-ssecs., 14min. 47secs., and 14min 30 4-53ecs. so that .1 E Tolliday of North Manchester who led for about 71,- miles, and eventually finished twelfth, 58secs. behind the winner, was moving a bit. The leading batch at the finish were always nicely placed. Glover failed to reproduce his Northern form, and F C Neaves gradually dropped hack to the 21st position ; time, amin 49secs. W H Begg (Sutton; ran consistently throughout, finishing well, and the selectors may[liave erred in ignoring his pretensions to interna- tional class.</p>
        <p>Last season the teams placing were Hallamshire points, Highgate 105, Warrington IA and many were content to look for a repetition hut after the first circuit the team points were eagerly totalled by enthusiasts. Hallamshire proved themselves a team of talents which would, however, have suffered a reverse without their new man, Askew, who only became eligible this season owing to the defection of his previous club, Woodhouse Institute. It was no disgrace for Warrington to be beaten by such a strong combination and for Sutton to fail by only two points to keep Highgate out of the team plscings, points clearly to the superiority of the North this season.</p>
        <p>)f. S.</p>
        <p>1 F N Hibbins, Thrapstone and Dis. C and A C GO 04.5 2 S C Greenway, Banbury H 6O 29 3 H Holbrook, Warrington A.O OO 36 4 C Vose, Warrington A.O 6O 34 5 J Murphy, Hallaroshire H and A.O 6O 43 , 6 Cll Ruffen, Highgate 6O 45 7 J Hughes, Surrey A.O 6O 46 8 W H Begg, Sutton II and A.O 6O 50 9 E Glover, Hallamahire H and A.O 5O 51 10 W Cottrill, Hallamshire H And A.C 6O 52 11 S Welding, Sutton 11. and A.O ..... 12 J E Tolliday, N Manchester H 6O 59 13 R Stanton, Birchfield H 6l 2 14 E Harrison, Sutton H. and A.C. 15 A Aldows. Hallamshire H. and A.O 6l 14 16 R Askew,Hallamshire H. and A.O 6l 19 17 W Bolton, Warrington A.O GI 20 18 B C Neaves, Surrey A.O 6l 29 19 3 G Coughlin, Higbgate H 6l 39 20 E V Loney, N Staffordshire H 6l 44 21 F C Neaves, Surrey A.O 6l 49 1 22 A Cannington, Thrspstone and Dia C. and A.C.</p>
        <p>23 W R Wright, Surrey A:C 62 0</p>
        <p>,t A Suokariiith., SaMin: H 62 4 25 J T Rimmer, West Cheshire H 62 14 26' T Johnson, Highgate H 62 16 27 A G Roberts, West Cheshire H 62 18 28 A Siddall, Warrington A.O 62 22 129 W_Antrobus,. Warrington A.O ...... _ 62 25</p>
        <p>30 E Skett, BirChfields 11. C2 27 One hundred and thirty finished, the last being J B Robinson (North Manchester), in lb 17min 27sec. Pt,,.</p>
        <p>CLUB PLACING&amp;</p>
        <p>1 Hallamahire H. and A.C. (5, 9. 10, 15, 10, 31) -80 2 Warrington A.C, (3, 4. 17. 28, 29, 34) 3 Highgate H. (6, 19, 26, 36, 37. 40) 4 Sutton H. and A.C. (8, 11, 14, 32, 44, 46) 166 5 Surrey A.C. (7, 18, 21, 23, 51, 82) 6 North Staffordshire H. (20, 35, 38, 43, 52, 5I) 242 7 Tbrapstone and Dist. C. and A.C. (1, 22, 41, 50, 59 74) 97 -._</p>
        <p>8 Hi-rat-leld H. (13, 30, 32, 60, 62, 68) 9 Salford H. (24, 39, 48, 67, 73, 76) 10 Wibrey Park A.C. (45, 63, 71, 79, 86, 91) 11 Herne Hill H. (45, 57, 64, 66,91,121) 444 12 West Cheshire H. (25, 27, 80, 83, 108, 128) 12 Sparkhill IL (42, 53, 65, 95, 96. 97) 14 Bolton United H. and A.C. (75, 85, 90, 98, in, 112) 571 15 North Manchester H. and A.C. (12, 116, 117, 118, 122, 123) 603 *Equal.</p>
        <p>Banbury Harriers and Ravenbead H. and A.C. were unplaced, the former having only one to finish and the latter club five. The Hallamshire Harriers and A.C. have now won the event four times (1008-10-11-12) and were second to Birchtield Harriers over the same course in )909. Last year Hallamshire beat Highgate by 51 points, Warrington being third 95 points behind the Southern champions. Highgate have also the credit of being first on four occasions, viz., 1893-1902 1904-5. _ _ _</p>
        <p>CONCERNING TYIE LOCALS. II Holbrook showed good judgment, and WU a fight- ing factor, and earned the laurels his friends expected him to get last season. C Vose showed a welcome return to form and was an important unit in displaying the strength of the local I men, as both have been selected to represent England. Warrington A.C. enjoy the unique distinction of being the only club to have two representatives. W Bolton ran extremely well, and one heavy fall cost him many positions which quite spoiled his chance of entering the charmed circle, better luck next time: A Siddall set a lively pace at the start, helping to extricate his club from the ruck, and afterwards stayed on to fill a very creditable position. W Antrobus ran as a team man and did splendidly. T Turton showed good form and will improve on this disky. F Antrobus lacked training and did well under the circumstances. F Shadwell run well for a junior and should be in the fighting line next season. J Parsonage did well considering that training operations were stopped at a critical period. II K Jepson, I` Rowlisson and A Gregson all completed the course in respectable positions and earned for their club tho dis- tinction of finishing a complete team.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS AND DISTRICT TEAM RACES. To-day's fixture starting at 3.30 p.m. will comprise a run over about six miles of country in preparation for next Saturday's races at the ground of the Sutton Commercial Football Club. Junior and novice teams of 6 to run and 4 to count and Warrington AC members desirous of being included should take part in to-day's run after which the teams will be selected. INTERNATIONAL CHAMPIONSHIP. THE ENGLISH TEAM.</p>
        <p>After the race for the National the Committee of the National Cross Country Union met and selected the team to represent England in the International Championship to be held at Edinburgh on the 30th inst. The following were chosen: F N Hibbins (Thrapstone), E Glover (Hallarushire), F C Neaves (Surrey)—the three divisional champions, North, Midland and South —S C Greenway (Banbury), H Holbrook (Warrington), C Vose (Warrington), C H Ruffell (Highgate), W Scott (Broughton) and W J Tucker (Reading). Reserves W H Liege (Sutton), R Stanton (Birchfleld) and J W Breed (Durham).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>GHENT EXHIBITION, 1913.</head>
        <p>GHENT EXHIBITION, 1913.</p>
        <p>We desire to call special attention to the Inter- national Exhibition to be held in Ghent next year. The International Exbibi Lions Committee of the London Chamber of Commerce have given cordial support to this exhibition, and strongly advise British manufac- turers to exhibit there. The Committee base decided to at once form an Anglo-Belgian Commission in aid of the exhibition, so as to help in ensuring a display worthy of the greatest manufacturing coun:ry in the world. As pointed out by Sir Albert K itolliit, chairman of the above-mentioned Committee, at its last meeting, the Ghent Exhibition will be one of the largest and most elaborate ever held on the Continent of Europe; it will cover more than 350 acres of ground and comprise 27 palaces, all beautiful in design, and situated in the world-famous gardens of the capital of Flanders. The powerful combination behind the exhibition, consisting of the Belgian Government and its Colonial Office, and the wealthy city of Ghent, has enabled tine Committee of Management of the Exhibition, disposing of nostinted funds, to carry out their ideal of %suet a great international exhibition should be. It will, moreover, be a business organization and market, without the usual mass of side shows which detract so much from many exhibitions in the view of business men. Under the direct patronage of H.M. the King of the Belgians, the exhibition is supported by everyone who is in a position to judge of the advantages it will afford to the manufacturer and trader In calling the attention of exhibitors to this exhibition, the British Consul-General particularly impress es upon them the advantages likely to accrue to persons exhibit- ing in the linen, hemp, jute, cotton, wool, silk and. weaving industries, while weaving machine y of all classes might be shown with advantage. In addition he recommends British firms to exhibit, in the horti- cultural sections, as machinery, and tools in particular, are not of the excellence produced in tne United Kingdom. To this it may be added that the Exhibition is one at which it is essential fur British manufacture's of all classes to be represented ; indeed tuey cannot afford to neglect this opportunity of making a counter- blast to the largo displays whicn are being arranged for by other nations. Germany, France and the United States are amongst the countries which have already agreed to officially represent, and to this aspect of tne. matter, as well as to the unique position of the city of Ghent as the home of the exhibition, we shall refer ma. subsequent article.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>GOOD CASE.</head>
        <p>A GOOD CASE.</p>
        <p>The appreciation of the public, the com- mendation of doctors', and the support of the Press make together a very good case in favour of margarine as a food. Those who have never used margarine before would do well to cast all prejudice aside and just try 'Stork.' by far the finest Margarine obtainable. They will discover, no doubt to their surprise, that .they can detect no difference in flavour or appearance between ' Stork' Margarine and choice country butter. In the certificate of merit awarded for 'Stork' Margarine by the Incorporated Institute of Hygiene, there is sufficient evidence of quality; medical testi- mony proves a standard of food value as high as that of butter; and the economy is obvious—indeed, it means a saving of half.</p>
        <p>FOR</p>
      </div>
