        <p>Another admirable year's work of that most deserv' ing institution, the Warrington Children's Summer Camp, was under review at the annual meeting held at the Old Academy last evening. The report presented bJ Mr WFL J Jenkins (hon. secretary) stated that " tte first note of thankful congratulation must be paid to the exceptional weather conditions of the season t° which much of the success was undoubtedly due. A greater number of children than hitherto have speo,t a holiday in camp during the season—two hundred an" forty-two boys and the same number of girl making a total of 484. Ten children recover"l from sickness were taken to the camp ffi, holidays and rest in order to recruit their streng',': The following ladies and gentlemen acted as otficilw in charge of the children during the season. Those ill " charge of the girls were Miss Bebbingto,ll (superintendent) and the Misses Davenport, Mdociefri, Emson, Ellison and Povey and Mrs Burge. The bd. Mr J J Firth (superintendent) and Messrs Appletw Droarke, Johnson, Lightfoot, Mee, Phillips, Parson and Roddy. The Committee tendered its wartaeli thanks to tt ose voluntary workers for the sustaine,.-, interest taken by them in the work and especially those who at times under trying nil cutnstanceiber' their part with fidelity and zeal. It was not surprisil that owing to the beautiful weather a considerable increase took place in the number of visitors. The` were 179, being an increase of :9 over the precedilt season. That was the largest number since establishment cf the camp end was a gratifYlv; acknowledgment of its usefulness. Little if 0135 - • -</p>
        <p>monetary profit was derived from this source, required increased work and attention from the stß" A pleasing feature of the season was, through tbe pro' kindly forethought of a number of friends, the oh vision of a "Coronation Swing " which was rauko appreciated by the youngsters. Thei r heartfelt were due to a gentleman, who bad sent a cheque 00 - £2l in order to erect a new large hut in memory olojos late mother and to bear her name. That would t e 0,0 in due course and his wishes observed. Tbe Comrait",,f.' &amp;ailed to record its sense of the valuable serviceg :f , the Director of Education and his staff in the work vi . selection; to Mr Charles White for his medicti.:, examination of the children, and to Lt.-Col. Fairclough for allowing the use of the Drill Hall as gs 1 assembly room. The HON. Box. TREASURER (Mr. John A. Ransorne) sAg mitted the balance sheet which showed a deficit of .'", 19s. Bd. as against one of £74 18s. 2d at the beginnio' of the year. • The donations amounted to £5l 15s. 9d. and scriptions £142 17s. Gd. and from the visitors to r"0 camp a sum of £lOO 15s. 10d. was received. "-,„"r largest item of expenditure was £153 6s, 2d. '- provisions. The report and balance sheet were unanimau°ll adopted. —Ai</p>
      </div>
