        <head>SATURDAY MARCH 9 1912.</head>
        <p>SATURDAY MARCH 9 1912.</p>
        <p>When Mr. Asauith told AN When the House of Commons Com,u TO on Wednesday, in reply SENSE. to a question by Mr. the Ramsay Macdonald, that tantrlst(,,ry about the introduction of a Mini- llon "age;4 Bill is a work of pure imagina- that' 1.,"e must not be understood as sayingdeal 'with %,arliament will not be asked to d Illait, 'He Coal Crisis. That possibilityb ere.- , , . and 'd be everything that .m.ay e. said (101113 t "e• But the Prime Minister is, no to , as anxious as anybody. Ise can be the 0i anxious legislation which stror,`"mg out of waters. There t abree like be o:reasons to hope that a settlement wmil! Deratri\r,fl'ed at before this step becomes i The nld s, 7, and even that it is not far off. slowo',ltg about the advisability of hastening Dr ' nas a particular applicability in the. ese,. par . Dress„"' case. The immediate . exertion of Ille ":re would be almost certain to harden IviticilriliTLers into resistance to proposaalsonf. 4blen,lleY Must soon see the entire leso Thrit.:'ss. The offer made by the Uo vern- , reas- 1 11°t likely to be improved upon. Its tilt srableness must become the more appar- 12ave hhe more it is considered. It would uecaliee.ri well to have accepted it at „\\ once, h'e just the scheme of settlement "e ard1.....v any variation, at least, that will litire,,..°tea in the end. Dot sne ntiners' leaders, a fr as we know, had Rreat 4.1 e e hand in the matter. There . cwasa_ \tvill fidenee'lood of exasperated opinion and °n Ilm.v be w °f victory behind them. hat is be-c°llle aPparent—and this we believe -hey n e°llle tbe determining factor—is that DeatTr,e MA, in spite of some spasmodic saeir ci-nces to the contrary. supported in etermination to resist the Government li't is -sals for bringing about a settlement. h - e ^ely ti,Derclived that they would not be at all suer " lose by those proposals. It is fur- the 11.1-ieen with really painful vividness that Nvill b„ners can only hold out at a cost that eorrirn„` .sinaller to themselves than to the i Cr' .nity I. , 111;'' Wlinlarge. Tens of thousands of 11-,ties - nave nothing to do with the coal laerans"tahvaet been throWn out of work. This kit.ie provoitretiii:n of thousands of families, a be °lls t of whom have no Trade back lot Nun . ck upon, have. suddenly eIY • ged into destitution, and it is abso- "4 the "Possible to exaggerate. the gravity t)ne result of the of t . le coal w,,ar. But it is only 1113 si- he distressing features of the present 1-41°‘:resi°tnio AI!L. e.lasses of society, from the 11-111 Y of "e u!ghest, are being punished, is them„ with terrible severity, ecauose. Nyht h thebte.thtewe..,e_nr coal trade a difference t cj I; I irlt.el 0 na temployers and employed, X)(1)e "tPut.N Y e most affects 23 per cent. e us e the word " output " in this case</p>
        <p>with a particular intention, for it is not true to say that only 20 or 25 per cent. of the miners are affected by the abnormal places question. All the miners are affected, because any miner may at any time draw a bad place. But the simple fact is that the workers in the coal mines won a great victory when the Government proposals were made. There is no reason at present to think that they did not win all that they were asking for. If they could have per- ceived this in time they would not only have prevented a great waste of their own money, but would have averted what is already be- coming a national calamity. Their duty is plain, and we believe that the recognition of that duty will coincide with their real interests. They must let the Government be their adviser.</p>
        <p>There is no reason SOUTH MANCHESTER. why Liberalism, which</p>
        <p>looks to the future always, and which cherishes ideas which cannot be hurt by any temporary rebuff, should fear to face any unpleasant incident. Toryism, which lives only on "tactics," is like the valetudinarian who takes his I temperature every half-hour, and almost dies with terror if he thinks he is "not so well." Therefore the result of the South Manchester by-election need not be" explained away " as such an event would have to be if it had happened to the Tory party. Let us frankly admit that the Insurance Act is unpopular where, as in South Manchester, the medical profession and the clerk and warehouseman constitute so strong an element. It is, and will be, unpopular until its benefits are actually felt. And the Coal Strike has undoubtedly lost the Government votes. If there was a devastating earthquake the Government which was in power at the time would lose votes— for the moment—irrespective of its ability and energy in meeting the crisis. But all this is no reason for Liberals to be down- hearted. The opposition lives from' hand- to-mouth." Long views and steady policy will prevail in the long run.</p>
        <p>Steadily, but surely,the THE PURCHASING demand for Feee Trade POWER IN grows in Australia,just AUSTRALIA. as it grows in Canada _ _</p>
        <p>and SOuth Africa. In all these Colonies the exporting producers realise that manufacturers cannot be pro- tected without unfairly affecting agricul- turists and miners, who have to buy their needs at unnatural prices. A recent publica- tion in South Austra!ia, entitled "Free Trade and the Mining Industry," pointed out that, "if the miners saw the real cause of their trouble in the increased cost of their necessaries of life brought about by tariff legislation they would agitate for the repeal of those duties which are oppressing them. This would be equal to a rise in wages of 5s in the pound." And we read in the "Sunday Times "of West Australia, January 14, a letter declaring that " the Government has again been tinkering with the tariff, in the vain hope of staying the clamour of certain protectionist manufacturers without arousing fresh envy, cupidity, dissatisfaction, or resentment in other quarters. . . . Surely it is time the Labour party, which claims to repre- sent the wealth-producers, should abandon a system which can only exist by decreasing the purchasing power of wages." A very shrewd summary of the fatuity and folly of Protection.</p>
        <p>Inspection of the Police Force. On Monday afternoon, Lieutenant Colonel Eden His Majesty's Inspector of Constabulary, made his annual inspection of the Warrington Borough Police Force. Lieutenant Colonel Eden arrived at the Police Buildings at 3 pm. and was met at the Chief Constable's office by His Worship the Mayor (Coun- cillor Dr. Joseph) and Alderman Bolton. The whole force, with the exception of one sergeant and a few constables who were on duty, was assembled in the parade room where the men's kits were arranged in military style. There were present the Chief Constable, Chief Detective Inspector Williamson. Inspectors Forster and Hodgkiss, three sergeants, 59 constables and six plain clothes officers, making a total of 72 of all ranks. Colonel Eden closely inspected the men and their equipment, after which the sergeants and constables fell in as a and were put through a number company in line, of evolutions by Sergeant Hesp, drill instructor, the various movements being smartly executed. It may be remarked here that the local force looks remark- ably well when assembled in a body, the recent marked improvement in physique being particulary noticeable, many of the young men recruited during the last three or four years standing well over six feet high, the tallest being 6ft. 41in. After the inspection of the men and their kits, Colonel Eden made his usual tour of the offices, cells, &amp;c., afterwards examining the books and records of the force in the Chief Constable's office. At the conclusion the Inspecting Officer expressed himself well pleased with all he had seen, commenting most favourably upon the light roomy offices of the police buildings, and upon the creditable manner in which the books of the establishment were kept. Among the many things of which the bcrough of Warrington may justly feel proud, by no means the least is its smart and efficient police force, which certainly can compare favourably with any in England.</p>
        <p>• Ironworks Fatality. The death took place in the Warrington Infirmary, on Thursday, of Ernest Ashurst (20), 15, Oakwood- avenue, Padgate-lane, Warrington. On February 10th he was engaged in shunting operations at Messrs. Pearson and Knowles' Ironworks, Warrington, when he was run over by a waggon, and his legs were badly mutilated. The injured limbs were amputated ten days later, but he never recovered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>The' Examiner, Saturday, March, 9, 1912.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <p>"?KalEN^..e.,,w,.NoTEs AND NO</p>
        <p>The Shops Act comes into force on May Ist next. As its provisions are of much import- ance and interest, and we have had several inquiries as to the effect on local businesses, we propose to give a specially detailed summary of the Act next week. * *</p>
        <p>Your bread will not cost you more— for the present. The threatened strike of Warrington bakers was fortunately avoided, the masters and men meeting to discuss the demands of the Union and both sides dis- playing a commendable spirit of give and take,</p>
        <p>* - We are rapidly re-approaching the never- to-be forgotten travelling conditions which obtained during the railway strike. The various companies are reducing their services by about half, and should the strike continue much longer, they bid fair to reach vanishing point.</p>
        <p>Our readers' attentio-n is directed to a front page announcement of the meeting of protest against Welsh disestablishment. They, will I note that " Churchmen and Christians are cordially invited: The comical conjunction of the two words calls to mind the stale witticism of the man who entered a merry gathering with the greeting "Good evening, gentlemen and others ! " ex</p>
        <p>Any ignorance which may have existed in Warrington on the provisions of the National Insurance Bill must surely have been dis- pelled, for the Liberal Association and Progressive Labour League have conducted a very successful ward campaign in support of this much maligned and misrepresented measure. Almost every aspect of the scheme has been fully explained and the thrifty man who may have been fearful lest his interests should be menaced should 'ere this have been fully convinced that the Act will reward his forethought by conferring the maximum of benefit,whilst at the same time compelling the thoughtless and improvident to lay by some- thing for the dark days of sickness and disablement. * * * The "Sunrise Annual" has received a lot of gratutious advertising this last few weeks, and at only a penny a line the publishers would owe us a considerable sum. But the greatest debt is to Mr. Spencer Leigh Hughes, M.P., who devoted his "Sub Rosa" column in the "Daily News" the other day to a commentary on the article "from the pen of a local alderman." He remarks: "It is with no intentional dis- respect that I apply to him the phrase used by the ungrateful prisoner to the emotional counsel who was defending him—' a dreary bloke.' That is to say, the good alderman is depressed by the course of events in last year, and he looks forward with appre- hension to the future."</p>
        <p>* * "That which Maculay said of Gladstone's style in 1839 may be said of the Warrington alderman also: He has one gift most dangerous to a speculator a vast command of a kind of language, grave and majestic, but of vague and uncertain import. And he knows how to point his moral and adorn his tale by means of familiar quota- tions. At the very beginning he trots out that old favourite, " red ruin and the break- ing up of laws," and this leads him on naturally to certain allusions to Hercules and the Augean stables, together i with a demand for" one still, strong man in a blatant land." This demand is just now being made by many gentlemen who are by no means still, and some of them are not quite free from the suspicion of being just a little blatant themselves."</p>
        <p>* * Alderman Bennett says that trees will not grow in the neighbourhood of St. Elphin's Park, and that the Corporation have wasted £2O in planting them there. Why? Does St. Elphin exercise a malign influence, or have the operations of wire-drawing in the neighbourhood a deleterious effect? There ought to be a satisfactory explanation for the Alderman's dogmatic assertion, because we well-remember Alderman Bolton once saying that grass would not grow in Warrington. Curiously enough Alderman Bolton now says:that "if trees will grow on Wilderspool Causeway they will live in Manchester-road." Obviously there is little wisdom in a multi- tude of counsellors—especially if those counsellors are Aldermen! * * *</p>
        <p>It is a matter of fairly general knowledge that for some time pictures have been offered and sold in Warrington as " Sheffields " and bearing the signature " George Sheffield," which were not the work of the famous Warrington artist. Last •week a young Warrington man named Brunt was sent to prison at Douglas for obtaining money on false pretences by this very trick, and some interesting evidence was given by C3uncillor Birtles, who may be considered an authority on Sheffield's works. Brunt seems to have carried on this nefarious trade to a considerable extent, and we can personally testify that some of his work was sufficiently good to deceive a practised eye. He possesses undoubted talent, and it seems a great pity that at the early age of 21, that talent should have been diverted into crooked channels. 411 *</p>
        <p>Warrington Bridge was a subject for both mystification and merriment at Tuesday's Council meeting. A plan of the approaches and training walls provided the mystification, and only about one Councillor in six appeared to understand which was being discussed. Councillor Birtles was responsible for the merriment by sug- gesting that if mooring rings in the shape of lions' heads were to be struck out on the ground of economy, as an alternative the heads of prominent members of the Council could be introduced in bas relief. The sug- gestion was made in all seriousness, but it produced many chuckles. Whose and what heads would be a nice point to settle. We have heard of the heads of well-known men being set up over a bridge before for public inspection. But in former time they were stuck on spikes !</p>
        <p>* The Member for Warrington, being fleet of foot, had the honour of announcing the result of the South Manchester election to the House of Commons. He just "beat Lord Tullibardine by a neck in crossing the bar of the House." The Manchester Guardian describes the incident as follows "The calm of the debate was interrupted by the sudden inrush of Mr. Harold Smith, the messenger of waving a piece of paper in his hands. He good tidings for the Opposition, announced in exultant tones the result of the South Manchester election, and the usual little demonstration followed; but so few Unionists were present at the time that their cheers did not sound very impressive. The Opposition could not have been confident of victory, or they would have • made arrangements for a more effective display in celebration of their triumph. The stage management was bad, and Mr. Smith's entry was 474, not 51 impressive as that of the messenger in a 47, great tragedy." It seems evident that the Member was better as Macduff, on an earlier occasion and in a greater tragedy</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>Half-penny tram fares will not pay in W arrington, so the Deputy Chairman of the Committee says in reply to a question. Possibly his arguments are irrefutable, but is there any reason why coal-black and oily workmen should not have cars to themselves on certain well-frequented routes which link up the home in town and the workshop on the outskirts? The penny passenger deserves , a little choice in the tune seeing that he pays the piper very generously. . .</p>
        <p>As previously announced in the "Examiner," Warrington will be an important centre for the administration of the National Insurance Act in the North Western district. We are now informed that Mr. A. H. Clive has completed the letting of Messrs. Greenhalgh's shop, Tower Buildings, Bridge-street to the first Commissioner of Works, on behalf of the Government. The building will be utilized as offices in connec- tion with the Insurance Act and about 100 clerks will be employed in it. The ground floor will not be used. The premises have been taken for seven years and are to be occupied as'soon as the necessary alterations are completed * * To-morrow is Hospital Sunday and the need for generous contributions from this important source of the Infirmary's income is exceptionally urgent. The Board of Man- agement of the institution, in their report on the past year's work, state : Congregational collections were £240 15s. Bd., as against £271 13s. Id. in the previous year. The Board regret the continued decrease in the income from this source, and they would respectfully request the Clergy and Ministers of all denominations to endeavour to obtain additional financial support from the members of their Churches. The Board sincerely thank the Hospital Sunday Committee for their valuable help, and trust their efforts during the year 1912 will result in an increased contribution. 0 0</p>
        <p>The marked differences of opinion on the success of our modern educational methods was perhaps never more strikingly expressed than at the Co-operative Society's interesting meeting on Saturday evening. On the one hand Mr. J. Upson, speaking from the teachers' standpoint, declared that during the past decade there had been an enormous improvement in the productof our elementary school system,whereas Mr. J. Jarman, speak- from actual experience from the employer's point of view, said boys were turned out of the schools no w-a-d ays who could not write a decent hand or read ordinarily well, and that, although they might be able to do algebra, they could not work an ordinary arithmetical sum. As Mr. Upson pointed out, some false impres- sions may be created by generalizing from exceptional instances, but there is undoubt- edly a strong feeling in many quarters— some of them influential—that we are not getting full value for the huge and constantly increasing expenditure on education.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARS.</p>
        <p>Wedding of Rev. Charles Posnett.</p>
        <p>The wedding took place on Wednesday at the Wesleyan Church, Bowdon, of the Rev. Charles Posnett, of Medak, Ni7ams Dominions, India, to Phyllis, youngest daughter of Mr. John L. Barker, of Bowdon. Mr. Posnett is a brother of Mr. R. H. Posnett, of Runcorn. Miss Marie Corelii's Visit. Miss Marie Corelli, the famous novelist, will aive an address at the Parr Hall. on Friday, March 22nd. under the auspices of the Literary and Philosophical Society. Her subject will be " The spirit of Shakes- peare.' Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., will give the illustrative readings. Seats may be booked at Mr. R. H. Pearson's, Bridge-street, on and after Monday next The proceeds will be in aid Children's Summer Camp. Welsh Disestablishment.</p>
        <p>As advertised in our column, a meeting of protest sgainst the proposed Disestablishment and Die- endocvnrnent of the Church in Wales, will be held in the Parr Hall, on Monday, March 18th. The Bishop of Liverpaol will preside and the principal speakers will be the Bishop of Landaff and Mr. E. W. Johnson, of Llandudno.</p>
        <p>The Pearson &amp; Knowles Coal &amp; Iron Co. Ltd. The directors have declared the usual half year's dividend at the rate of six percent. per annum on both the Preference and Second Preference Shares, also an interim dividend at the rate of three per cent, per annum on the Ordinary Shares. all less tax. MOTOR CYCLES.—Agent for " Douglas " &amp; "Premier." New Models now in stook. Trials and all information free.-J.S. DARLA ND, 51-53, Bridge at., Warrington.-Adat Plcturedrome. There has been an exceptionally good " show " st the Picturedrome during the week. Many interesting pictures have been thrown on the screen and large houses have bPen the rule. The management announce that Miss A Hurst, 205, Orford-lane, was awarded a prize in the picturedrome alliteration competition. Her alliteration appears in our advertisement columns this week. A capital programme has been arranged fohmext week and many humorous and tragic films will be shown each night—commencing at 0-30, for a continuous performance. _</p>
        <p>Newsagents' Dinner. The members of the Warrington branch of the Northern Counties Newagents' Association held their annual dinner at Bibby's Idestautant on Thursday evening, and the gathering proved a complete success. —Mr. John Gandy (president) occupied the chair, and proosed the loyal toasts. The health of "The local publishers " was honoured on the proposition of Mr. Albert Smith, and Mr. W. H. Atkinson (" Examiner "), in reply, expressed his pleasure that such a good feeling existed between the agents and the proprietors. Miring the evening Mr. W. Critchley contributed several songs Warrington Photographic Society. One of the most gratifying things this session has been the number of evenings filled up by our own members. Tuesday evening was spent in Lincolnshire with Mr. W. Burrell, the hon. curator and Itinternist to the Society, and rarely are the pictures and material of a lecture so well blended. The towns of Boston and Grantham, and the city of Lincoln were the scenes visited, and the advantages of cultivating mutual acquaintances amongst fellow photographers is illustrated by a fact narrated by the lecturer. He had visited Lincoln many times, and thought he knew the city almost to perfection, but the secretary of the Lincoln Photlgraphic Society easily convinced him that be was only just beginning to know it, so many new and picturesque scenes did he reveal Ito him. Mr. Brown occupied the chair and 30 members were present. " PREMIER " CYCLlM—Speoial lines at £5 105. or with 3 speed gear, £6 153.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Presentation to Mr. Cecil. Locker.</p>
        <p>There was a pleasing little ceremony at the works of Messrs Thomas Locker and Co. Limited, wire cloth manufacturers, Church-street, on Tuesday evening, when the employees presented Mr. Cecil Locker, eldest son of Councillor J. T. Locker, with a token of esteem and goodwill on the occasson of his twenty- first, birthday. The present took the form of a hand- somely fitted travelling case, a travelling rug and a silver mounted umbrella. There was a full muster of the employees to witness the interesting proceedings which were opened with a few remarks by Mr. Lawrence (chief clerk), who alluded to the kindness which the staff and workpeople invaribly experienced at the hands of the firm. Mr. E. Woods (works manager) made the presentation, and expressed the hope that Mr. Cecil Locker's active connection with the firm, which only commenced a few months ago, would be a long end happy one, and that the gift of the employees would be accepted as a mark of appreciation and encouragement. He testified to the good feeling which had always existed between the firm and their em- ployees, and mentioned that evely employee had sub- scribed to the present—Mr. Cecil Locker suitably returned thanks, and short speeches were also made by Councillor J. 'I:. Locker, and Mr. Thomas. Locker, each of whom cordially thanked the employees for the kindly feelings which had prompted the gift.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>Junior Liberal Association. Mr. E. Lloyd Jones delivered a most interesting lecture on" The Hon. D. Lloyd George, the Christian statesman," at a meeting of the Warrington Junior Liberal Association held in the Assembly Itoom at the Liberal Club on Wednesday evening. In the absence, through illness, of Mr. G. Toulmin, the chair was occupied by Mr. P. IT. Brown. There was a large audience and the lecture was listened to with rapt attention and great appreciation. Next Week at the Hippodrome. Nothing pleases Warrington audiences more than a good,strong variety programme. They will no doubt I be amply satisfied with the " bill " arranged for next week at the Hippodrome. What is described as the greatest comedy act ill the world will be presented by Silbon's Cats, including " Tillie," the only cat looping the human loop. Verno and Voyce will, no doubt, score a success in their satirical scena " John Bull's Cash Box." The originators of the rag-time waltz, the Four Casinos, along with many other turns are down to appear and in addition the usual " Perfection Pictures " will be shown. Clergymen's Will.</p>
        <p>The- will of the Rev. Edwin Sandys Jackson, of Monkstown, Co. Dublin, vicar of Great Sankey from 1880-1900 has been proved at, 1..7,718. He left X. 1,000 each to the Representative Church Body of Ireland, the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel, and to the Society for the Promotion of Christian Knowledge ; £5OO to the Dub:in University's Mission to Choto Nagpore ; .t.:400 to the Vicar and churchwardens of Great Sankey upon trust to apply the income there- from for the poor of the parish ; £3OO each to the Bishop of Liverpool's Fund, Universities' Mission to Central Africa, and other bequests for charitable purposes. LAWN MOWERS ground by special machinery. Send your machines now before the rush.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge et., Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Church Society. "The power of good thoughts" was the subject on which the Rev. Canon Howson, vicar of St. Andrew's, Southport, delivered an interesting address to a large gathering of women in the Parish Church yesterday afternoon. These Lenten lectures promoted by the Warrington Church Society continue to draw large congregations, not only so far as the women's meeting is concerned for in the evening when the Rev. J. B Lancelot, Principal of Liverpool College, took for his subject "Our Lord's Temptation "there was an equally good muster of men, and the rev, gentleman's interesting lecture was thoroughly appreciated. The women's service next Friday afternoon will be conducted by the Rev. Prebendary Wakeford,vicat of St Margaret's, Anfield, who will take for his subject "The Christian character" and in the evening the same clergyman will speak to the men on" Power and Government." Bakers' Strike Averted.</p>
        <p>No small concern was caused in Warrington last week by the news that there was some probability of a bakers' strike taking place. However, we are glad to state that the threatened trouble has been averted The Master Bakers' Associatian and other employers in tho trade received a deputation from the Amalgamated Union of Operative Bakers and Con- fectioners on Tuesday night, when the demands of the men were discussed. Each side, we are informed, made concessions, and the trouble was satisfactorily adjusted. Arrangements were made for the formation of a permanent committee, composed equally of masters and men, to settle future difficulties. The oporative bakers were agitating for a 54 hours' week, including meal times, but the men consented to a 60 hours' week, including meal times, and the mastbrs agreed to the men's demands for increased pay. VACUUM CLEANERS, thoroughly reliable machines from 375. 6d.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmongers, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Stockton Heath Council School. _</p>
        <p>The second annual echo 4 concert was held on Thursday last in the Assembly Hall of the school and gave great pleasure to all who attended. The first part of the programme was occupied by sketches and movements given by the infants and juniors and the second part was carried through by the senior scholars. The infants gave" Who killed Cock Robin" and " Where are you going to my pretty maid" with admirable effect. Standard I. gave action songs and Standard 11. " The Sleeping Beauty." The sketches of the second half of the programme included fine action songs by the boys of Standard IV., and the boys and girls of Standard 111. A sketch "The Tea Party" was very well rendered by the girls of Standard IV., and evolved great merriment. A scene from "Alice in Wonderland," in which Alice joins at the "Mad Tea Party," was splendidly given, and Miss Freda Morriss mane an inimitable Alice. Ile girls of Standards VI. and VII. had the most ambitious part assigned to them in their efforts to render the Trial Scene from Shakespeare's "Merchant of Venice." The parts tax the powers of very able actors and to say that the scene was arrestingly given is to give great praise to the young actresses Miss Nellie Berry made an admirable Shylock and Miss Lena Blackaurn an equally admirable Portia. The render- ing certainly surpassed any expectations that could have been formed by those who know the demands made by this fine Shakespearian scene. The concert was repeated with equal success last night, the hall. being again crowded tn excess. Very great credit attaches to the teachers for the careful training given, and those who share the happy results will look forward to next years concert with considerable pleasure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>PROSPEROUS INDUSTRY. RICHMOND COMPANY ATTAINS ITS</head>
        <p>A PROSPEROUS INDUSTRY. RICHMOND COMPANY ATTAINS ITS</p>
        <p>MAJORITY.</p>
        <p>SOME IMPORTANT CONTRACTS. A very prosperous year's work was under review at the twenty-first ordinary general meeting of the share- holders of the Richmond Gas Stove and Nleter Company Limited which was held at the registered office of the company, Academy-street, on Thursday. Mr. T. Dwerry- house (chairman) presided and there were also present :—Messrs Horace NI Thornton, (vice-chairman) John Coxon, J F Atkins, M.B , John A Ransome, J W Glover (dire3tors), Alderman A Bennett, F A Frost, E 3 Hall, John Plant, Grine, W Duff. John Booth, Louis A Voibey (auditor) and J W Slack (secretary). A letter of apology for non-attendance was read from Alderman. J. Smethurst, who was confined to his home through indisposition. THE REPORT.</p>
        <p>_ In their report the directors stated :—The balance standing to the credit of Profit and Loss Account (after deducting debenture interest) including the amount brought forward is 02,621 15s. 10d. which the directors recommend be appropriated as follows :—deprecia- tion on buildings, plant, patterns, fixtures, &amp;c., £5,609 I:3s. 9d. ; dividend on preference capital at the rate of 0 per cent, per annum (less income tax), £S,SSO Ms. 10d. ; dividend free of income tax) on ordinary shares at the rate of 10 per cent, per annum, £8,161 12s. ; bonus of ed. per share on ordinsry shares, £2,040 Ss. ' • reserve fund (bringing this amount up to £45,000), £5,000 ; balance to next account, subject to directors' remuneration, £5,919 es. Bd. After placing the entire cost of advertising as in former years against revenue, the directors recommend a divi- dend at the rate of 10 per cent per annum and a bonus of fid. per share on the ordinary shares. Tke Com- pany's works at Academy-street, Grappenhall, and branches have as usual been kept in good condition and maintained out of revenue. The continued success of the Company has again compelled the directors to make still further extensions at Grappenhall works and addi- tional buildings are now in course of erection for castings stores, fitting and grinding shops, Sc. Toe retiring directors are Mr John Coxon and Mr J W Glover. Their r.-election by the Board is hereby reported in accordance with the Commuay's Articles of 'Association. The auditor, Mr L A Voisey, chartered ' accountant, retires, and is recommended for re-appoint- ment. THE CHAIRMAN REVIEWS THE YEAR'S WORK The CHAIRMAN, in moving the adoption of the report and statement of accounts, said that after perusal of the balance sheet and report he thought the share- holders would agree with the directors that the year just closed had been a good one for the company. (Hear, hear.) Orders were not very plentiful at the commencement, but they came later on, and the latter part of the year found them very busy. They had suffered from the fire which occurred at the works about twelve months ago, the entire offices and contents and a portion of the works being destroyed. The inconvenience and expense to which they were put in finding and fitting up accommodation for the office staff to carry on the business must have made some difference in their net balance. The staff had worked well in their temporary quarters and deserved every praise for having carried out their duties under trying and dis- agreeable circumstances. The offices were now corn- pleteland in use and were more conformable to modern requirernents.Lacik of space compelled the directors to proceed with the building of additional workshops,grind- ing and fitting shops and stores at Grappenhall rather sooner than they otherwise would have done. The buildings were now in an advanced state of completion and the directors honed they would he in use in a month or two. With regard to materials, which formed so heavy an item in their trade, they had best rather fortunate in most of their important contracts but prices bad gone up and were still rising, and it was difficult at the present time to express an opinion of any value on the subject. As to their</p>
        <p>PROSPECTS FOR THE PRESENT TEAR, under normal conditions they should consider tfietu good, but, under such conditions as existed to-day, it would be wiser perhaps not to express any opinion. The only thing to do was to face their difficulties as they arose, and do the best they could under the circumstances for the good of the company. Competition was as keen as ever, but having the services of a highly trained and efficient staff, they did not fear losing their share of any orders going. That was the twenty-first meeting of the company, which had now attained its majority. Unlike many companies established by private firms and afterwards formed into a company with capital,business and premises complete, that company commenced its existence as such in a very small way. It had had many difficulties to contend with in its growth, in raising capital, in making the business and in finding suitable premises in which to carry on its business, after out- growing thoss in use, but it had overcome them all. It was pleasant for those who had to d with the company in those darker days to see it to-day a</p>
        <p>STRONG, RELIABLE COMPANY. During the past ten years, including the year under review,the amount added to capital was £12,979 lOs 7d. A part of that, however—El,2so —was part payment in shares of businesses which they took over. Additions had been made to buildings, plant, &amp;c, during that term to the amount of £78,231. There had been written off buildings, plant, &amp;c, as depreciation £50,166 and added to reserve fund £35,000. They had paid during that term a dividend averaging 111 per cent. Reviewing the balance sheet, the Chairman pointed out that the liabilities with regard to shares were the same as last year. The arnouut due to creditors was £15,516 3s 7d— an increase of £5,926 which was owing in great measure to some large accounts which they had at the end of the year. It was, however, counterbalanced by the larger stock. The reserve fund stood at £40,000, an increase of £5,000. On the assets side, the amount set down for freehold and lease- hold works and other buildings, plant, &amp;c., was £06,194. That was £1,389 Is 8d less than last year, owing to the depreciation being larger than the additions. The gond wi If (.65Q0) remained the same. The trade stocks at Warrington, Stratford, London and branches were valued at £38,627 us 8d which, as he had explained, was larger and coneterbalaneed the increased amount due to creditors. The cash at bankers LE 6,017 2s Gd) was larger than last year. Sundry debtors amounted to £50,180 19s. sd. •On the whole, he considered the balance sheet was a very good one He congratulated the directors and shareholders on the prosperity of the company which had enabled it to produce such a report and balance sheet. (Applause). Mr. H. M. THORNTON seconded. He said the reunite; shown on the balance sheet did not convey, except ire figures, the very considerable amount of hard work which such a result had involved not only ca the management but on the part of the directors who had been associated with the company for so many years. He would like to mention one or two factors which had helped very materially the advancement of their profits and their reputation sagas, stove and meter manufacturers. Their business was largely made up of apparatus for gas cooking and gas heating, although, of course, there were other departments which were developing and which would add materially in the future to their success. Gas cooking had for years been popular, but gas beating had only become so iii very recent years. In the opinion of the directors, it was destined to become far more popular as time went on, and it was in that direction that they were antici- pating their future profits, to some large extent. Tuey had been successful last year in securing TWO OP THE LARGEST CONTRACTS for gas fires that hod ever been given out to one firm of manufacturers—he referred to the two great centres of Manchester and Liverpool. At Liverpool the gas company had not developed gag fires until the begin- ning of the present winter, and after very careful deliberation and thorough examination of the different makes, the gas fire of that firm was chosen as the sole one to be hired out in Liverpool for a period of years. That in itself showed that they had a god article and that they were able to face competition. (Applause.) At 'Manchester a similar result occurred. With regard to the future, ha would be very unwise if he did not follow the example, of the Chairman and refrain from expressing an opinion. An association bad been formed—The British Commercial Gas Association— consisting of nearly all the gas companies and Corpora- tion gas departments in the country for purpose of</p>
        <p>GREATER PUBLICITY IN THE ADVANTAGES. OF GAS over other commodities which competed with it. That association had now become an assured fact. A veiy large sum of money had been subscribed from the various undertakings and in the near future a very important development in advertising and publishing statistics in connection with gas would take place and would, he hoped, con- tinue for some years. It must be to the benefit of the industry and that firm was closely connected with the management of the scheme. All that they had done in the pest could only brve been accomplished by the whole of th.ir staff and all associ tted with the firm working in full harmony. That spirit still continued, and he knew of nothing which could destroy it. Although in</p>
        <p>THESE DAYS OF UNREST AND UNCERTAINTY it was not wise to speak of the future, he had sufficient confidence in the solidity and soundness of their busi- ness to anticipate that they would not have any less likelihood of being in us good a position at the end of this year as they were at the present moment. The resolution Wft.l unanimously nsssed. THE DIVIDEND.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN proposed : " That a dividend at the rate of 10 per cent per annum (less 5 per cent already paid) and a bonus of 6d. per share for the year ended December 31st, 1911 (free of income tax) be now declared and paid." Mr JOHN CONON seconded. He said he had been a director of the comk any for eighteen years and had never seen the business look in a healthier condition than to-day. (Hear, hear.) They had had 10 per cent.. dividend for many years but during the last two or three years they had not had a bonus. The only ground on which they could possibly criticise the directors was on the extreme care which they had taken in the management of the fiances. It would not, however, have been wise to have paid a bonus every year. The works at Grappenhall had bad to be enlarged and the Academy-street worlis had required some re-modelling in order to effect economies, bnt the shareholders would in future reap the benefit of their forbearance. (Applause). The resolution was approved. DIRECTORS' REMUNERATION. _ - .</p>
        <p>Mr. W. DIiFF proposed: That the sum of £7OO be placed at the disposal of the directors for their services during the year 1911." He congratulated the directors on the splendid balance sheet and hoped the bonus would be continued every year. Mr. Jolts: Boom seconded. He said the excellent balance sheet reflected very great credit upon the directors and the management. Personally he did not object to not having received a bonus for several years, because he believed in building up a substantial reserve fund. Seeing that that was the twenty-first anniversary of the company, it was fitting that it ehould be marked by something special in the way of a bonus. He believed there was a great fu'ure for the business in cookers and appliances. The resolution was carried. THE AU DI COIL Mr E J Ham. proposed the re-appointment of Mr L A Voisey as auditor, at a remuneration of .£l5O. He added his congratulations on the attainment of the 21st anniversary of the company and slid that, with one exception, he was the only man in the room who Was present at the first meeting of directors. He was note shareholder in tbosl days hut attended on legal busi- ness. Mr L A Voisey himself was secretary. It there- fore gave him (Mr Hall) more pleasure than anyone else in the room could feel in moving that resolution and adding his congratulations on such a very properous attainment of a very happy majority. (Applause) Mr. F. A. FROST seconded. He said he had seen a great deal of Mr. Voisey in connection with other companies of which he was the auditor and he could not imagine that the shareholders could possibly have their interests better protected by putting the matter in other hands.</p>
        <p>The resolution was passed. Mr. VOISEV, in returning thanks, said the amount of business passed through the books in the course of a year was enormous and it gave him great pleasure to take that opportunity of testifying to the very ae,urate and n.at way in which the work was done. It reflected the greatest credit upon the staff. THE CHAIRMAN THANKED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pt cx-T-m—oved--a—v-ote of thanks to Mr. Dwerry- house for presiding. Alderman BENNNTT, in seconding, said he was proud to be a humble shareholder in a concern which had met with such uninterrupted success. The vote was heartily jpassed. The CHAIRMAN, in reply, said be only hoped they -ould have as good a report to present next year. Under the present circnmstanoes he had his doubts, but still they would do their best to bring it off. THE STAFF. Dr ATKINS proposed a vote of thanks to the staff, and said it was largely duo to their loyal and efficient services that the year's work bad been so satisfactory. He particularly mentioned the excellent and hard- working managing directors (Messrs H M Thornton and J A Ransome), upon whose shoulders rested a large part of the responsibility for the working of the con- cern. Mr Slack and his able staff in the office had also done hard and good work. They were also indebted to Mr William Glover and his son Mr. Albert Glover for their valuable services. (Applause). Mr. MANY seconded and tine vote was carried. Mr ItAssomE, in responding, said the chiefs of the departments and the whole of the staff were heart and soul in the work and giving complete satisfaction. a were The plant, machinery and buildings were in good working condition and new machines were constantly being introduced to keep the firm abreast of the times and to eiable theta to produce their commoditieg so as to compete against all corners. The new buildings were apparently somewhat massive, but it would not be long before they were filled and be hoped they might have to extend still further. (Applause.) , Mr. GLOVER, also returned thanks. Notwithstanding the clouds on the horizon, he hoped the present year would be as bright and perhaps even more successful than the one which they had been reviewing.(Applanse)</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' is a good Cough Mixtureo Effective and Economical. Of Chemists only.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDGE CONTENTIONS.</p>
        <p>Questions of Approaches and Extras.</p>
        <p>There was a heavy agenda for the monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening, and t:2e result was three hours of discussion on a variety of topics. The Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph) presided, and there were also present :—The Deputy Mayor (Alderman Forshaw), Aldermen A Bennett, W Bolton, T Bprton, John Evans, James Evans. D Tinnion ; Councillors G Archer, T II Ashcroft, W H Bellian, T ' Birtles, J Broadhurst, T Cl.antler, E Cunnington, J Dickenson, J lialliwell, J Hawthorn, R. Henshall, C Jackson Holmes, W H J Jenkins, F T Lashford, J T Locl er, Dr McKee, J Pemberton, H Roberts, J Sankey, .T Skelland. T S Steel, 0 Toole, J Wilkinson and W J Wilson ; Messrs A T Hellaway (Deputy Town Clerk), T H Spinks (Assistant Town Clerk), J Fairhurst (Borongh Treasureri, W S Haddock (Gas Engineer), F V L Mathias (Electrical Engineer), J M Murray (Director of Education), Dr J C Hibbert (Medical Officer) and J Gray (Water Engineer). THE LATE PROFESSOR YOUNG.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR made a sympathetic allusion to the death of Professor Young, who, be remarked, was one of Warrington's most distinguished and worthy sons. He had the pleasure of being a fellow-student with the late Professor for two years. Even at that early time he seemed to be marked out for his splendid career, which gained credit for himself and brought honour to his college. It was something truly great to be a teacher of so important a subject as anatomy—the basis upon which knowledge of medicine and surgery was founded. Professor Young was essentially a War- rington man and he (the Mayor) thought they had not made sufficient of the fact.</p>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE CO-OP rATIVE MEMBERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>VENO'S</head>
        <p>VENO'S</p>
        <p>4.1</p>
        <p>On the motion of Councillor Roberts, Mr John Upson and Mr Edwin Plinston were appointed co-opta- tive members on the Education Com mt ttee, the former on the recommendation of the Warrington branch of the National Union of Teachers and the second on the recommendation of the Trades and Labour Party. Councillor PEMBERTON : is there a Trades and Labour Council now? The MAYOR: Yes.</p>
        <p>The Street Improvement Committee, at a meeting on February 28th, considered the lines of frontage from Warrington Bridge along Knutsford-road and Wilders- pool-causeway, and after discussion, it was resolved that the plan showing open spaces at the corner of Wilderspool-causeway and Knutsford-road be recom- mended for adoption by the Bridges Committee. It was also resolved that for the time being the property belonging to the executives of the late Joseph Ashton be not acquired. At a joint meeting of the Street Improvement Cl m mittee also the Bridges Sub-committee on the same date, the resolutions passed by the Street Improve- ment Committee were considered. It was resolved that the plan showing open spaces at the corner of Wilders- pool Causeway and Knutsford-road be not approved; that the line commencing from the end of the curve of the balustrade wall on the east side of the new bridge be approved ; that at the present time no line be defined on the west side.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT having moved the adoption of the Street Improvement Committee's minutes. Alderman TINNION moved an amendment that the recomendation with regard to the approach to War- rington Bridge be referred back. He pointed out that the resolution of the Street Improvement Committee was only carried by the casting vote of the chairman &lt;Alderman Bennett) and immediately afterwards the joint committees passed a resolution to exactly the opposite effect—also by the casting vote of the chair- man (Alderman Tinnion.) He contended that by adopting the plan suggested by the Street Improvement Committee they would be wasting land for which they would have to pay a considerable sum. Alderman BOLTON seconded. The large open space which the Street Improvement Committee recommended would not bring in any rates ; it would need paving and it would become a football ground or a place for vans and gipsy tents. (Laughter). Conncillor BELLIAN said the Bridges Committee had full responsibily for the approaches to the new bridge and the Street Improvement Committee's resolution was merely a pious hope which could be over-ridden. Alderman BENNETT made a strong plea for the retention of the open spaces, giving an uninterrupted view of the bridge, as suggested by the Street Improvement Committee. He was so absolutely amazed at the proposal of the Bridges Committee that he bad been wondering whether the world was really going on in the ordinary way. The whole idea seemed to be contrary to what was the settled policy of the Council from the first, and it took him some time to realise that such a drastic change was contemplated. If the Bridges I,Committee's idea were adopted, they would be making a mistake which they -would regret as long as Warrington remained a town. It would be utterly fatal to the whole design of the bridge, and would render the training walls completely useless. The Alderman strongly condemned the com- mittee's proposal to prescribe a building line so that the space could be built upon, he presumed right up to the balustrades of the bridge. They would have some- thing like a boulevard on ode side, and it would be like a pig with one ear unless they had something to match it on the other. Councillor PEMEERTON supported the amendment that the minute be referred back. Alderman Bennett suggested that they sbould have a 55ft. space, but he (the speaker) believed the public would never make use of it. They had to consider the rates. They had only just started the bridge and in two or three years they would have the bill for it. They would regret it, if they threw any money away. Alderman Bennett liked everything jo be nice and beautiful, but they often had to check him. (Laughter.) His intentions were good, but he ought to live in Southport, Bournemouth, Eastbourne or some other resort. (Laughter.) He was not fit for a manufacturing town. (Laughter.) The inhabitants of Warrington would be quite satisfied to have some use made of the land when they had to pay another 7d. or Bd. in the .£ for the bridge. Considerable discussion ensued. Councillor BELLIAN said he could not see what good object would be served by expensive balustrades if they allowed buildings to obscure them. They were proposing to hide the ornamental portion of the bridge. Councillors Roberts, Broadhurst and others having spoken, the MAYOR expressed himself In favour of the minutes of the Street Improvement Committee. They ought to have a space on that side of the bridge and make the scheme really symmetrical. From a utilitarian point of view it was desirable that when people had passed over a crowded bridge they should have some "mace in which to collect their scattered thoughts. (Laughter.) The fact that there was a fine open space I In front, would also tend to enhance the attractiveness of any shops that might be built. Alderman TINNION : You ask a tradesman. Councillor HAWTHORN said they would "spoil the ship for a ha'porth of tar" if they built up to the balustrades. Councillor LOCKER said the matter had heed brought before the Council in to crude a form for the members to be able to vote upon it. Eventually it was agreed that the resolution of the Street Improvement Committee and of the Joint I committees should be withdrawn with a view to further consultation. THE SMOKE NUISNACE.</p>
        <p>Alderman JAMES EVANS moved, and Alderman BURTON seconded, the confirmation of the minutes of the Health Committee, which contained the following resolution :—" That this committee be not represented at the Exhibition and Conference of the Smote Abate- ment League of Great Britain, to be held at the Agri cultural hall, London, in March," Alderman BENNETT asked why the committee had decided not to be represented at a conference of such importance. The Algvoß replied that a circular was sent to him as Mayor, asking him to support the movement and he signed it. That proved at all events that as a member of the Health Committee he was not inimical to smoke abatement. The circular was sent out by the Smoke Abatement League. Alderman BoLTON : Another old women's society. (Laughter). Alderman BENNETT expressed the pleasure it afforded him to hear the Mayor's association. Alderman JAMES EvANS remarked that the committee cause to the decision not to be represented owing to the fact that they did not think the League of sufficient importance. Alderman BENNETT moved as an amendment thst the matter be reconsidered. The conference would</p>
        <p>Lii=htninir</p>
        <p>_ COUGH CURE ALBERT SNITS!. Dn.: M.5.11.5c.: the r analyst, says :—Vatro' fa s Ltairrutwa . I compo L sed of pure Ingredienta, and la a . remedy for bronchitis. asthma. and all lung: , London r CU tus</p>
        <p>oelebrated Couon , • mart</p>
        <p>ei-ceffeni</p>
        <p>The oureat and surest remedy obtainable for COUGHS and COLDS I INFLUENZA COLDS MORES'S COUGHS ' 'IAD BREATHING</p>
        <p>have under consideration a Bill which would abso- lutely fevolutionize the methods of dealing with the smoke nuisance and which, be believed, had the support of the President of the Local Government Board. Councillor BROADHURST seconded and said it would be in the best interests of the ratepayers and of the iron and steel workers if the committee sent a deputation to the conference. .</p>
        <p>Alderman 13ouroN objected to an outsider lecturing the Health Committee on its duties.</p>
        <p>„ Councillor PEMBERTON pointed out that Warrington was a manufacturing town. At the conference there would be representatives from Eastbourne, Southport, and all the beautiful places of England, and resolutions would be carried which would be detrimental to Warrington if carried out. The committee ought to leave such a conference alone. Whether they liked it or not they made their living in Warrington by the smoke.</p>
        <p>• Alderman BOLTON : I wish there was more (Laughter). Councillor PEMBERTON : We cannot have a place here like Southport. The manufacturers will do every- thing they can to save their pockets, and it is a well- known principle that black smoke does not pay. I object to the manufacturers being harassed more than they are now harassed. They are harassed enough already by heavy rates. (Hear, hear), The INlA:vort : lam afraid that with the coal strike we shall not have much ciuse to complain about the smoke. __</p>
        <p>Councillor HAWTHORN thought it would probably be advantageous if the difficulties of a town like War- rington were brought before the conference. The amendment was defeated by a large majority. only three members voting for it, viz., Alderman Bennett and Councillors Broadhurst and Hawthorn. WILL TREES GROW IN MANCHESTER ROAD? Alderman BENNETT called attention to the proposed expenditure of £2O by the Fown Hall, Parks and Baths Committee, on trees and shrubs for St. Elphin's Park. He contended it would be an absolute waste of money, as trees would not grow there. They might as well drop £2O Into the river. Of course, he could quite understand the desire of the committee to hide the hideous erection they were placing in the middle of the gronnd, but they would have to do it in some other way. He had tried to grow trees in that neigh- bourhood and had failed.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON said Alderman Bennett must have planted his trees wrong way up. (Laughter.) If trees would live on Wilderspool Causeway, they would live in Manchester-road.</p>
        <p>The minutes were approved. SALARIES IN THE GAS DEPARTMENT.</p>
        <p>The Gas Committee have passed the following resolu tions : " That the scale of salaries now in operation in the Borough Treasurer's office be applied as from this date to the clerical staff of the Gas Department, to a maximum of £lOl in each case, and that the question of the bonus for examinations be referred to the secretary, assistant secretary and the Borough Treasurer to report upon " ; and that the salary of Mr. James Dobson, chief clerk at Longford GAS Works, be increased from £lOl to £l2O per annum." The minutes were approved. HALF-PENNY TRAM FARES. WOULD THEY PAY?</p>
        <p>Councillor Holmes had given notice to ask the Chair- man of the Electricity and Tramways Committee whether the Committee have considered the advisability of introducing half-penny stages on the tramways, and if so, what was the decision arrived at. Councillor WILKINSON (deputy-chairman), expressed regret that,owiug to indisposition, Alderman Smethurst (chairman) was unable to be present. The adoption of half-penny Stages on the Tramways had been considered on several occasions by the Electricity and Tramways Committee and the decision arrived at was," that to introduce such fares on so small a system would prove disasterous. " Many small traruNsays bad been induced to try these fares and with- out 000 single exception the result had been a tre- mendous drop in revenue. The Warrington committee's margin of profit at the present time was one-tenth of a penny per passenger, which could easily be swept away by a small concession of the fares. It should also be remembered that the maintenance charges on the rolling stock and permanent way were increasing every year. They were also bound by resolution of the Council, they were bound to find a sum of £l6OO each year from these profits towards the the renewal of the permanent way. That would reduce the net profit per passenger, to 1-12th of a penny. If halfpenny fares were adopted they would carry more short distance passengers to the exclusion of Id. passengers, but at the same time the revenue wonld decrease. At the present time nearly the whole of the cars were in use at the week end and to carry more passengers would mean the purchase of more cars. There was not the slightest doubt in his mind that the introduction of half-penny fares would mean a drop in the revenue of the trams, together with an increase of the working expenses, and instead of being able to build up a very neccessary reserve fund and assist the rates, they would have to call on the rates to help the department. Councillor HAWTHORN asked for information with regard to the proposed trackless trams. He hoped there would be a time limit to the state of suspended anima- tion in which the question now was. A deputation had waited upon him and the inhabitants of the wards affected were anxious that the matter should be pushed forward as quickly as possible. Councillor PE3II3ERTON said there was a great want of enterprise on the part of the committee in their neglect of largely populated parts of the town. SOllle of the Council, added the speaker, had known of , certain irregularities that had been going on in , the department, for many months. He had been I very patient but it seemed as if the whole thing was I going to be buried without the Council knowing a word about it. He thought that when there were grave irregularities in connection a ith any servant of the 'Corporation, the members were entitled to know full 1 particulars and there should be an investigation by an independent committee. He did not like hole and corner meetings. From what he had heard, the affair amounted to a public scandal. Councillor WILKINSON asked Councillor Hawthorn and his friends to be patient. The committee had two schemes under consideration and intended to bring them forward as soon as possible. They had, however, to get an Act of Parliament to do the work and certain formalities had to be complied with. Councillor Pemberton's remarks were very uncalled for. A sub- committee had investigated the affair and the irregularities were such that if they had occurred in a private concern of his own he (Councillor Wilkinson) would willingly have forgiven them. They were not as serious as people would like one to think, but as they occurred in connection with a public department they could not be overlooked. -.. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON What are they? Councillor WILKINSON: You know all about them. Councillor PEMBERTON : Tbe Council does uot. The minutes were approved. WARRINGTON BRIDGE. THE QUESTION OF EXTRAS.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS'S VIGOROUS PROTEST.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Bridges Committee a letter from the Warrington Trades and Labour Council with regard to the Bridge contract was read, and the Town Clerk was instructed to communicate with them. A plan showing the design of mooring rings proposed to be fixed in the river walls, was not apnroved, the Com- mittee considering that such rings were unnecessary. Mr. 3..1. Webster attended the sub-committee with regard to certain details on construction, which he explained. He intimated that in his opinion the altera- tion of the south-west training wall, as approved by the committee, would not entail additional expenditure. He asked for Instructions as to how the surface of the concrete of the outer ribs, walls, &amp;c., of the bridge and training walls, should be left, and suggested that the surface should be brushed over with a wire brush, at an estimated extra cost of .C6O. It was resolved that the engineer be informed that this committee require the surface of the training walls and the Bridge to be finished in accordance with the contract; also that the tender of the contractors for the coursing horizontally and vertically, of the concrete of the bridge to represent masonry, at an extra expense of .£73 be accepted.—The tender of Mr Harry Fairclough for pile foundations for Howley Footbridge on the Victoria Park side, in accordance with specification, at £6O, was accepted. The MAYon contended that the resolution with regard to Mr. Fairclough's tender was irregular although he should not take the responsibility of opposing it. The resolution was only arrived at by the Bridges Sub-committee and was not confirmed by the full Bridges Committee, and therefore it was not eligible for consideration by the Council. He thought ke knew one reason why it had had to be done in that way. He retired from the committee as a protest aviiust the way in which the committee was being con- ducted—he did not say by the Chairman. No notice was taken of the protest and he felt it was his bounden duty to mention the matter to the Council. Councillor WILKINSON": I take it if you take excep- tion to one, you must take exception to the whole of the minutes of the sub-committee. The MAYOR : Not at all. This was a sub-committes resolution and did not come before the Bridges Committee.</p>
        <p>The DEPCTY TOWN CLERK explained that the Bridges Committee broke up hurriedly and there was no quorum. The tender for the bridge had to be considered Abet day, or it would have meant delay- ing the erection of the bridge for another mouth. He therefore brought the matter before the sob-committee and he had now submitted it to the Council. It was irregular but he took full responsibility for it. The matter would have to be decided now or a month hence. It was for the Council to say whether they wished Howley tolbe deprived of the bridge for a month or not. Councillor JENKINS asked if the Council had power to confirm a minute which was irregular? The DEPUTY Toww CLERK: If the Council con- firms the minute it will cure all defects. Councillor JACKSON Hotuss deprecated anything that would cause further delay in the erection of the long-talked-of bridge for Howley. Councillor TOOLE said he understood there had been trouble with various trade unions in Warrington with regard to the contracts for Warrington Bridge. There seemed to be a lot of dissatisfaction that the con- tractors were not complying with the local trade union</p>
        <p>conditions. He had been approached on the matter and promised to raise it. The MAYOR : Nothing definite has beeu settled yet. Conncillor BURTLES expressed regret that the com- mittee had rejected the proposal to place mooring rings on the new bridge. The rings were not actually for mooring purposes but would take the form of lions' heads which would give a degree of strength and solidity to the appearance of the bridge. If the Council did not care for those, he suggested that circular panels should be substituted. The heads of prominent mem- bers of the Council could then be introduced in bus relief. (Laughter). Councillor PE MBERTON said the amount of extras, in comparison with the amount of the contract, was already too high. Unless the Council put its foot down there would be no end to the extra expenditure. If the Council would be advised by the Bridges Committee, who had a grasp of the subject, they might get the bridge built within a few thousand pounds of the estimate. If not, they would have a very serious extra expenditure. There was quite sufficient ornamentation. Some members would soon be asking the Council to gild the bridge with gold leaf. (Laughter.) Councillor BIRTLES pointed out that the lions would be symbolical of strength. Councillor PEMBERTON : Would not elephants give a greater appearance of strength ? (Laughter.) The MAYOR said he would bring Councillor Birtles's suggestion with reference to the panels before the Bridges Sub-Committee. Councillor ROBERTS hoped the matter would not be brought up again. The expenditure proposed, which was extra to the original estimate, had been carefully considered by the committee three times over, and they decided that it should not be carried through. In sub- stitution for it other proposals had been agreed to, end he hoped the C3l.loCil would adhere to them. The time had come when Mr Webster must please take "No " for "No." They had shown every possible consideration to every suggestion he had made but there were times when they were prepared to exercise their own discretion. This was one of them. The comrnitteewas almost unanimous with regard to it, and still Mr. Webster, having received direct informa- tion through the Town Clerk of the committee's decision, persisted a second and third time in bringing the matter forward. There was no finality under con- ditions like that end he for one could not afford to discuss questions three or four times over when there was no chance of altering his opinion. Alderman BENNETT said Mr. Webster was treated with gross discourtesy by the committee. Councillor ROBERTS : Nothing of the kind. Alderman BENNETT : The item has been struck oat simply through pique, and not because it is not in the interests of the town. . .</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS protested strongly against the suggestion that Mr Webster was treated with any discourtesy. The minutes were approved. THE MAGISTRATES' CLERK'S SALARY. The General Purposes committee had passed a resolu- tion to the effect that the Home Secretary be recom- mended to increase the salary of Mr G F F White, magistrates' clerk, from £5OO to £6OO per annum. The minute was approved without comment.</p>
