      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"The Wild Girl of the Forest."</p>
        <p>One of the many welcome features about Mrs Kimberley's new play "The Wild Girl of the Forest" is its charming originality of plot. It is not one of those dramas in which the audience after witnessing the first and second acts wait with impatience for the termination which they can easily foresee, but it is a production thst grips from the first and holds them spellbound until the final curtain. loris, Prince of Floralia, whose august father brought disgrace on the royal household by his cruelty and wrong doings, strives to convince his people, who are all against him, that although he has inherited his father's title he has not inherited his wickedness, and that he is willing to make reparation for the crimes of his sire. He meets Maritza, the wild girl, who along with her parents hates the prince for some personal wrong his father did the family. Not knowing his high rank, she allows him to woo her and he is successful. However, she discovers his true identity but is so impressed by his kindness, which so belies the popular conception of his character that she tries to persuade her father, the Count, who along with Captain Riccardo (also in love with Maritza) is incensing the people to revolution, that the prince was not the cruel person they imagined him to be. Riccardo i 3 aided in his villiany by the Princess of Floralia, a traitoress who is anxious to obtain possession of the crown. The pro- duction is full of exciting incidents and the various situations are unique inasmuch as they have a very dlfferent aspect from those of the old-fashioned dramas. The character of the wild girl is a very difficult 000 and the breezy manner and ease with which Miss Margaret Frame fills the part earn for her the unstinted applause of the audience. Mr. Alrzernon 1 Hicks. as loris, is a clever actor and proved a decided favourite. Be maEters the many difficulties which arise with perfect composure and finally he is rewarded for all his troubles by having as his queen the wild girl of the forest. Mr. D. Lawrence Doyle portrays the revolutionary count in convincing style whilst Miss Helen Madell as his wife shares equally the appreciation of the audience. Eiss Dulcie Lawrence submits a fine portrayal of the traitress, Princess of Floralia, whilst Mr. George E. Butler also makes the hest of the equally unpopular part of Captain Riccardo. As the crippled servant, Mr. Harry Sainsbury shows himself to be an actor who puts his soul into his part. Mr. Edward Morgan as Trilio and Miss Lynn Darlington as Mims supply an abundance of humour. The other i parts are n equally capable hands, and the whole production is one of the most pleasing of its kind that has been stages in Warrington for some time.—The orchestra, under the direction of Mr. Tom Boothroyd, playa an excellent programme of music.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>The booking for the Court Theatre commencing on Monday next, is somewhat out of the ordinary run of drama, for the play announced, "The night side of London," is written with a specific object. The author, Mr. C. Watson Mill, is rapidly gaining favour as a writer of modern plays, and his earnestness of pur has much commended itself to the play-going</p>
        <p>MR I kr,' :</p>
        <p>Li Yld 131 t nd Man. public. In this particular play the aim ster work Of a wealthy alien Jew, who abuses the hospitality that Britain offers to all nations is vividly shewn up, and it is said that the incidents portrayed are based upon actual facts which have been unearthed by the author. Mr. Mill presents many artistes of note and the full London Company, with a large amount of elaborate scenery and costumes carried with the tour.</p>
        <p>Washing Wears Away Clothes</p>
        <p>faster than ordinary use. That is, washing in the old way —rubbing and scrubbing. Try this plan—rub the clothes with Fels-Naptha and then let them soak for half an hour in cold or lukewarm water. In that half- hour the Fels-Naptha will loosen the dirt more than all your scrub- bing. Then rinse in cold water with just a little rub to get out the loosened dirt. That doesn't wear away the clothes, does it ? But it makes them whiter and sweeter than the old way.</p>
        <p>Fela-Naptha 39 Wilson street London E C</p>
