        <p>'.c..., -.... . • c ..... ..... , ..,, ~., ), ( h . ti. WAR 0 ~:ir : • . Ire • . fGT • ; tit, 0„, .• , , ......:. ...„ _ - / ----..• -„, ..., , , 5 ",</p>
        <p>" First Step to Disown Religion." St. James's Parish Magazine" has the following on Welsh Disestablishment :—" If the Bill is passed, it will go forth to the world that England has taken the first step to disown the public profession of the Christian Religion. The Church will be deprived of lSs. GO. out of every .0 and untold bitterness and strife will be engendered. We cannot idly sit by while our fellow Churchmen are in such imminent peril ; the very least we can do is to show them our sympathy and at the same time make known to the Government that we bitterly resent the measure which is to be introduced into Parliament with the object of crippling and despoiling the Church in Wales. Churchpeople already have made it impossible for Governments to pass bills for the destruction of their schools. By our strenuous opposition to the Disestablistimentproposal we may perhaps prevent them from disendowing the four Welsh dioceses of our Church.</p>
        <p>Church of England Men's Society. Our clergy (says the Warrington " Parish Church Magazine ") have carefully considered toe question of the formation of a branch of the above society, and are favourably disposed. The objects of the society are prayer and w,rk, and the rules lay upon all who join the solemn recital every day of a short prayer, and participation in some definite church work. To those who are already engaged in the Sunday school or singing in the choir, membership will make DO addition to their labours, to ethers it will mean something, but not necessarily any very arduous duty, probably only some little act of love which they can well perform. One great advantage to us all beyond that earnest prayer and definite Church work, will be the wider and iarger view of man's part in Christ's great scheme for the Redemption of the World, and a kindly interest and co-operation in other men's work in accordance with the Apostolic precept, " Look not every man on his own things, but every man also on the things of others."</p>
        <p>We do not propose starting this Society until after Easter, but we shall he glad if our Churchmen will in the meantime, give the subject their careful consideration.</p>
        <p>of fact, both Church and Dissent were alike established in the same way, namely, that the civil power recog- n zed and protected them in their worship and in their right to possess property. It was that fact which made it difficult to see why the rights of the Church as regarded the mode of government and the possession of her property should be interfered with, while those relating to Nonconformity were left intact. On the motion of the VICAR, a resolution was unanimously passed "protesting against the proposed disestablishment and disendowment of the Church in Wales, as such a measure was against the best interests of Christianity.</p>
        <p>[4=;„.. vql7 v- , 4-)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Proposed Patten Hall.</head>
        <p>The Proposed Patten Hall.</p>
        <p>We think the rumour of the benevolent scheme of the Honourable Misses Wilson-Patten—ths kindest and most generous friends of Holy Trinity Parish, as indeed they are of the whole of Warripgton—has gone sufficiently abroad, to justify now some reference to it. If the design as approved by the donors is carried out (says " Holy Trinity Parish Magazine,") it will be a I most valuable acquisition to the parish,and under proper safeguards valuable also to the Church people outside the parochial boundary. At present, however, it is pre- mature to discuss the various uses to which such a hall may be put. We hoped the builders would have been at work on the job before tl.ir,but the Street Planning Committee, we understand, have objected to the build- ing not having been set sufficiently far back from the street (Wintuarleigh-street), and this has caused delay. We apprehend, however, that this small difficulty will be speedily got over. It seems to us that a charitable gift such as this hall will be and a gift by Warrington's chief benefactors, and to the memory of one of Warring- ton's sons, revered above all others, is one that may claim special consideration and indulgence, if any might. We hope to be able to refer again to this work in next month's Magazine, when, we true., substantial progress can be reported.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Bishop of Liverpool and Social Unrest.</head>
        <p>Bishop of Liverpool and Social Unrest.</p>
        <p>Writinri in the " Diocesan Gazette," the Bishop of Liverpool earnestly asks the Church clergy and laity of the diocese to pled with God in private, family, and public prayer for the restoration of peace in the industrial world. The unrest of the colliers, he says. is only one symptom amongst many of the strained relations which exist at present, in so many parts of the country and of the world between capital and labour and which are never likely to be completely removed until some system such as co-partnership or profit- sharing is established on a sound basis. There can he no question that the large majority of masters desire to deal justly with their men, and it is equally true that the large majority of men with to act fairly toward their masters. But it is also certain that there are hard and grasping masters who regard their men as mere machines for making them rich ; and there are selfish and unprincipled men who regard their masters as fair spoil out of whom they will wring as much as they can. Clergy and laity alike can help to form a public opinion both amongst masters and men so widespread and strong that this lack of common humanity and fair dealing will not be tolerated in English society. They can seek in private and in public to practise and inculcate the great principles of love and righteousness, and so to restore peace and contldeLce to the great masses of our people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Very Poor Man.</head>
        <p>A Very Poor Man.</p>
        <p>Our Vicar (says tbe Burtonwood Parish Magazine) was recently described as a "very poor man." lie is not prepared to deny the soft impeachment. "A very poor man." Such indeed he is, such be is likely to be. The minister of Christ always should be poor ; he never should be rich. The rich parson is a reproach to his Master and his Master's church. The poor parson needeth n)t to be ashamed of his poverty. Poverty is his honour, his glory,—if it is for Christ's sake—" Blessed are ye poor,—poor parsons." The very poor man in question has spent many hundreds of pounds on the Church and in the parish during his score of years as incumbent. This is a good investment which, however, will yield no yearly or half-yearly dividends in £s d. The interest will accrue on " The other side." About this expenditure there are no regrets, no repining. There is just one item of enforced expenditure which "the very poor man " resents and kicks against—the necessary expenses incurred in connection with the Builaing Fund. These, roughly, are as follows :—Pogtace and other charges, £55 ; Magazine spice £25 • hall furnishing, £35 ; making a vicarial bad debt of £ll5, anyhow. And this isa specimen of ‘• the businesslike methods of the laity," which is perpetually dinned into the parson's ears ! This suggests a good subject for debate— " Are the laity more business-like than the clergy ?"</p>
        <p>Welsh Dlsestablishment.—Protest Meeting. A meeting of protest against the proposed measure to Disestablish and Disendow the Church in Wales was held in St. Paul's schools, Kendrick-street on Monday evening. The Vicar (Rev W Bracecamp), who presided, said he hoped the meeting would realise that the Welsh Church was a part of the Church of England. It was the weaker part—in numbers, though not in usefulness —and therefore the stronger should support the weaker in the great struggle which was going on. Discussing the effects of disendowment, he said that thirteen years ago, when his predecessor accepted the living of St Paul's, the endowment was about £BO a year. During the three years he held the office be did his best to raise it, and since he left the wardens and; others had done their duty, and now the endowment stood at £lBO. How did it come about ?—by the voluntary contribu- tions of the members of the congregation, assisted by various associations making them grants from time to time, which they handed over to the Ecclesiastical Commissioners, who met the parish with a similar amount of money. Therefore no State, or Government had a right to say "The money belongs to us." (Applause.) It was the freewill offering of the people, and the Government should not touch it. (Applause.) Mr W H WESTBROOK said the attack on the Welsh Church was the preliminary attack on the English Church. The Church in Wales was the strongest religions body in Wales and It was neither weak nor inefficient. (Applause). Mr H J Stansfleld maintained that it was really truer to speak of dissent being established by law than to speak of the Church being so established, because they could in the case of the former point to a definite Act of Parliament whereby Nonconformity was recog- nized by law, namely, the Toleration Act. As a matter</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
