      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</p>
        <p>On Sunday week the Rev. W. Reid gave his fifth discourse on Immortality," which was continued on Sunday evening. Speaking of the Resurrection, he claimed that it and the Resurrection of Jesus Christ stood or fell together, and proceeded to prove that to account for Christ after the Crucifixion called for more and greater miracles than that os the Resurrection. He contended, however, for a rising from and not of the flesh, that, which sown at birth into the natural and corruptible, rising at death and taking to itself a body prepared of God, spiritual and possessed of form. The grain of wheat, the grub and dragon-fiv, and the bird formed within The egg-shell served as illustrations. He denied the rising of the physical body, and a period when the spirit was disembodied. He believed Christ's resurrection, like his birth, life, and death to be unique for reasons obviously necessary ; that His post- resurrection body was the original crucified body, re-u,ed a natural body for forty days which a natural environment demanded, and re-placed at their expiry by the spiritual body already in existence?; and that the two methods mentioned by St. Paul, Resurrection ' and change ' were exemplified in Him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Nonconformist View of Lent.</head>
        <p>A Nonconformist View of Lent.</p>
        <p>" Wycliffe Congregational Church Magazine " con- tains the following reference by the Pastor (Rev. John Yonge) to the season of Lent :—There are reasons why, if Christian people would accept the religious teaching (which the annual 40 days of Lent are intended to emphasize) they might find very great personal religious advantage. It is a good thing to be reminded that the Christian year is not altogether made up of Festivals. . . . Sorely, the wondrous memory of our Lord's Temptation, of his marvellous endurance, and Ills absolute triumph, must have teaching and inspira- tion for all who are His Disciplos ! It is a good thing for us to effectively call to mind the facts of our sinfulness, of the continual spiritual peril which besets this earthly life, and of the need of vigil- ance, and conflict, and conquest, if we are to realise the supreme purpose of life. Earnest and religious Chnch people cherish peculiar regard for this particular Church season, and diligently keep it as a period wherein they especially refrain iron) many social pleasures, recrea- tions and amusements. Probably with advantage! And it might do some, who pride themselves upon their Ecclesiastical Nonconformity, but who appear strangely " conformed to this world "by the persistency of their pursuit of more or less dissipating amusements—it would benefit them if they would improve this Church season by cultivating a more serious and earnest spiritual thoughtfulness ! It is a very serious sign of the times that the thirst for amusements has become so abnormally acute. The degree to which some professedly religious people devote spare time to such pursuits, week after week during leisure hours, is become almost a scandal ! It can only be derogatory to their Christian faith, and injurious to their Christian life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Primitive Methodist Church.</head>
        <p>Primitive Methodist Church.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the Warrington Circuit of the Primitive Methodist Church was held on the 29th February, the Rev IV Cooper occupying the chair. The yearly shedules of all branches of church work were found to be in satisfactory condition, and the member- ship in particular was found to be on the increase. Many deaths, emigrations and removals had taken place, but the circuit reported an increase of eight for the year. The representatives elected to the district committee were :—The Rev IV Cooper, Messrs It Bridge and .1 Griffiths, and the delegate appointed to attend the district meeting at Blackpool in May was Mr G Faulkner of Penketb. In connection with the Latchtord Church, several new trustees were appointed. Tole church is showing new signs of life and spiritual activity and a new set of officials are banding themselves together to carry on the work which bad been sustained under difficulties for many 3 ears. A special adjourned meeting had been arranged to make arrangements for a forward move- ment, and to consider what steps could be taken to deepen the spiritual life of the circuit geuerallY. Arrangements were also made to hold the next quarterly meeting at Hatton, which would he the occasicn of a demonstration. The services of Dr Elwin Dalton had been secured, and he would be present to preach and lecture. Invitations will be sent to surrounding circuits. The accounts were passed as satisfactory, and much satisfaction was expressed at the happy relation- ships existing between officials and the churches themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>BABY A SIGHT WITH SCALP TROUBLE.</head>
        <p>BABY A SIGHT WITH SCALP TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>" What the Cuticura Ointment and Soap did for our son was simply wonderful. Seven weeks after his birth my wife was very ill and had to go to the hospital for some few weeks. Consequently baby was handed over to the care of a person close by. Within a few weeks there appeared mattery wounds on the top of his head which rapidly spread to two large wounds. We honestly believe the person who had charge of baby did her utmost to cure him, obtaining various ointments for the purpose, but to no good. It became much worse and when my wife took him again he was a sight, poor little fellow, and the pain we could see was great. It spread rapidly to the back of the ears and appeared on the chest. " Of course we concluded it would necessitate the calling of a doctor, but my wife decided before doing so to try Cuticura Ointment and Soap for a few days. She started without delay, cleansing the affected parts with a sponge and Cuticura Soap, using warm water, then well applying the Cuticura Ointment. In a couple of days the change was great, and in a very short time all was well, his hair grew wonderfully, and to look at him now it is hard to credit that he ever had such wounds." (Signed) Francis Underhill, Pilton St., Barnstaple, Eng., July 17, 1911. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post free from nearest depot : Newbury, 27, Charterhouse Sq., London ; R. Towns &amp; Co., Sydney, N.S.W. ; Len non, Ltd., Cape Town ; Muller, Maclean A Co., Calcutta and Bombay ; " Cuticura," Boston, U.S A. Tender faced men should shave with Cuticura Soap Shaving Stick.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>EARLESTOWN RIOTS.</head>
        <p>THE EARLESTOWN RIOTS.</p>
        <p>Home Secretary and Riot Sentences.</p>
        <p>The Women's Freedom League recently passed a resolution sympathising with its members at Earles- town whose relatives are now serving sentences of imprisonment, charged with causing riots during the recent strike. The Home Office has now sent the following reply to the league's request for a reconsideration of the sentences :—" lam directed by the Secretary of the State to acquaint you that David II ulme appe.led to the Court of Appeal in November last for leave to appeal against his conviction, but the court refused his application. It was open to the other persons convicted on that occasion to bring their cases in like manner before the Court of Criminal Appeal,hut they have not done so. The Secretary of State bad the cases under consideration, but found no ground to justify him in advising his Majesty to reduce the sentences imposed."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>LEAGUE OF YOUNG LIBERALS.</head>
        <p>LEAGUE OF YOUNG LIBERALS.</p>
        <p>The fourth annual meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire Federation in association with the National League of Young Liberals was held on Saturday after- noon at the Reform Club, Manchester. The President, Mr. Robert Hopwood, of Stockport, was in the chair, and delegates attended from all parts of the two counties. Mr. Francis D. Acland, M.P., Under Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs, was also present. At the close of the meeting ther. was a reception by Mr. llarold Cawley, M.P., for Heywood division. The report stated that the League improved its position year by year. The total number of branches affiliated At the end of the year was 104, and the approximate membership 10,367. . -</p>
        <p>241 r HopWooa was re-elected president, Mr John Harrison was again appointed treasurer, and Messrs J W Percy Smith' and John Hugh Jones were elected hon. secretarees.</p>
        <p>Mr Acland, M.P., who was received with cheers, indicated the advantages which the Insurance Act will confer on the workers of the country generally, and said that if Liberals as a party were to go down after that Act was known then they would go down gallantly and be glad they had passed it whatever the result might be. (Cheers).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>1101RWAA..7</head>
