        <p>DOAN'S INVESTIGATIONS CONTINUED</p>
        <p>Warrington Man Comes Forward.</p>
        <p>Our recent announcement, that we were arranging t° conduct an inquiry into Doan's numerous local cone, has met with the widespread appreciation of Or readers. To-day a well-known Warrington residellt comes forward and gives his personal experience' wnic' he authorises us to publish for the benefit of others. Mr. E. Oakley, of 1, Bond-street, Sankey Bridge' Warrington, says :—" I can truthfully say that Dna° pillslsve done we a lot of good, and I have recopy' mended them to toy mates. " I used to be a sufferer from dropsy and kidsei trouble ; my limbs swelled very much. I,was subjent to severe pains in the small of my back, hips and lob* I consulted a doctor, and he advised me to go into et infirmary. I was in there for a time, and I got a hII better. Altogether I was laid up for ten weeks, got no real benefit until I tried Doan's backache kidne, pills. " Doan's pills are a splendid medicine ; they Wive made rue well again, and I shall take pleasure in log about them. (Signed) E. OAKLEY." After a lapse of more than eighteen mostlis, one ef our representatives had the pleasure of seeing Mr. Ortklq; who said:—' I have not needed to use Doan's 111.'t since they cured me so thoroughly. It was dropsy 09, I had, my body swelled considerably, and the kidnel excretions were unnatural and contained sediment' Doan's pills corrected them. I was treated for kidil trouble at the hospital, where I remained a month—e this time I spent twenty-one days in bed. " Doan's pills are indeed splendid. Numbers boe asked me about my case, end I always give the medicine high praise. I can't do otherwise ; four boxes of the pills cured me." Price 2/9 a box, 6 boxes 13/9 • of all dealers, or froill Foster McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Don't ask for backache and kidney PLII!,' ask distinet/y for boart's backache kidney pills, ID' same as Ur. Oakley had.</p>
        <p>PICKPOCKET AT THE FOOTBALL MATCH.* The assembly of a large crowd of people oiT exceptional facilities for those who wish to replenl their own coppers at the expense of members of crowd. There were no doubt a few of the lip': Lingered gentry at the football match at Wilderspool u` Wednesday and one case of alleged pocket-picking WW, brought before the magistrates at the Police Court Thursday morning. George Miller, 86, Shakespenreed street, Chorlton-on-Medlock Manchester, was charg.o with frequenting the Warrington football ground intent to commit a felony. Prisoner pleaded gui'" The Chief Constable stated that during the inter/ Mr. Paul, licensee of the Abbey Hotel, St. Helens, WI, occasion to leave the stand on the enclosure side of tl3' ground. Whilst passing down the staircase a numl of people collected, including the prisoner and t coniederates. During the wait Mr. Paul felt sorneont, hand in his hip pocket. On feeling in the pocket P, found that his purse, containing £2L in gold and cdhe,, ,coins,had been stolen. The prisoner tried to get aWIIOJ, but after a struggle he was given in charge of Inspelti Forster. His confederates made good their escape W. the money. s I Prisoner who was respectably dressed said he ships steward. He had been out of work four or ;al weeks and came to Warrington to see the foott'r match. He was going for a drink when the prosecul.°,l charged him with stealing his purse.—Prisoner AS 3" to prison for one month.</p>
        <p>Borough Police Court. TUESDAY.—Before Councillor Roberts (in the Colonel Edelsten and Messrs. W. Richardson ss" Locher. r o°, ON ENCLOSED PREMISES.—John W n oosev or settled abode and John Higharn and William .Spice.'lll Edge-lane, Liverpool, were charged on remand id having teen found on enclosed premises at AO to Station on Tuesday February 27th with intentti, commit a felony. Defendants pleaded not Piod Mr. Tait, of London, on behalf of the London 1.50 North-Western Railway Company, stated that at tbe on Monday February 26th prisoners were seen in or booking hall at Arpley Station by a foreman PO'„,GO named Bennett. Woosey was at one time in the sets i# of the Company as a porter. When questioned .titje prisoner replied that he was only going to staY I°oo station a few minutes. On going off duty Bee es called at the Wilderspool crossing and information _de given to the police. A search of the station waSl33:O by three police officers but the prisoners had run s"tly in the direction of the bridge. They were subseollo, arrested. Prisoners said they had no intention of ing but went to find somewhere to sleep. WooseY_„"to fined 20s. including costs and the other two defend,'" were discharged. 1163 UNLAWFUL LOITERING.— Thomas Keen' of no set t 1 vies charged on remand with loitering slie intent to commit a felony on the 29th February. joy Chief Constable stated that about 7 o'clock on Thursber evening the prisoner was seen in company with andt tie man by P.C. Cameron at the rear of Goroey-lane. .00 seemed to be acting in a suspicious manner. Keel= " sent to prison for 14 days. WIFE DLSERTION.—John Joseph Tomli W nsou,.oo, Laira-street, was summoned by his wife for disert%v Complainant said he had not given heranything for ellie weeks and detendant on the other hand said hie,' pr had an uncontrollable temper. An order was rnaae favour of the wife of 10s per week. ‘VEDMESDAY.—Before Mr Charles White (in the c" Mr Jackson Holmes and Mr W T Williams. 003 ALLEGED THEFT or CLoTHlNG.—Charles Bate lice Elizabeth Bate, 66a, Buttermarket-street, and Doraville, 38, Cockhedge-lane, were charged with stve7l, log a quantity of wearing apparel from Veronica Feted 68, Buttermarket-street. The Chief Constable 040 that the prisoners lived in a cottage adjoining the o,rho of Mrs Peers, who had missed drapery goods. a* matter had been reported to the police, who felll3or window insecure by which anyone from the neiglib°,oo ing property could effect an entrance. The fera-ca' prisoners had admitted the offence. On the first sion Mrs Bate had persuaded her husband to stes.,g' oriS property and it, would appear the other prisoners fof lin from time to time. The case was adjource. seven days.</p>
        <p>A Caution Against Hasty Judgment. 00 At the close of his sermon at the Parish Charnticie Sunday morning the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) 1:11Iver brief reference to the terrible cloud which was 'lO hanging the industrial world. There were three P° which he desired to impress upon the congregatil;o v 7 (I)They ought to pray to God for the spirit of '5 90c1 Justice and love. (2) They could refrain from harsh 00cl hasty judgment. They did not know everything.Aeo• there was perhaps a great deal to be said for both el'be (3) They should practise economy. They might., asked to deny themselves to supply the anso necessities of their fellow creatures. They did Pei know how long the strike would last, but they rds that if it continued for any length of time the lore of the Unions would soon come to an end and t tie would be great distress. They would therefore,od well to practise economy and not to spend their ril-tbeo on anything which was not absolutely necessary, ocilv if the time di el come they might he able to suPP some measure the needs of their fellow creatures.</p>
