      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Eitt It 'l4lotor</head>
        <p>Eitt It 'l4lotor</p>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER "FIND."</p>
        <p>The "Mysterious find" repot ted in the last issue of this paper reminded me of a trick played on a keen young reporter at the outset of his career on a journal with which I was formerly associated. Three or four of 119 were leaving a meeting together when a colleague rushed up and said to the " tenderfoot " " P.C. kloxen's founi a human hand." "Indeed," exclaimed the youngster excitedly, "where has he found it ?" The answer was: "In his pocket ! " A SHARP LESSON. In the same town we were troubled with a lazy fellow who preferred to prig his news rather than gather it for himself; and when one of us was writing up local " pars" at the Police Court or elsewhere he would frequently glance over the scribe's shoulder and help himself to the news. At last, to cure, the unscrupulous one of his objectionable habit the rest of us deliberately set a trap for him. Manu- facturing a startling but entirely fictitious piece of personal intelligence concerning a man whose place of business was directly opposite the office in which the troublesome one was employed, we agreed that the one who happened to sit next to him on the following day shnuld transcribe this from his notes, taking care to begin with a sensational headline. The fellow for I whom this was intended copied it as had been expected, and, notwithstanding his proximity to the townsman chiefly affected, published it without verification. There were ructions, of course ; it was ' a costly experience, but the offender became quite a reformed and exemplary character. THE UNION OFFICES.</p>
        <p>The projected lease cf premises in King-street by the Warrington Board of Guardians recalled a singular scheme of the kind in another union with which lam familar. The Board resolved to build a new suite of offices of their own and the scheme was of a scandalously elaborate and extravagant character. I incurred the wrath of the ambitious promoters by adverse criticisms and comments, contrasting the treatment of the poor and destitute with the reckless expenditure on a palatial pile, furnished as the Guardians individually would not think of furnishing any building the cost of which had to come out of their private purses. The chairman took serious notice of the newspaper opposition, and in triumphant tones thought to vindicate the Board's policy by calling attention to the fact that the scheme was being com- pleted well within the estimates. It sounded all right at the moment, but on reference to the items it was easily shown that the estimates were ridiculously high, so that if they had been exceeded it would have been an everlasting disgrace. THE MAYOR'S ADVICE.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the excellent Alliance of Honour last week the Major of Warrington, speaking as a medical man, urged that parents and others in positions of responsibility and influence in relation to youth should make it part of their duty to judiciously instruct their charges respecting sexual matters. False modesty or a feeling of inability to present the truth without being grievously misunderstood lead to general avoidance of the subject, and not infrequently to great evil which might have been anticipated and prevented. I know parish clergymen who as to these very things systematically instruct the young fellows in their respective circles, with decidedly good and encouraging results. Toe Itev. Paul Bull, a well- known navy chaplain, has undertaken a special campaign of the sort, and handles the question splendidly. He is a High Anglican, affects a monkish garb, and is called " Father "—indeed, I believe he is connected with a celibate community ; but he is a magnificent specimen of manhood, and he certainly knows how to deal with young men. He exerts a remarkable influence on the lower deck, and has effected considerable improvement in the habits and manners of Jack ashore and afloat. Among other things, he induced the men on his ship to take a pledge against the use of bad language, with a condi- tion that anyone using an expletive should pay a fine, the money thus accumulated to be applied to the common benefit. The habit thus aimed at had in some cases obtained so strong a hold that the fines led to the speedy acquisition of gramophones and other luxuries; but it was eventually overcome by the great majority. Once " Father" Bull overheard one of the men exclaim in reference to a vile smell from below, " Oh, wot a delicious haroma! "and his comment was that, under similar circumstances a while before the same fellow would have growled, " Wot a -- stink ! " A BANNED JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>May I Irak back to the pressmen for a moment? The other day the Executive Council of the National Union of Journalists made emphatic protest against the action of the Blackpool Town Council in deptiving Mr. W. R. Bayes of his seat on the Library Committee of the Council on account of articles appearing in a newsaper with which he is connected in the ordinary course of his journalistic avocation, and expressed the view that such an intolerant attitude to the Press on the part of a Corporation which has been indebted to it so much in the past is contrary to the best traditions of English local government and deserving of the highest censure and disapprobation. I happen to know Mr. Bayes well. He comes of Quaker stock, and has all his life belonged to news- paperdom. His father first began business as a printer in the town of Todmorden, on the East Lanca- shire and West Yorkshire border, but removed thence to Brighouse, in the West Riding, and there spent the greater part of his life. He established a weekly paper there, and on that Mr. W. It. Bayes bad his training. He went thence to his present situation in Blackpool. He had an uncle who attained eminence as an artist in London. A FAMOUS ENTERTAINER.</p>
        <p>When I read of the death of Mr. George Grossmith, I thought (as the walrus and the carpenter in "Alice" talked) of many things. When first I had to write an account of one of his inimitable two hours' entertain- ments, I was amused by the remarks of some who oat near me. They made quite a grievance of the fact that, over and above his enviable mental equipment, Mr. Grossmith presumed to appear before them with merely a piano and a dress suit. He had crowded the biggest hall in the town, but these people in the reserved seats audibly expressed disappointment because there was no change of costume. What, then, had they gone for to see? Evidently a common or garden comedian, with artificially-reddened nose, an absurdly small hat, exaggerated pantaloons, a raucous voice, and doggerel verses with stale and sickening jokes about beer, cheese, mother-in-law, and the rest. When next I encountered Mr. Grossmith on tour, I was stationed in a smaller town to which his name and fame had apparently not penetrated, for, to the enter- tainer's amazement and disgust, there was a wretchedly poor attendance. He did not do as I recollect the late Madame Antoinette Sterling doing under the like circumstances ; depressed by the sight of the beggarly array of empty benches, the eminent con contralto sent emissaries into the highways and hedges to compel the poor to come in! The late Mr. , George Grossmith's two sons are both well-known ' comedians, but how many of my readers know the deceased's father ? My first reporting job was an address on disestablishment, and my very next engage- ment was at a "Humorous lecture on lecturing " by the grandfather of the surviving Grosstniths, who sometimes afterwards had a fatal apoplectic seizure while entertaining his fellow-guests at a dinner patty. IN VIEW OF HOSPITAL SUNDAY. As Mr. James O'Donovan has observed in a column article on the subject, the position of the voluntary hospitals of the country in relation to Mr. Lloyd George's National Insurance Bill has so far atm acted less public attention than other aspects of the scheme not of greater importance. Those engaged in hospital management, however, are under no delusion in the matter. At the conference of hospital representatives held in London a while ago, Mr. William A. West, chairman of the St. George's Hospital, who presided, expressed the view that these institutions would be profoundly affected when the scheme came into opera- tion. The possible effect of insurance against sickness as indicated at the meeting, may be thus generally outlined :-1. A serious falling-off in voluntary sub- scriptions and legacies on the part of those who will be compelled to contribute t the Insurance fund. 2. The consequent necessity of a State subsidy, accompanied by State supervision or control. Even if the hospitals did not suffer from a decline in volun- tary aid, the fact that so many of them will be utilised for the institutional treatment of insured persons would of itself bring about some system of inspection on the part of the local health committees, who would naturally desire to know if the patients were receiving adequate and economical treat- ment. Thus public supervision will have to be faced by many hospitals, apart altogether from a State subsidy. Should a system of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>NEW TURKISH AMBASSADOR.</head>
        <p>NEW TURKISH AMBASSADOR.</p>
        <p>Ahmed Riza Bey, a member of the Senate, and ex-President of the Turkish Chamber, has been nominated Ambassador to London. He is close on seventy, and a man of com- manding personality, tall, erect, and hand. some. In the history of nations, there are few more romantic caxeers than that of Ahmed Riza Bey.</p>
