      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>"THE NATIVES AND BIG GAME OF EAST AFRICA."</head>
        <p>"THE NATIVES AND BIG GAME OF EAST AFRICA."</p>
        <p>Interesting Lecture.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. O. Thompson, A.M.1.C.E., lectured before the members of the Warrington Society at the Old Academy on Monday evening on "The natives and big game of East Africa." Those who were fortunate enough to hear to Mr. Thompson's account of the con- struction of the Uganda It iilway two years ago anticipated a treat on the present oocasion, and they were not disappointed, a fascinating subject being handled in a most instructive and entertaining manner. Mr. L. Greening presided over a good attendance and at the outset confessed to n great admiration for the men who ventured to tbe far end.; of the earth to encounter " the wizked elephant and the vindictive rhino." " Quick excitability," declared Mr Thompson," 13 the chief mental characteristic of Africans. They are dominated by the transient emotions or impulses of the moment and neither remember what has preceded nor look forward to what is likely to follow. The African is greedy and covetous enough, but he is too indolent in his ways and too disconnected in his ideas, to make any attempt to better himself, or to undertake any labour which does not produce a speedy and visible result. His mind is far nearer to the animal world than is that of the European or Asiastic, and exhibits something of the animal's placidity and want of desire to rise beyond the stage he has reached. In respect to decency in the wide sense of the word, he seems to stand on a lower level than most races. This is particularly noticeable in the disposal of the dead who in nearly all East African tribes are simply thrown into the jungle for the hyenas to eat. The East African throws his parent or child away without any sign of reverence or affection. The absence of political development, continued the lecturer, was accompanied by a correspondingly rudimentary stage of religious ideas. We now knew sufficient of the East African races to say that none of them possessed any religious mystem comparable in development to the Juju of West Africa. Another indication of deficiency of religionv, feeling was the little progress made by Mohammedanism. The simplicity and directness of that form of theism made it attractive to most savage races who had any concep- tion of a personal God, and its success in northern and western Africa had been remarkable. In East Africa it bad never made any progress at all, except on the coast and even there the strictly religious side of its teach- ing seemed to have remained without effect. The most definite religious beliefs seemed to prevail among the Mosai, who prayed to God (Eng-ai) for children, rain and victory, and believed in two deities, one black and benevolent, the other red and cruel, but fortunately also distant. _ _</p>
        <p>Perhaps the most remarkable, as it was certainly the saddest characteristic of the African races, was the burden of suffering which they had borne. It was far greater than that which had fallen to the lot of other uncivilized people, such as the aborigines of America or Australia. Yet the African races as a rule were neither physically weak nor cruel. But they were in the immediate neighbourhood of much stronger races, who long regarded them as the legitimate victims of the slave trade ; they lived in a country exposed to sudden natural visitations, but were not sufficiently civilized to prevent or in any way mitigate floods, epidemics and droughts. Their chief idea of activity was to wage wars in which mere plunder and destruction, rather than conquest, were the object, and there had rarely been an African potentate who did not wallow in blood. Having given a most interesting description of the modes of life of the various tribes,Mr.Thompson quoted the opinion of Sir Charles Eliot that " We have every reason to congratulate ourselves without undue self- laudation, on the accomplishment in East Africa of one of the greatest works of humanity which the world has seen. It is only ten or fifteen years since the whole country from the ocean to the Congo groaned beneath oppression and bloodshed. . . . Now tha slave trade is at an end. Massacres are equally a thing of the past and if raiding and inter-tribal wars still continue they continue only in the remoter districts and it may be hoped that with the extension of our effective adminis- tration they ,s ill disappear altogether. Africa has not yet become civilized but it has become possible to think of civilization." . .</p>
        <p>—.— Mr. Thompson devoted the second part of his lecture to a description of the big game of East Africa. The most wonderful variety of animals was found there—in fact Mr Roosevelt, writing of his first journey up country, said "it was literally like passing through a vast zoological garden." The lecture was well illustrated with lantern slides many of them depicting incidents in Mr. Roosevelt's expedition. The lantern was manipulated by Mr Frith. The CHAIRMAN made some interesting comments on the animal life of East Africa and also referred to the devastation wrought by sleeping sickness. Mr E J HALL. in proposing a vote of thanks to the lecturer, said one could not help feeling pleased by the comparison between the British colonisation of East Africa and the colonisation of the Congo. While the advent of the European on the Congo aggravated the evils of slavery, the appearance of the Britisher in East Africa had destroyed the slave trade— s fact which redounded to the honour and credit of our country. As time advanced and all the healing and helpful influences of civilization and religion were brought to bear, Africa might become one of the noblest lauds on the face of the earth. Mr. LoFrnousE seconded and the vote was heartily passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>STOMACH CATARRH And Digestive Troubles Cured by DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS.</head>
        <p>STOMACH CATARRH And Digestive Troubles Cured by DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Black, 34, Marlbour St., Dublin, writes :—" I suffered over two years from very severe pains in the stomach and digestive troubles and was so bad that I could hardly sleep or eat, and no food would stop on my stomach. I tried many different medicines in vain and I got very weak and low. Then I tried Dr. Cassell's Tablets and almost at once my food agreed with me, and noxv I can eat anything and am a different woman." Dr. Cassell's Tablets will cure all forms of nerve, organic and physical weakness in children or adults. Price 101 d , lid., and 2s. 9d. of all chemists. Send 2d. in stamps to Dr. Cassell's Co. Ltd., King at. W., Manchester, for a free trial box.</p>
