      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>RETALIATION.</head>
        <p>RETALIATION.</p>
        <p>It is extremely unlikely, in the preront condition of opinion in the leading protectionist countries, that we shall be abie by negotiation to secure any very sub- stantial lowering of tariffs.—Mr. L. S. Amery, M.P., a well-known Protectionist, in " The Great Question." THE AMERICAN TRUST MOTHER. "The Republican party has come to realise that high tariff duties encourage combinations of capital by suppressing competition."—President Taft at New York, February 12th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>RATEPAYERS' BURDEN.</head>
        <p>THE RATEPAYERS' BURDEN.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER DEBATE ON THE COST OF EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR ON SCHOOLBOYS' INSOLENCE.</p>
        <p>The much-debated topic of the ever-increasing cost of education was again raised ht the meeting of the Town Council oa Tuesday evening when the estimates of the Education Committee came up for confirmation. The discussion chiefly turned upon the urgent necessity for an increased contribution from Imperial sources towards the relief of the ratepayers' burden, and some striking comparisons of the expenditure on schools and salaries locally were submitted. Councillor Roberts, as chairman of tbe Education Committee, formally moved the adoption of the minute requesting the Town Council to provide £24,000 from the borough rate for educational purposes during the ensuing financial year. Councillor BIRTLES seconeed. •</p>
        <p>Councillor BENSRALL moved an amendment that the minute bs referred back. He said he had thought it hater not to dwell upon the general question of our education policy, but, to invite the serious attention of the Council to the increasing contribution which had to be paid by the ratepayer as against the taxpayer in an unfair and unequitable proportion. The figures sub- mitted by Mr. Jolley at the meeting of the Education Committee were really alarming. The expenditure on education bad increased 67 per cent, within the lost ten years. The increased contribution made by the tax- payer worked out at 59 per cent., as against an increase for which the ratepayer was responsible of 158 per cent. When the Education Committee took over the control of affairs, of every £3 that was spent on education in Warrington, the ratepayer found £1 and the taxpayer £2. Had those ratios teen maintained, the taxpayer would be now finding £36,000 and the ratepayer £lB,OOO, but instead of the last-named sum the ratepayer had to provide £24,000. Councillor ROBERTS : I submit with great respect that this is absolutely out of order. The proportion paid to the cost of education by the taxpayer and the ratepayer respectively is a question fixed for us by Imperial statute and we ourselves have unfortunately nothing whatever to do with it. Therefore I submit it does not come within the purview of the discussion of the estimates of the Education Committee. __</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : I hope you will not allow Councillor Roberts to carry his ruling from the Educa- tion Committee into this Council. It is sufficient for him to rule it out of order there without trying to do the same here.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR it is not ttrictly in order but I think it may be allowed as being interesting and illustrative of Councillor Henshall's arguments generally. I think ; It is in the interests of the public too. Councillor ROBERTS said he accepted the ruling, although he felt be must call attention to his point of view as Chairman of the Education Committee. He did not object to any discussion which would put before the reople of Warrington the facts as they obtained and give the local authority the opportunity of showing now utterly helpless it was in dealing with the question referred to by Councillor llenshell. Councillor HENstiALL said that whoever was to blame for the constant increase of the burden, it was a very serious thing. There seerred to be some misunderstand- ing as to the relations between the ratepayer and the taxpayer. He had heard able financial authorities in that Council say it did not make very much differenee who paid, as in the long run it all came out of the same pocket. He would, however, point out that only those persons who had an income of over RHO per annum had to pay income tax. He did not suppose that more than one out of every six ratepayers paid taxes. He con- tended, therefore, that the constantly rising expenditure was falling upon those who were the least able to bear the burden, lie need not dwell upon the tragic position of those who had only a little property upon which to live, and of those whose earning power had passed sway ; or of the small shopkeeper struggling to make ends meet when the education rate was increased by another 6.1. in the R. That would be a very serious item indeed. It might be asked supposing his contention to be right, what was the use of bothering ? The general attitude seemed to be that he was the seise man who " pays his taxes and nothing axes." They all agreed that education should be a matter of Imperial expendi- ture, but still the attitude of the ratepayer seemed to be summed up in the words : "Their's not to make reply, Their's not to reason why Their's but to pay and sigh, Thankless 600." (Laughter.) All the education authorities were seething with disco ntent, because of the inadequate aid received from the Imperial Exchequer. Additional obligations were being imposed upon the committees. At Warrington they had to provide a clinic—an excellent thing, but the Government was penalising them for keeping the children healthy. The clinic would cost £BOO, and a loss of £1,200 in grants would be sustained through loss of attendances in trying to improve the children's health. Ile believed that if local authorities would unite and siy to the Government "We will no longer carry out your Act unless you contribute your fair proportion of the cost," their position would be impregnable and unassailable. Councillor LASHFORD seconded the amendment.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNET'. said the progressive increase of the education rate was a matter which they could rot fail to regard with considerable seriousness. Whilst there was point in what Councillor Henshall said about the income tax, he had quite overlooked the fact that the whole of the revenue was by no means raised through the income tax. The ratepayers were called upon to pay indirectly through their tobacco, whisky, tea, coffee, cocoa, es well as through the Post Office and in a dozen other ways. Broadly in the long run there was not a great amount of difference as to whether the burden was borne by the taxpayer or the rateyayer. What he felt was that the incidence of taxation as between the taxpayer and the ratepayer should be very drastically adjusted. Municipalities ought to have a large share of the increment and development taxes on land, and if only they cried aloud the Government would realize their grievance and endeavour to lighten the burden upon the ratepayers. By a statesmanlike method of readjustment, local authorities would be able to carry out urgent improve- ments and no-one would suffer, while the community as a whole would undoubtedly benefit.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON : Wen you have spent the money you have got to pay. Continuing, he said the estimates provided for an increase in the rates of 31d in the Some wanted to know why there had been that increase, and why it could not be kept down. If they were not getting value for their money they were not doing their duty as trustees of the public purse. The question was whether they were getting full value for the increased call upon the rates. (A Vows; "No.') The incre- ments to teachers were costing £1,500. They were not earning any proportionate increase in grants, but were sustaining a loss under that head, and he considered that they were therefore being overpaid. Annual increases were being made until everybody was getting £l4O per annum, or more, for doing work which in some cases was not worth more than £25 or £4O. Thrre was something radically wrong. It did not matter whethe the money was raised by local or imperial taxation ; it they ware not usiug the people's money in the beef interests of the community, they were making a great mistake. The Education Committee should see that full value for money was obtained instead of providing for every fad and idea that cropped up, and paying for school buildings which were half empty,but staffed with full staffs who did not earn their wages. Councillor (PEMBERTON said *the question' of the rising education rate was very much more serious than had even been represented. The estimates provided for a substantial increase,but they were already committed to a future expenditure of £50,000. That was only for the next few years. Some of the education enthusiasts —or fanatics—said that was only the commencement, and that a great deal of leeway bad to be made up. Already the borough rate practically amounted to 10s 7d in the £, including is lid in the £ which was derived from profits of municipal undertakings. When the Town Council took over the administration of education the number of scholars was 13,382, and now the numbhr was 13,600. Apparently there was only an increase of 220 scholars. It was a fact that when the schools were taken over children tinder five years of age were admitted, and the number approximately was 1,000. Therefore there was practically an increase in the number of children of school age of 1,300 more than was the case in 1902. The total salaries of teachers in 1903 was £18,500, and now they had estimates for £27,162, but the number of teachers was only the same. The increase under that head was equal to £8,600 or a rate of 81d in the £. The number of teachers was 275, and there were 12 student teachers. In 1903 the teachers numbered 335, but they included 68 pupil teachers, as against 12 now. They could look forward with certainty to another sixpenny rise because they were committed to the outlay of £50,000 on new schools. That meant that the sinking fund and interest would absorb a rate of 2..(3, and the staffing of new schools ts 31d rate. With regard to medical inspection, they were placed in the shameful position that because children bad to be kept at home for their own good, there must be a loss of about £1,200 in grants. They could gain money by being medically cruel to the children. Whatever value there might be in education, even the fanatics admitted that it was for the good oi the nation as a whole, and that being the case the nation should pay for it. If they were meeting their estimates properly they would have to pay a Is. ..OJ. rate, but £5OO was being taken from the reserve fund. They were told they must have a reserve fund of £7,ooo—the Council was coerced into it—but the fact that £1,571 had been given back in two years proved that it was not wanted, it was inequitable that the ratepayers of Warrington should have to meet a rate of is. 104. in the £ for a national service like education, while the rate in the neighbouring county of Cheshire was only 11d. They ought not to tolerate such a state of affairs but should join other Corporations in petitioning the Government against the present inequality of taxation. The speaker complained that money was being wasted on the technical classes which were not fit to be mentioned in the same breath with the old apprenticeship system. Ile had an old foreman working for him who could do a job while a student trained in the modern style was preparing his T square and other paraphernalia.</p>
        <p>I The MAYOR laid stress upon the fact that the con tinned rise in the rates, which was in great measure due to the increasing cost of education, had a deterrent effect on the commercial activities of centres like Warrington. All classes of , inhabitants were dependent upon trade but I advancing rates almost prohibited the introduction of new industries. The suggestion that they should , petition Parliament to remove from them a fair pro- portion of their burdens was a very good one. Whether full value for the education expenditure was being received or not, they did not want to have to pay all the account. (Hear, hear.) He saw as much ignorance in the world in the course of his ordinary observations as he did 25 years ago. (A voice: A great deal more.") With regard to the effect on the children who were looked upon as the growing hope of the future, he thought one striking thing was that they were comparatively destitute of any proper respect for their seniors. Ile saw a great change in that respect. They were ' INSOLENT AND CHEEKY TO THEIR ELDERS, rind seemed to think that so much was made of them that they were the most important part of the commuaity. The whole system was wrong and wanted re-modelling. The sooner it was done the better, In any case they ought strongly to support any petition to Parliament for a readjustment of their burdens. Alderman JAMES EVANS said that if the position was as stated by Councillor Pemberton, it was difficult to explain satisfactorily to the ratepayers for what purpose all that money had been spent. That was the point which was creating the greatest dissatisfaction in the town. They did not realize what they were getting for the difference l etween a rate of 106, and a rate of Is. 101 While evety other committee was practising economy with efficiency, the Corporation was faced every year with an increasing expenditure on educa- tion. Councillor RonenTs, in replying to the discussion, said that on the general question as to whether the adjustment of the cost of education as between tax- payer and ratepayer was a fair one, he was absolutely in accord with every sentiment uttered in that direction. He agreed that education was an imperial question but unfortunately the Town Council had nothing to do with that. They were only charged by Parliament with the administration of local education on the lines set out for them in Education Acts as interpreted by the Board of Education. Their helplessness in that matter was pitiable. He quite admitted there was plenty of room for criticism on the general question of the administration of education through the Board of Education. The Council had previously expressed</p>
        <p>itself strongly on matters of that kind, and if they could do anything, in conjunction with other education authorities, to bring additional pres- sure to hear on the Government, with a view to a fairer adjustment, by all means let them do so. They had done their little best already by passing a resolution in 1910 on that very question. As a result of the joint action of education authorities there was at present sitting a committee appointed by the Government for the purpose of considering how it was possible, and to what extent some adjustment might be brought about. He expressed the hope that the amendment would not be pressed to a division. If the minute was sent back, nothing could be done with the matter. Continuing, Councillor ROBERT; said he admitted that the criticism which had been offered was candid and honest, but some of the statements were based on mistaken impressions. For instance Alderman Bolton said that the salaries of teachers had gone up £l5OO. The scale under which automatic increases were given, was fixed by the Council several years ago. If there was any desire to alter it, the Council had power by resolution, to rescind the scale and submit an amended one. The net increases to teachers amounted only to £445 on thescale, and £6OO was required for the staffing of the Evelyn-street school. The working bilance, which was built up in accordance with an Act of Parlia- ment, had been depleted for the purpose of preventing the rates for the year being required to find the total amount of the deficit on the estimates. That was made somewhat urgent because, by the abolition of the school fees, the Council lost £l,BOO a year. They had to keep a sufficient amount in the bank to meet the requirements of the administration of the Education Act, because the auditors appointed by the Government would not allow any debit against their funds for interest on an over-draft. The Education Committee were Simon Pores in that respect; they did not have overdraughts whatever other committees might do. No committee of the House of Commons would ever have the opportunity of coming down on them. When the Council took over the administration of education in 1903, Warrington was lamentably behind its require- ments both in regard to equipment and accommodation. For some time to come they would have leeway to make up. The standard of staffing and accommodation to-day was very different from the standard which was in operation in 1903. They were required to have a larger proportion of adult teachers and a smaller proportion lof apprentices. They were obliged to have more cubic feet of air space per child in every school, and the general cost of equipment was considerably in excess of what it was in 1903. Alderman TINNION : Are we any better for it ? . . . . -</p>
        <p>The AYOR : I was going to ask "Cal bono- Councillor ROBEttTs : lam not justifying what we have to do. lam simply trying to explain how utterly impossible it is for us to set up any standard different in any respect from the generally accepted standard set up by the Board of Education, which is tle all- powerful authority. Alderman BOLTON: You don't need to go in for fads.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS : Whatever fads we have gone in for—</p>
        <p>Alderman BoLToN : Are all costing money. Councillor RousnTs : Everything we have done has had to be submitted to this Council as the education authority and duly sanctioned. Summing up the causes of the increased deficiency, Councillor Roberts said the staffing of Evelyn-street school accounted for £6OO ; equipment of the school, £255 ; increase of teachers' salaries, £445 ; professional services for school build- ings, .C2OO ; dental clinic, £190; repayment of loans for Evelan-street school and sites for new schoals, £989. The Education Committee had no objection to the kind of criticism, which had been uttered that night, but in the main it was like" water on a duck's back," because it did not apply to them. He denied that there was any extravagance, so far as they were able to prevent it. Councillor PEMBERTON said the number of teachers in 1903 was 267, but in the same schools to-day there were only 263. The salaries were, however, £8,500 higher. Alderman TINNIoN suggested the withdrawal of the amendment, as no good purpose could be served by pressing it to a division. The MAYOR said the bettor course to take would be to hold a special meeting of the Council with a view to considering joint action with other authorities in petitioning Parliament. The local Education Com- mittee was merely automatic and had to do what it was told according to the Act. Alderman BOLTON objected to the withdrawal of the amendment, as it was intended to be an expression of opinion that the Education Committee was not as economical as it should be in the use of the ratepayers' money. Councillor PEMBERTON : If the Education Com- mittee is to be allowed to continue to spend money as it likes, I don't wish to be a member of it. Money is chucked away. Alderman JAMES EVANS said the responsibility largely rested on the Council, so far as any neglect of duty was concerned. The amendment was put to the vote and defeated, only seven members supporting it. Councillor PEMBERTON asked for the names of those who had voted to be recorded. - _</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY TowS CLERK •. It is impossible to take them now that the vote has been taken. It is too late. Councillor PEMBERTON : I insist upon it in accord- ance with the standing order. The Alikvoß ruled that the names could not now be recorded. Councillor LASIIFORD: I think Councillor Pemberton is quite in order. The MAYOR: Is he in order in not recognizing the decision of the chair ? The matter then dropped.</p>
        <p>" GOODALLS " Furnishers and Decorators, EXPERTS IN</p>
