      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>DECORATION.</head>
        <p>DECORATION.</p>
        <p>Designs and estimates free.</p>
        <p>KING STREET, MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. N.--,N,AwiNe-•^^,N,—,•e•N,.,v•-‘1 ,</p>
        <p>AN OLD STORY RE-TOLD. THE MOUSE IN LIQUOR.</p>
        <p>A mouse accidentally fell into a bowl of liquor which the housewife had set by the door to cool. The family cat happened to come by at the moment, and to its traditional enemy the drowning mouse made pathetic appeal for aid in its extremity. It pretended that it would prefer to die as so many of its relatives had done, urging that none of these had precipitated the end by intemperance, so that it would be disgraceful to die of intoxica- tion, whereas if it were killed by the cat, the jury would have no hesitation in returning the honourable verdict that it had died by a catastrophe such as might at any moment overtake any of them. He closed by saying that if pussy would help him out and put him on the floor to run about until he was dry,she might eat him. No sooner said than done— the former part, that is, for as soon as the mouse was assisted to the ground it darted into a hole beyond reach of its friend the enemy, so that the cat, who was to have had the next move in the game, was unable to 46 go.lt - .</p>
        <p>You're a nice article ! " it said to the mouse who had turned round to grin in its face.</p>
        <p>"How do you know ? " asked the mouse. "You haven't tasted of me, so you can't tell whether I'm nice or not."</p>
        <p>"Well, I know this," said the cat "you're not a man of your word." "How do you make that out ! " demanded the mouse adding if you talk like that, and let other people hear you, I shall put the matter into the hands of my solicitor. I would have you know that I have some regard for my reputation." "You haven't kept your promise, at all events," said the cat. "What promise? " asked the mouse. "You know quite well that our bargain was that if I lifted you out of the liquor I might eat you," said the cat. "Did I promise that ? " asked the mouse, pretending to be surprised. "You did indeed," insisted the cat. " Ah, but I was in liquor then," shyly explained the mouse. "Well, that's a licker," said the cat "you're going to run off, then ? " "Yes, I'm going to run off," assented the mouse, suiting the action to the word. LIGHT AFTER SUNSET.</p>
        <p>Just now a curious thing may be seen in the heavens. It is very pretty, and, more- over, it is one of the things which has never been explained. If you will watch after sunset you may see a cone of pearly light which stretches from the western horizon far away up into the heavens. Look westward after the sun has disappeared and watch for a curious light in the sky which stretches upwards and sometimes reaches the zenith. This light has been watched for long ages. It was known to the ancients and has puzzled astronomers ever since the Chaldeans tried to account for it. As it is always near the Zodiac it has been known for centuries as the Zodiacal Light. It seems to be of the same nature as the Aurora Borealis and Australis, and some day our scientists may be able to tell us how it is caused.</p>
        <p>But the fact that we don't understand it need not prevent us from enjoying a very pretty and very interesting phenomenon.</p>
        <p>- • a • - - • This week I am giving my readers a fine little poem on "Pluck," which I have taken from an American Magazine.</p>
        <p>PLUCK. Did you tackle that trouble that came your way With a resolute heart and cheerful?</p>
        <p>Or hide your face from the light of day With a craven soul and fearful? 0, a trouble's a ton, or a trouble's an ounce, Or a trouble is what you make it; And it isn't a fact that you're hurt that counts-- But only how did you take it? You are beaten to earth ? Well! well ! what's that ? Come up with a smiling face. It's nothing against you to fall down flat, But to lie there—that's disgrace. The higher you're thrown, wily the higher you bounce. Be proud of your blackened eye. It isn't the fact that you're licked that counts, It's how did you fight, and why? And though you be done to the death—what then? If you battled the best you could, If you played your part in the world like men, Why, the Critic would call it good. Death comes with a crawl or comes with a bounce, And whether he's slow or spry, It isn't the fact that you're dead. that counts, But only bow did you die?</p>
        <p>—Harrison Lee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance at the Wycliffe P.S.A. meeting. Mr B Farrar presided and made referenoe to their 19th anniversary. When they formed the society there was a very severe coal strike, which lasted about tbree months, but he hoped that when they celebrated their 19th anniversary next Sunday they would have heard of a-settlement of this strike. Mr Kinsworth gave *a address upon "is it well ? " Mr Payton played an organ solo in his usual masterly style. Mr Joseph Owen and Mr W Beswick also took part in the meeting. This was the first Sunday in the society's year, and it was reported that 221 members had been enrolled,which was regarded as a good start.</p>
