      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March, 9, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March, 9, 1912.</p>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>[ALL .RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>llows mat:-</p>
        <p>"He put it into the hands of a very good detective at Scotland Yard, and the other day this man went over the studio with Mr. Ten- nant, and after searching for ages, like these people always do, he found something in the dismantled place that has, I believe, given them a good clue. It was almost hidden under the wainscoting, and was half a gold sleeve link, and She paused suddenly. Clifford Hawke had started so violently that he had jerked her elbow.</p>
        <p>"I beg your pardon," he said quickly; "but I am suffering" from an attack of nerves, I think. I've been working too hard lately; I must knock off soon. But go on with your story." "There's nothing more to tell," she said ; "the whole thing stands at that, but it's very confusing and exciting, because I believe this policeman or detective connects the robbery with the man for whom jaspar Tennant is searching." Clifford Hawke was silent. It was a shock to learn that the man whom he hated for having been the cause of Stella's coldness to him should be so keen a favourite of Lady Mary's, and now he had another and a deeper reason for hating and wishing him out of the way. He listened to his hostess's light chatter with a pre-occupied air, his 'mind revolving round the figure of his enemy with fresh insistence, and the voice of tne woman by his side might have been the mur- mur of a distant tide on the shore for all the meaning it conveyed to him. But not by a look or a gesture did he let her see his burn- ing desire to escape from his role of honoured guest. He knew Lady Mary did net Mut Inr.n. m-4:11 this, sudden favour on the</p>
        <p>looxea at tne collar, 'l(Tfi allowing her to do so under the bribe of a piece of cake. It was a light, simple thing of silver, and inscribed on it were the words: "Fifi—a small gift in friendship's name from J. T. to S. T."</p>
        <p>The wllrm colour flooded her face as she road it.</p>
        <p>"Thank you very much," she murmured. "I shall never part with this gift—never. The other things might be lost or put away in cases, but Fifi will share my life.' "Lucky Fifi !" he muttered softly, but the words, faint as they were, reached her, and she bent her head to hide the hot blush that swept her face again.</p>
        <p>The next moment Jitspar was standing be- side her, so close that she could almost catch the hurried throbbing of his heart. "I must tell you," he said in a low, tense voice; "think of me as you like, a brute, dis- honourable, and unworthy, but I love you—l love you, and I must tell you—it's no use tffetdhia, tbmi T orthr frionelly towards you, beause you are the only woman in the world I have ever loved, and I shall love you to the end of eternity—" He paused. "That's why I envy Fifi—she will share your life, while I shall always be outside it." Then he added 'lmre quietly, "I am sorry I came; I'm afraid you will think me very• weak, but I could not resist the temptation of seeing you this last time; I am going abroad instead of into the country, and may be away a long time." She raised her face to him, and as their eyes met Tennant gave a cry of triumph. "Stella!" he said swiftly, "you do care a little—your eyes tell me you do!" "Yes," she answered in a low, but steady voice. "I do care, Jaspar, but I am weak and wrong to admit it He caught her hands in his. "It is not so weak to admit it now, dar- ling, because there is yet time to make everything right, even at the eleventh hour!" _</p>
        <p>She looked at him with shining eyes, and when he drew her into his arms she made</p>
        <p>tell everything. And ir I ntaber wire nor write--" he paused, then added, "you will know that I have failed with your father and Lady Mary. But you shall hear from me, darling, before that fatal day arrives—only death will prevent me from coming to you!' As he left the house, wrapped in the glori- ous knowledge that he had won the love of the one woman in the world, he did not notice that Some distance behind on the other side of the road he was being followed by a shadowy figure, a figure that never left him until he reached his own temporary lodging near the studio. A couple of hours later a telegram was brought to him. He opened it quickly, then uttered an exclamation.</p>
        <p>"Come at once," it began, "am in great trouble. Lottie," and the address was Commercial-road,. E.</p>
        <p>"Poor little girl!" he muttered, "I must go to her at once; I can't understand how she happens. to be in such an East End abode, though. I'm afraid I've been . neglecting my poor little protegee the last few days, but I must make up for it now. I will call on Mr. Train° to-morrow." It was nine o'clock by the time he reached the address that Lottie had put on her tele- gram and as he walked up to it, having dis- missed his cab before turning into toe street, he was struck by the particularly sordid ap- pearance of the place, even in such a, gener- ally sordid thoroughfare, and again the thought occurred to him that it was a strange place for Lottie to be staying in. As he entered the house the door was closed with a sharp bang, and he fancied that a swift figure slipped past him into the dark- ness of the passage. In answer to his re- quest to see Lottie, the dirty old woman who preceded him opened a door, and mumbling something under her breath shut the door be- hind her with a peculiar little click that fell upon his ears with an ominous sound. While he Waited Tennant mused upon the events of the day that had been to him the happiest in his life. Stella loved him, and he was certain of being able to prevent this loveless mar- riage with Clifford Hawke. "Her father loves her," he muttered; "he won't see her made unhappy, and if he re- fuses, then Lady Mary will have to help us, even if we elope !" A sudden sound switched his thoughts back to Lottie, and he wondered what it was that was keeping her from coming to him, and why she chose to stay in such a gruenome house. He looked at the door, and then uttered a cry of amazed horror. ' While he had been thinking about Stella and their love he had allowed himself to be trapped- by a fiendish ruse. Lottie was not there—she never had been—and as he realised what had happened he sprang for- ward with a hoarse shout, and flung himself against the door, battering upon it until his knuckles were sore and bleeding. Then, as he paused to breathe, a great sweat broke out on his brow, and a strangled cry rose in his throat. The floor of the room was slowly sinking I</p>
        <p>(To be Continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>BID FOR A BRIDE</head>
        <p>A BID FOR A BRIDE</p>
        <p>BLANCHE EARDLEY, Author of "Kitty Bell—Actress," "The Lady Killer," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XII. A NEW DANGER.</p>
        <p>Clifford Hawke opened his morning paper, and turned the pages quickly, his keen eyes glancing from column to column with light- ning-like rapidity, as though searching for something of importance. Presently he caught his breath and his face grew pale with excitement, though the paragraph he read was not concerned with the money market. _ _</p>
        <p>"Fire in Pimlico," it was headed, and ran: "In the early hours of the morning a fire broke out at No. 98, Claverton- street, Pimlico, iAnd within a short time the whole of the premises were gutted, in spite of the efforts of the fire brigade to save them. So far as has been ascertained, there were four fatalities, two of the lodgers on the top floor, and the landlady and her crippled mother. The cause of the fire is a mystery, but the police suspect that a lamp must have been upset by someone, for a broken lamp was found amongst the debris on the kitchen floor."</p>
        <p>Clifford Hawke smiled grimly. "So! It has been quick in coming, but though no names are given I am sure that it is all right. There will be no further fear of those damning proofs being held up now— they have gone in the fire that destroyed the house where they were treasured!"</p>
        <p>Then flinging the paper aside he finished his breakfast, only pausing occasionally to glance at a photograph of Stella that stood on the mantelshelf in a silver frame. In each room of his flat there was a similar photograph of the girl whose wedding-day could now be counted on his fingers. _</p>
        <p>"So you have taken to treating- me coldly and disdainfully," he muttered. "You hope to force me to give you back your liberty, but you don't know me, Stella—you don't know that what I want I always get— whether it is money, position, or a woman. And if I can't win your love then I must be satisfied with your obedience. I only hope that that puppy of an artist will soon be out of your way—and mine!" Half an hour later he was at the office in the City, and soon was lost in the throes of money-making, his strong, flexible brain evolving ideas and bold plans with Napoleonic generalship. When he left for his brief luncheon hour he remembered the fire in Pimlico, and called a clerk to him. "Where is Mr. Steinway?" he said care- lessly; "tell him I want to see him before I go out. "He's not come down this morning, sir!" the man answered. "They say that he might be one of the people who were killed in that fire, sir," he went on. "I believe he lived in that district." "I suppose we shall find out soon," Hawke replied carelessly; "tell someone to make in- quiries if he is not here to-morrow. It is most unfortunate he should be away, especi- ally now." But when he was alone again he dropped his cold, indifferent expression and smiled grimly. "I was right to go and call on Li Yen that day, it was a happy thought of mine. I think I might consider that I have managed this little affair very well. I shall be able to put everything behind me now and turn my thoughts back to Stella." In truth he was very much concerned about the manner in which his engagement with Stella was turning out. In his way, the only way possible for a man of his domineer- ing masterful nature, he loved her, but his I love had that tendency to brutality in it that mars the love of men who regard a woman through the senses only. He could not understand that a girl with Stella's fine principles would shrink from a man whom , she could not respect, and that anything in the light of cruelty would assuredly kill any faint spark of regard with which she might have begun her engagement. "I will buy her a present this afternoon," he mused. "I'll go and see what Spinks has in the way of pearls; she said the other day that she loved them better than anything else. All women love jewels, and they might make the coldness between us melt a little." And a cynical smile played about his thin lips. Clifford Hawke's experience of women was that their love could be bought by a sufficiently large bribe. It was late when he left the big jewellers in Piccadilly, having bestowed upon the head man a cheque large enough to pay the rent of the premises for a year, and carrying away i with him a velvet case n which there lay a string of pearls that a queen might condes- cend to wear. As he left the shop a light touch fell on his arm, and, turning quickly, he saw Lady Mary Stainer. "How do you do, Mr. Hawke? Are you busy, or can you come and have a chat and a cup of tea with a stupid old woman?" she said pleasantly. "I saw you coming out, and stopped my carriage." "I shall be delighted to have tea with you," he answered after a pause, "but I cannot agree that you are a stupid old woman, Lady Mary. One moment, while I send my car home to the garage." As he helped her into her neat victoria and turned to give his instructions to his chauffeur, Lady Mary's clever face wore a triumphant smile. Quick as lightning had come The thought that that afternoon Stella had asked Jaspar Tennant to call on her with the dog, and if she could detain Hawke with her for an hour it might keep away the fear of a meeting between the two men who hated each other already. "I hope you are not going to do anything very important," she said as he sat down by her side. "I know what you City mammon worshippers are." He laughed. "I am taking a holiday, and was just going to call on Stella and take her a few pearls, but I can go on to her afterwards," he added. "She doesn't expect me." As she looked at his hard, ambitious face, with its grim lean outline, Lady Mary was glad that she had done what she had, and for a moment she wondered whether, after all, it would not be better for Stella to break off this marriage even at the eleventh hour. Life with Clifford Hawke would not be all roses, in spite of his wealth. But she exerted her best powers of entertainment to keep him interested, and unconsciously touched on a subject that sent a thrill of startled wonder I through him. I "Yeu remember that day Mr. Tennant was showing us his picture at his studio?" she said abruptly. He nodded. "Yes. Why?" "Only because he has given up Art, and is going in for a rural life," she answered briskly. • "A very wise decision," he said grimly; "he is a protege of yours, is he not?" "Yes. His mother wag my best and dearest friend, and I promised to look after the boy. He is one of the best men in the world, she adfled inconsequently, "and clings to his purpose in life with wonderful tenacity." Clifford Hawke looked at _her shdrply.</p>
        <p>“And that purpose is---?” _ _ "To find the man who broke his sister's heart, after taking her away from her home. I believe he was an awful scamp, and left her and the child destitute in London years ago. But I am boring you," she said suddenly; "forgive me." "On the contrary," he said hastily, "I am most interested. Has Mr,_Tennant made any</p>
        <p>part of one of the lights of society had pleased him at first. At last he murmured his adieux, and maintained a cool demeanour until he left the house, and then, after walk- ing some distance, he sprang into a hansom, and gave an address that caused the cabby's eyebrows to be suddenly elevated, while he congratulated himself upon the freshness of his horse.</p>
        <p>As he leaned back in the hansom Clifford Elawke's face dropped its mask and grew hard and sinister.</p>
        <p>"I must see Li Yen," he muttered; "he must double his watching, and perhaps—who knows? strange things occur sometimes— and Mr. Tennant might find that he has no time to devote to his high and mighty ideas of reparation to the dead; his mind must be switched off on to the present moment of imminent danger in a more personal channel."</p>
        <p>...... Suddenly he uttered an exclamation of amazement, and sat forward in the hansom, staring with eyes of horror at two figures, a man and a woman, who at that moment were cresing the road by Piecadilly-circus- It was in a flash only that he saw them; the next moment he had lost sight of them, but the words that had fluttered to his lips were hoarse with fear,</p>
        <p>"Am I mad?" he muttered thickly. "I can't have been mistaken in them, yet bow can Isobel Frant be •walking up Piccadilly with the man who last night was burnt in his lodgings in Pimlico?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>DEATII OF MR. GEORGE GROSSMITH SENR.</head>
        <p>DEATII OF MR. GEORGE GROSSMITH SENR.</p>
        <p>The death took place last week of Mr. George Grossmith, senr., the versatile society entertainer. He was sixty-five years of age, and had been in a delicate state of health for some time. Starting life as a journllist, he soon found singing bis own songs v.) his own accompaniment more to his taste, and</p>
        <p>he used to perform at small concerts and parties. Being fortunate enough to attract the attention of Mr Arthur Sullivan, he was given a part in one of his operas, and was a huge success. It is as a society entertainer, however, that he will be best remembered.</p>
