      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>CHAPTER XIII. TRAPPED.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XIII. TRAPPED.</p>
        <p>" ADVICR TO MOTHSRS."—Are yen broken in your rest bye sick child suffering with the pain of cutting teeth? Go at once to a chemist, and get a bottle of MRS. WINSLOW'S SOOTHING SYRUP. It producer natural, quiet sleep by relieving the child from pain and the little cherub awakes "as bright as a button." Contains no Poisonous Ingredient. Of all Chemists 1. lid. per bottle.—Advt. CLARKE'S B, 41 PILLS are warranted to cure in either sex all acquired or constitutional discharges, from the Urinary Organs, Gravel and Pains in the back. Free from Mercury. Established upwards of 40 years. In boxes 4s. 6d. each, of all chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors throughout the world, or sent for sixty stamps by the makers The Lincoln Midland Counties Drug Co., Lincoln.—Advt.</p>
        <p>procrrees yet in his—quest?" "Not yet,,poor fellow, for some weeks ago he had all his important papers stolen by some mysterious thief. But I believe there is some chance of his tracing them." • Clifford Hawke brushed a speck of dust off bis eleeve.,</p>
        <p>Stella stood looking down on the table in her boudoir that was smothered with some of her intimate friends' wedding presents. The bulk of them had to be unpacked and laid out on the long table in the library, but these were just small offerings on the altar of friendship that had arrived that afternoon. The table glittered and scintillated with jewelled bracelets, brooches, costly fans, pieces of old china and real lace, and each with a little card attached bearing the donor's name and best wishes in pencil or pen. The last case she took up with a sigh of disgust. She was so tired of these presents that reminded her of her coming marriage to Clifford Hawke. -</p>
        <p>"I wonder who sent it," she murmured. "I thought everyone had sent things by this time.</p>
        <p>She took the case from its wrapping and opened it idly. It had just come by a special messenger, and as she opened it she uttered a little cry of amazed pleasure. Lying on a bed of blue velvet was a rope of pearls of the first water, and on a card that accompanied them were the pencilled words in her future hus- band's writing : "To my darling, from Clif- ford, hoping she will take as much pleasure in wearing them as he did in choosing them." . _</p>
        <p>She replaced the card with the pearls and turned away. If only she could care a little ! it only another face would not intrude be- tween +1,...m okino hap futilrp husband's look hard and cruel in comparison clasped her hands loosely and glanced at the clock; very soon Jaspar Tennant would come to give Fifi into her keeping, and of all her wedding presents she knew she would like the dog best—the dog that he had held in his arms and caressed ! She flushed at the thoughts that were struggling into expression in this brief spell from her constant round of calls and shopping. "I wish I had asked Isobel to stay in,' she murmured. "She would have talked and have kept my thoughts from wandering." The door suddenly opened, and a soft- footed servant announced the man who had been filling her mind since they had last met. "So you have brought Fifl," she smiled, when their hands fell apart. "I was afraid you would repent of your offer." "Yes," he laughed, putting the tiny black creature on the ground. " Fifl has been washed, combed, and honoured with a new cellar to suit the occasion, which is the greatest in her life." litella lifted the don en her knee, and</p>
        <p>but a slight resistance. rot the moment she forgot everything except that she was at Last in the arms of the man she loved. But a second later she tore herself from his em- brace and buried her face in her hands.</p>
        <p>"Jaspar—Jaspar ! You made sue forget— my own heart made me willing and glad to fnr,m+. .v.rothinz hut —aucl now I musk send you away, dearest!" His face went pale. "Then you will keep your faith with Clif- ford Hawke in spite of our love for each other? I know I have nothing to give you like he has, but I will work for you, my darling, work and fight to give you what you are giving up now." "I cannot!" she moaned sadly. "I cannot break off my engagement with him, Jaspar. I—l am afraid of him—hi 3 vengeance would be so terrible if I refused to marry him now. I can't tell you what I feel." she went on more calmly, "but I only know that I love you, and must send you away." ".A...2:11 refuie. to be sent away !" he re- joined- sWiftly; "So long as we love each other what can prevent us from marrying and defying Clifford Hawke? Other men have given up their claim to a woman who has found out her mistake."</p>
        <p>She shook her head. ,"But he is not like other men," she' said. "Oh, Jaspar, it is so hard to say 'Good-bye,' but I must!" He dropped her hands and paced the floor without speaking. He had thought that with their confession of love all other obstacles would be swept away by the tide of a new, fresh, mutual passion, but Stella was rais- ing a barrier between them again—the bar- rier of a barren honour that compels a woman to keep to the letter of a loveless bond rather than boldly declare herself in re- volt. "What is the date of this miserable marri- age?" he said abruptly. "September the 11th," she replied in a low voice, "in ten days' time. Oh, Jaspar, don't you see, darling, that I have gone too far to draw back now?" Ho caught her hands in his. "I see nothing except your love for me and mine for you!" he answered, "and no- thing else matters. Listen to me, darling," he went on quietly, "will you promise me that if I get Mr. Trainc's consent to marry you that you will tell Hawke the same day? I know Mr. Traine wants you to be happy, and it is not happiness to marry a man you can't love." "Papa would never consent," she said wearily, though her eyes brightened with a sudden hope; 'he likes Clifford, and wants me to marry him." "But he surely would not if you loved an- other man," he replied. "Anyway, darling, I let us make a bargain. If I have a satisfac- tory interview with your father I will wire you at once, and if it is bad, and I see that he will not help rne to 7:.lak,‘ you happy. then I will call on Lady Mary Stainer, and we will talk over some other way of escaping this loveless marriage. Only be brave, darl- ing, and trust to me. Better to run away even than spoil your life." She smiled wistfully as her fingers clung round his. "I am afraid I must seem a coward, but I feel that if I break off this engagement Clif- ford Hawke will prevent my ever marrying you, dear, and for your dear sake I would go on with it." "Don't be afraid," he said tenderly. "Ere cannot hurt me, darling. I am not a finan- cier, but for your dear sake I would become one even." "I hate the word finance now !" she an- swered with a slight shiver. "I would rather be the wife of a poor artist than a wealthy financier."</p>
        <p>........ "I am afraid you wiil be the wife of a poor artist for a while, darling," he answered ten- derly, "but I will paint a masterpiece, and then fortune will come to us. Now remem- ber," he went on, as a tiny jewelled clock struck the hour, "I will wire it my interview is satisfactory, and if not I will write asking you fa nail en Lady Mray..to .whoro, I shall</p>
