      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Be sure to ask for INGTON'S</head>
        <p>Be sure to ask for INGTON'S</p>
        <p>YM</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The 205th quarterly meeting of the Warrington Equitable Industrial Co-operative Society, Ltd., was held on Monday evening, Mr Wadsworth presiding. About 200 members were present. Tie committee's report and balance sheet were submitted for the half- year ending January 23rd, which showed sales for the half-year £134,718, an increase of £10,618 over the corresponding half-yr, ar, and a total for the rear of £261,193, an incteise over 1910 of £19,236.— The membership had increased by 773 on the 3 osr, and now stood at 11,455. The net balance disposal after providing for an interim dividend of 3s 2d in the £, amounted to £11,963, and it was again recommended that a dividend of 3s 2d be paid, a grant to the educa- tion fund of £219, to the employees' thrift fund £65 odd, and the balance of about £2BO to the reserve fund. Mr Chappell, public auditor, attended the meeting and testified to the sound financial position of the society. After some discussion, the report and balance sheet were adopted. Messrs Hill and Haley were elected as members' delegates to the C.W.S. quarterly, meeting for the ensuing 12 months. It was resolved that two guineas annually be sub- scribed to the fund of the Warrington Citizens' League of Help, and that 10a 6cl annually be subscribed t) the old folk's treat at Penketh. The question of granting a sum of money to the widow of one of the first mem- bers of the society, was deferred, and an amendment carried in its place, instructing the committee to bring forward a scheme for a benevolent fund from which aged members or others in financial distress, might he assisted. It was resolved that a superannuation allow- ance of 15s per week should be granted to Mr J J Fairhurst, who had been in the employ of the society for 30 years, and was unable, owing to ill-health, to continue his employment. It was decided to run trips on Walking Day night to Torquay, at an inclusive coat of 17s. 9d. and to Bourne- mouth at an inclusive cost of 20s.</p>
        <p>Vie Committee had intended that the Secretary should introduce the question of Collective Life Insur- ance, but owing to the lateness of the hour, it was decided not to bring this up, although a resolution was moved and seconded, declining to have anything to d with the scheme, but this was not put to the meetino</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>SOUTH MANCHESTER ELECTION.</head>
        <p>SOUTH MANCHESTER ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Conservative Wins by a Majority of 579.</p>
        <p>The result of the by-election in South Manchester, caused by the appointment of Sir Arthur Haworth as a Junior Lord of the Treasury (unpaid) was declared on Tuesday night as follows : Mr. Glazebrook (C) Sir A. Haworth (L) 0,472</p>
        <p>Conservative majority ..••••••• 579 This is a gain to the Conservatives. The results of contested elections in recent years are se follows : Jan. 1910. 1006. Mr A Haworth (L) 8,121 Mr A Haworth (L) 8,002 Capt. Jackson (C) 5,669 Mr L E Smith (C)... 3,770 Lib mai 1900. May 1900. Hon W Peel (C) Mr E Holt (L)</p>
        <p>Con maj At the last General Election there was no poll, because Mr. Glazebrook's nomination papers, were handed in too late. Sir Arthur offered to resign in order that a contest might take place, but:the offer was not. accepted. A Manchester correspondent of the " Daily Chronicle" states that the Liberals of the division, convinced in the great personal popularity of their candidate, did not fight with the same energy as their opponents. They could have had Official assistance in election work Irom London Members of the House, who have made a special study of the Insurance Act and were ready to assist, but were not invited to speak, or their services were not accepted when offered. One well-known expert upon the Insurance Act went to Manchester, but was left in his hotel the whole even- ing, as no meeting had been arranged for him, and he I concluded that bis services were not wanted. " I have never seen an election so inefficiently managed on the Liberal side," adds the correspondent, " while the Tory organisation was strengthened by great contingents of fighters from outside. Forty fight- ing Orangemen came from Ulster,but they devoted more attention to the Insurance Act than to Home Rule Tariff Reformers %eta discreetly silent, and declared that Tariff Reform was not an issue of the election. All the Tory workers utilised to thefull advantage the strike and the suffrage riots to criticise the Government and the Liberal candidate. There was a continued and persistent misrepresenta- tion of the Insurance Act during the campaign, and Sir W C Macara, who at the last two General Elections threw the weight of his great influence in favour of Free Trade, was foremost in his attacks on the Act. Moreover, south Manchester is peculiary a doctor's centle, for in its area are the two biggest hospitals in the city anti several large nursing homes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>RULES FOR PRUNING ROSES.</head>
        <p>RULES FOR PRUNING ROSES.</p>
        <p>1, Cut out any dead vviod and see that the cut is made in live wood. 2, Cut out any shoots that cross others. 3, Cut out very weak shoots right down to the ground. 4, Always prune to a plump, almost dormant bud that is pointing outwards. 5, Prune vigorous hushes lightly. 6, Prune weak bushes severely. 7, When roses of moderate quality are desired in abundanca for garden decoration, leave more shoots, and leave them twice as long, as you would if only a lew blooms of exhibition quality are recptired. 8, Prune severely all newly- planted roses, whether they were planted last autumn or this spring. 9, If not done last autumn, cut away the old wood from Hybrid Japanese Roses and just remove the soft tips from the firm young rods. 10, Never cut out a shoot unless you understand why it, should be removed.—' The Garden."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Parliament and Women's Suffrage.—The Coy( Bill to confer the Parliamentaty franchise on householders, will be discussed in the 11 Commons on March 22nd. Mr. Lloyd George the Albert Hall that thirteen of the twenty</p>
        <p>Ministers favoured Women's Suffrage, and be ?nu; there would be a House of Commons majority "to port it. One fourth of the Liberal party, eni,, t I fourths of the Conservative Party were opposeu,o°l That,broadly speaking, was how the two great esr."„ the State viewed this question, and, if that isi3"o.ll clear that if there is to be a majority for the e°,l3/10 tiou Bill, it must come from the Labour members. But it is difficult to see how the 1-11)th:or Commons can give a straightforward vote on tbe The Government is pledged only to give tbewi facilities if it can be amended on a democratic awl its restricted character is disliked by the and Labour advocates of women's suffrage. other hand, its Conservative friends would reso"o extension of the Bill to include married won3ev'orte with all sections of opponents united, and its stlPPtotio divided, there seems little prospect of its i.e ode progress. There may be a compromise on the I standing that women's suffrage is shelved Otiobi introduction of the alvernment Heforni Bill, veil there is some idea that the final solution may tn form of giving the vote to all men over 21, toi women above 25 years of age. An All-women Orchestra.—The Orchestra 140,011 which the whole of the 45 members are women, ggt, first performance in London this week. We hs° 0( ladies orchestras before, but in these the exectitillt the double bass, the woodwind, the brass, flit tri percussion have nearly always been men, en" hitherto been the accepted dictum that 10"8 performers were indispensable in the orchestral clot The establishment of the Orchestra Femina, that that view is a delusion, and the marked 5" e' its first public performance shows that women exPe ot no more difficulty in handling the bassoon,the the trombone, and the horn, than they have violin, the 'cello, or the harp. It is moreover 9.,,er British corporation, without any foreign perforfio In this the Orchestra Femina differs from the recceo</p>
        <p>leading orchestras, and it shows that feminine .P„, is not lacking in this country for the product!" high-class instrumental music. Co-operative Housekeeping.—Another moverhel afoot to persuade the people of the land to go co-operative Lousekeeping. When we remember 0t waste there is in keeping a large number of fires tc). dinners, which might be very well produced IP, kitchen, how much taboo; might be saved by r„ the washing of a number or families, how nreci„ and costly house space might he saved by the " common dining-roonis and so forth, we must fee sympathy N itb the movement. At the same tire cannot forget that British home life has been 1416 the good old plan of one family to one house, CC this system is the envy of many continenral peopl% have been brought up on the " flat " system. a number of German working men visited this CT; last summer, the one thing that excited their sot. Hon more than anything else was the British hoflle. have also to remetuber,as a factor in the consider: of the question, the exclusiveness of the Britisher. He loves to withdraw within the cr.°, his house, which he his been taught to regsN„ castle, and there he feels himself intrenched age' "- intrusion. ..t</p>
        <p>A Useful Hobby.—A new hobby that appeals ti of us not particularly interested in treedlewor who possess some idea of colour barmou stencilling, and to the home worker it opens up ' wide field cf interest. The cost of the outfit jo</p>
        <p>parativoly trifling since it couaists of some P63'il; few brushes and patterns, all of which may be PC,ft.t at any art needlework shop. It is astonishing be accomplished by the girl or woman who has no idea of drawing or designing, provided she a sense cf colour harmonies. Those among tie keenly artistic and can make our own designs, Wilri no limit to _our activities in this direction.</p>
        <p>and blouses of ninon, muslin and other ttansPare, serui-t,ansparent materials, lend themselves • daintiest embellishment by means of delicate t," of blossoms and leaves. Theatre and other admit of charming treatment, indeed there is ec, an acessory of the toilette may not be /06; in an original and effactive manner by the borne who is an enthusiastic adept in handling the 11 brush.</p>
        <p>Stencilling in the home—There is practicaL'o'o limit to the possibilities of this art as applied to I'gg bold decoration. All sorts of ideas will °`ll themselves to the girl who likes to make her rovio pretty and decorative as circumstances will all° some of the many advantages of this art are the it 4 and effective results to be obtained by means or the c )pe it affords for individual taste and 1"-pol Bedroom window curtains of Bolton sheetin,g,..td</p>
        <p>expensive material—may be stencilled in S "701 design, whilst a bedspread of the same fabrie decorated to match. Should the well paper be 00 tone, and not patterned, a frieze similar to thet curtains would present no difficulties to an enthtl,,o worker. Almost any flower and its foliage mekei'd able de&amp;gns, water and other lilies, magnolia bOO roses with the foliage of each flowar, fere'r.t th suitable subjects, but designs and ideas siiggeZ„ro selves constantly and herein lies the chief stencilling. oDalte The Art Applied to Childrens' Frocks.— --not original but most effective means of dee" childrens' frocks, is afforded by stencillingli patterns chosen for this purpose should be 8004 purely conventional aud are more satisfactorldi only two colours are used. Hungarian embroi°Bo be very easily simulated if rich shades of crinle°roi indigo blue are employed. Djebbabs, so univvors worn by children just, can be very prettily o°oo with stencilling round the neck or on the front, 0,11 dark colours for the material are chosen me 0.41 and durable garments may be made for wear del' spring and summer months. The Chinese Revolution.—To the German —pet the Chinese revolution suggests the' and the eventual over-running of Europe 'lel Oriental races. The proposed Chinese conscriP,r, lends colour to such imaginations, but whste'oo outcome of the resolution may be, it has had thee effect of cheapening silk and the prices of hunieod In forswearing their pigtails. the Chinese are e:/vest ing their former style of dress in favour of clothes. The well-to-do Chinese, in adoP"ral European style of dress, are giving up the silk 841,04 they have teen accustomed to wear, and it is that very much larger quantiti.a of raw 8i1;,00 exported to Europe in future. It is the same discarded pigtails, but Chinese hair is black arle unAlitable for purposes of coiffure adornment, ee Chinese pork, altogether undesirable.</p>
      </div>
