      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Penketn.</head>
        <p>Penketn.</p>
        <p>FRIFNus ScHoot„—On Monday evening last, in the Lecture Hall of the above school, a fascinating lecture was given on " Life in Tripoli," illustrated by lime- light views, by Mr George E Thompson (of Birken- head.) Mr Thompson, having spent a holiday in this part some time ago, was able to give a descriptive outline of the history and features of this interesting country, which was much appreciated by those present. The views (which were very good) consisted of street scenes, the markets, the natives engaged in the various industries, and life and customs of the Arabs travelling with their camels through the desert. Mr W E Brown (headmaster) was in the chair.—The best thanks of the meeting was expressed to Mr TbompsGn for his lecture, proposed by Miss Moorehead, seconded by Master Arthur Roberts, and heartily agreed to.—Mr Robinson had charge of the lantern.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>PENKETH WESLEYAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Suecessful Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>With the object of raising about £BO for the renova- tion of Penketh Wesleyan Church a sale of work was held in the schoolroom on Thursday afternoon. No doubt the fact that the Mayor of Warrington was to perform the opening ceremeny was responsible in some degree for the large number present. The room had been tastefully decorated by the ladies and the stalls were lavishly decked with an abundance of useful and ornamental articles. The stalls were managed as follows: Sewing meeting stall, Mrs Gunnell and members of the sewing meeting ; young ladies stall, Mrs Dodds and the young ladies; Sunday School stall, Mrs Withinshaw, and Miss Wilkinson; pound stall, Mrs Loxley •, refreshment stall, Mesdames Ambler, 'Middle- wood, Dunabin and Bell. The Rev. W. A. Beckett presided and be was supported by the Mayor of Warrington (Dr Joseph), Miss Taylor, the Rev JGunnell and Mr C E Parker. In his opening remarks the Cattirman said he believed the object of the sale was to raise about £BO for the cost of the renovation of the church and for certain improvements. They were in the habit as Methodists of getting into debt but they were also in the habit of getting out of debt. They congratulated those who were workers for the Cause on toe splendid work they were doing. It was a pleasure to have the Mayor of Warrington present with them. He bad many duties to perform and it must have been with a great deal of sacrifice and inconvenience that he was present on that occasion. (Applause). The Mayor in formally opening the sale said it was a sacrifice he made cheerfully to be there that afternoon. The country districts gave their advantages to the town in the way of pleasant walks and they in the town gave their stare in the way of shops aud nice streets. Those, however, were lighter aspects of the affair. He was always willing to accept an invitation to any gathering of people who undertook the propagation of religion. He had attended all kinds of religious functions and be felt it his bounden duty to do so as Mayor. (Applause). When the enemies of religious and social and moral reform, chiefly religious, were so powerful it behove all of them to make one grand stand against those great forces for evil. With the progress of education the evil forces seemed to have more power than formerly. Ignorance was a very gleat evil but the power of the educated masses who were determined to do evil was far greater than that of the ignorant classes. In conclusion he would say that he would like to see more reciprocity and co-operation between all districts and all bodies for that was the only way they could make satisfactory progress. (Applause.) The Chairman then acknowledged several monetary gifts, and the Rev J Gunnell, in moving a vote of thanks to the Mayor, said that was the first occasion, whilst he had been there, at which the Mayor had been present. It was a great honour and a pleasure to have him with them. (Applause). Mr Parker in seconding said they were under a deep obligation to the Mayor for coming out to help them In that village, (Hear, bear). It seemed to him in times like the present when a condition prevailed which seemed to shake our industrial progress as a nation to the very centre and brought about conditions which threw a great deal of responsi- bility on religious bodies that they should all combine as one and help each other, and honour the men who were in the midst of it. (Hear, bear.) Whatever was before them it was satisfactory to know that they had a gentleman, like the Mayor,in the responsible position be held. It was very kind of him to come there and I help them in the way he had done. (Applause.) Arrangements for a series of entertainments by local pierrots had been carried out very creditably by Mrs Stevenson and Mr Foden, and the following persons gave of their hest to the delght of the company present: Misses Dolly Baker, Marjorie Dodds. Maggie Routledge and Beatrice Moston, and Messrs Wilfrid Foden, Jim Hughes, Harry Carman. Hubert Shaw, Walter Foden, Albert Bates, and Victor Owen. Mrs Stevenson per- formed the duties of accompanist. The sale proceeded in a brisk manner and the pro- ceeds amounted to about £lOO. Grappenhall. PARISH COUNCIL MEETING, The usual monthly meeting of the Grappenhall Parish Council was held on Wednesday evening in the council schoolroom. M 1 F R Roberts presided and there were also present Messrs A Lister-Kaye, .1 Carter, J Bradford F Fox, Dr Hutt, N Cock, jun., WE Ashton and Charles H Honk (clerk). CLERK'S REM USER ATION. Mr Hoult, assistant overseer and clerk,applied to the Council for an amount to cover the cost of postage and out-of-pocket expenses. Mr Hoult stated that since his appointment, the work of the parish had increased very considerably. Over 80 houses had gone up, the meetings had been altered from quarterly to monthly, and he had also had to attend ertra meetings at Dutton. All the extra cost had to be taken from his salary, and consequently this had been reduced. Mr Kaye pointed out that the clerk had had an increase of salary, and he understood that it was with the intention of covering postages and out-of-pocket expenses. He admitted that Mr Hoult did well for them, but the work had been reduced somewhat by compounding the rates. Mr. Fox moved that the Clerk be allowed extra for postage and out-of-pocket expenses. The work had increased and the clerk was deserving of the increase asked. There was no reason why he should be asked to reduce his salary by paying these extra charges. Mr. Carter seconded and the Chairman declared it carried unanimously. OVERSEERS' ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>charity and Mrs Bratt's charity. He stated that an annual statement should be in the bands of the 'chair- man of the parish, and yet they failed to get any information on the subject. Mr Kaye: Mr Jenkins know quite well that we have had this question up many times, and we flnd it impossible to alter the conditions. Mr Jenkins asked the Chairman if be could inform them, as a member of the Education Committee, what had been done with the £:100 left by Mrs Brett. The Chairman ; I have only had the opportunity of attending one meeting of the Education Committee since my appointment, and I am sure that in that time I could not give Mr Jenkins any information on the question. Mr Lofthouse : I think we may take it that our Chairman will only be too glad to give us all the information possible on those questions, when he is in a position to do so. It is hardly fair to ask questions before one has had the opportunity of investigation.</p>
        <p>TICK SEWERAGE S6IIEME. Mr. Leach said he was sure that the thanks of all the ratepayers were due to MrKaye for undertaking the work of raising a fund with a view to a less costly scheme. He stated that if further concerted action on the part of the ratepayers of the three parishes was persisted in, much of the burden might be taken from the shoulders of Mr Kaye. With a view to this, Mr Leant' moved "tkat it be a recommendation to the Grappenball Parish Council, to invite another joint meeting of the three parishes (Latchford Without,Thelwall and Grappenhall) to further consider the scheme. Mr Fox seconded and it was carried. Mr Kaye stated that he was doing this as the duty of an overseer of the parish.</p>
        <p>Overseers' accounts for the half-year ending September 30th, 1911, were read and showed a balance in hand or £383 48 5d on account of Poor Rate, and a balance in hand of £27 3s on special rate account. THE SEWERAGE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>The Chairman pointed that as a Council they would have to decide whether they should endeavour to carry out the instruction given at the ratepayers' meeting held on February 27th. He (Mr Roberts) understood that Mr Lister-Kaye was already raising a fund with a view to obtaining further expert opinion as to a revised scheme at a less cost. Mr Kaye : That is so. My opinion is that we may he able to adopt a scheme at a oost equal to about a sixpenny rate. The Chairman : Seeing that we have no power to spend the rates for the purpose of opposition to the scheme, we had better decide as to what course to adopt. On the Chairman's recommendation, it was decided to do all in their power as a Council to modify the original scheme. ANNUAL PARISH MEETING.. . .</p>
        <p>At this juncture the meeting was resolved into the annual parish meeting, Mr Roberts presiding. Mr It Burrows raised the question of the footpath going through Mr Parr's estate. He stated that Mr Parr had no legal right to close certain paths which had been closed. Mr Waring, as a member of a Footpaths' Committee formed under the late council, stated that he along with other members of the committee, had seen I documentary proof that the footpaths in question had been legally closed. Mr Kaye supported these remarks and stated that the paths had been closed by order of Quarter Sessions 80 years ago. The footpaths were closed only once a year on the Ist May.</p>
        <p>CUARITIES.</p>
        <p>Mr Jenkins opened the question of local charities in the pariah. He asked the chairman how many parochial charities there were in the pariah? The Chairman : I really cannot tell you. You may as well ask me how many flies there are in the parish? (Laughter). Mr Jenkins then raised the question of Johnson's</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March, 9, 1912.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
