        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Daresbury.</head>
        <p>Daresbury.</p>
        <p>THE RIFLE Cum.—The annual meeting of the Rifle Club was held in the Sessions Room on Monday even- ing. There was a large attendance, including the Revs T T Lee-Jones, and W S Adams. Messrs R I Wynne- Jones, C Shepherd-Cross, C H Tinsley and W .1 Bleckly, Mr J E Seeker presided.—The Hon Treasurer (Mr Shepherd-Cross) produced the statement of accounts showing a balance in the hank of £4 18s 3d, and it was was approved.—The Secretary presented his report which stated that the season of 1911 had been a most successful one. The crack shot, Mr J Grosvenor, brought the club into prominence by making top score for Cheshire in the second round of the Queen's Cup !Competition. (Applause.) At the Egerton Cup competition in July, eight members of the team won prizes to the value of £2. Ihe club secured third prize in the competition for the (Cheshire) President's Cup, and finished second with Macclesfield in the Summer Postal League, The following officers were elected : President, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart, C.V.O. ; vice-presidents, the Rev T T Lee-Jones, Messrs J E Seeker, and J J Whitley; hon. secretary, Rev W S Adams; hon. treasurer, Mr C Shepherd-Cross; committee. Messrs H Potts, sen, T Speed, 8 Whitlow, I Wright, W J Bleckley, C H Tinsley, A de Prez, J Ravenscroft, C Ryder and B Ryder. It was decided to establish a Boys' Club this year between the ages of 11 and 14.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Lymm.</head>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>LECTURE OE " SEEDS." —On Monday night in High LBO Schools a lecture under the auspices of the local Agricultural Society, was given by Mr. Garton. of Lynne. The subject was that of" seeds "introducing, wheat, oats, clover etc, end was illustrated by lantern views, under the operation of Mr. W. T. Moss. DR. RANARDOE'S HOMES.--The enthusiasm on behalf of Dr. Banardoe's Homes was further heightened at the Grammer School on Tuesday night, when a most interesting lecture on the work of the Homes was given. Lantern views adding to the interest were manipulated by Mr. C. Moss.</p>
        <p>OUGHTILINGTON CIIUROFE,-Ith 0 G Smith. Mna.Bac. has given several organ recitals at St. Peter's, Onghtrington, during the season in aid of a fond for providing hymns hooks for the church. On Thursday evening we were plea.ed to see ths largest audience of the season and as Mr Smith was at his best the evening was a very enjoyable one. The programme was indieionslv wAriflrl and comprised characteristic pieces by Batiste, Mansfield, Wiegand, Grey and others.</p>
        <p>Ting COMING COUNCILEULiCTION.--Tn connection with the forthcoming Urban District Council election there are prospective contests in all the three wards whose representative's time of service expires. In Booth's Hill and Heatley Wards, Messrs .1 H Holt and T H Warburton, the respeotive retiring councillors, we believe are rn he opposed in their efforts to seek re-election. In Lymm Ward Mr R TI Gotohed, the retiring Councillor. is to be opposed by Mr F E Evans. At this election Mr Gotobed, though a Liberal in politics, will endeavour to be re-elected as a non-party man, it being his belief that politics should not be introduced into Council matters.</p>
        <p>MISERY GUIID.—A special treat wma given to the members of the Lymm Wesley Gnild on Monday even- ing when Mr C Wilcockson. of Stockton Heath, kindly gave them a lecture on " Wildest Wales," illustrated by some beautiful slides of his own making, which proved his great artciency both with the camera and the art of lantern slide making. Mr Wilcockson took his audience on a cycling tonr through some of the beautiful spots in Wales by way of Beaton Castle, Chester, on through Llangollen, Bala, Dolgelly to Abardovey. then round to Herlech, Criccieth and hack through Conway to Lymm. It was a delightful trip and the nicturee were se real that one could almost smell the pines and hear the surging of the waves. Mr William Moss manipulated the lantern. Tins COAL STRUM—DOW as a topic of conversa- tion the great event of the dav—the coal strike—has.so far, touched Lymm but slightly. Some little incon- venience has of course naturally followed as the result of the restricted train service,but husineasmen,reali zing the temporary character of the change, have cheerfully adapted themselves to it. The seating capacity of the coaches is severely tested on the morning and evening trains, and though this is taken In good part by the travelling public we think the Railway Company might, without over-taxing their ' resources provide a little extra accommodation. Owing to the foresight of our Council, we understand our gas snooty will be equal to all reasonable A.mrincla we may make upon it for some time to come. This is a pleasant reflection, especially in view of the curtailments we hear of as necessary in less favoured localities.</p>
        <p>LTMM AND DISTRICT WOMEN'S LIBERA.L ASSOCTA- TION.—The final social evening of the above organiza- tion was held in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom on Thursday evening Mr. James Radcliffe of lleatley occupied the chair. Mr A Andrews opened the musical portion of the programme with a violin solo which was deservedly encored. Mr Seddon came next with a song entitled "Take a pair of sparkling eyes." Miss Elsie Partington, an old favourite was in splendid form, her song " Come sing to rue" being an well applauded that she responded with "Daddy." Mr Seddon oppeared again, only on this ocoasion as al elocutionist, his rendering of "The Revenge" being excellent. During the interval refreshments were banded round. After another violin solo by Mr Andrew, Mr Seddons sang "Nirvans."Miss Elsie Partington then favoured the audience with an artistic interpretation of "Good-bye." A humorous recital "Babies " was productive of Con- siderable mirth. There was a good company present. The motel votes of thanks terminated a very pleasant evening.</p>
        <p>BarTtsT TF:4 MBE:TING.—In connection with the Higher-lane Baptist Church the " Busy Bees " held their annual missionary tea and meeting on Wednesday last. A large number of persons sat down to a splendid tea disnensed by the ladies of the church. There was a capital exhibition of articles, both useful and ornamental, all made by members of the Society, and reflecting great credit on their skill, some of thwe were disposed of in the course of the evening and the remainder will be sent out to one of the Baptist China Missions.---In the evening Mr. Sutton Smith, a returned missionary from the Congo, gave a vivid and interesting lecture on "For Christ at Yakut's." The lecture was illustrated by a large number of limelight views from excellent slides of Congo subjects, Mr. Charles Moss being the lantern operator. Miss Owen in her annual report announced that the work of the year was most encouraging and satisfactory. 14Tuch regret was felt at the unavoidable absence of Mr. Parser through ill-health and hearty wishes were expressed for his speedy and complete recovery. After a vote of thanks to the lecturer had been enthusiastically carried the gathering was brought to a close by singing the Doxology. Mr Clarke ably carried out the duties of chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>NO MEN NEED APPLY.</head>
        <p>NO MEN NEED APPLY.</p>
        <p>A WOMAN'S UNUSUAL WAY OF PUBLISHING</p>
        <p>A BOOK.</p>
        <p>Having a message to women alone—a message so delicate and personal in character that she would not like it to fall into any hands but women's—The Lady Manager, c/o Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., 42a, Hampstead-road, London, N.W., has adopted the unusual course of refusing copies of her book, except to women. To them she sends it free of all charge in s private envelope post paid. Mrs. Pinkham has had a larger number of confidential letters on the subject of female ailments than any other person in the world's history. Thanks to her enormous experience she learned that the sufferings which from time to time afflict nearly every woman can be ended, safely and with great benefit to their general health. This is effected by means of a mixture prepared at first from the herbs of her own countryside, and now obtainable under the name of Mrs Lydia E Pinkham's Vegetable Compound from all chemists, or post paid at 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. per bottle from The Lady Manager, Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., 42a, Hampstead-road, London, N.W. Every woman should write for the private book above named.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held at the Town Hall Earlestown, on Monday. Mr. W. Thompson presided and there were also present :—Messrs C Horton, W Collingwood, E T Evans, E b Bent, J Sankey, Dr Latham, W Goslin, W Davies, J Iligham, T Ritchie, M Seton, and C Cole (clerk), A Bowes (surveyor), and R T Surtees (gas and water engineer). The gas stokers were given an increase of is. per shift, owing to the use of slack, and other requests from ,the gas-workers were left over. It was decided not to increase the price of coke at present. It was also decided to advertise for an assistant in the Surveyor's department for two years at a salary of £7O, rising to £75. The Council had before them a formidable list of new streets which it was decided to sewer, level, pave, flag, channel, and make good.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>LORD IVOLNIER'S IDEA OF AVOIDING STRIKES.</head>
        <p>LORD IVOLNIER'S IDEA OF AVOIDING STRIKES.</p>
        <p>Lord Wolmer, M.P. for Newton Division, speaking on Saturday night at a Conservative smoking concert at Haydock, said striking was a very wasteful and a very horrible way of settling their differences, and the misery caused by a strike or lock-out could be avoided if some form of arbitration could be devised—some common-sense method of settling differences by com- promise and re-arrangement. The difficulties and troubles we had got at present showed that there was something wrong in our system, and he believed we should never get the question of wages settled or in a way to be settled until we had Tariff Reform in England. (Applause.) He said this because it stood to reason that if the foreigner was to be allowed to bring his goods into the country and undercut British workers at every turn, his doing so must keep wages ow. (Hear, hear).</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN EISTEDDFOD. The yearly eisteddfod under the auspices of the Earlestown Welsh people took place on Saturday in the Earlestown Town Hall. Lord Wolmer, M.P., and Lady Wolmer, and Mr J H Profit (London) were the patrons. Mr Arthur Venmore (Liverpool) presided, and delivered an appropriate address. The Rey William Roberts (Liverpool) acted as conductor. The musical adju- dicator was Mr G W Ilughes,G and L.T.Sc. (Liverpool), and the action song adjudicator Mr D James (Warring- ton), Madame Dellie (Earlestown) acting as accom- panist. The awards were : Challenge solo (soprano or tenor) : Miss Edith Evans (Cefn). Mixed chorus: Leigh Wesleyan. Action : Miss Morris's party (Earlestown). Challenge solo (contralto or bass), Mr William Hughes (St. Helens). Male choir (" The Night March ") : Three choirs entered, and after a keen competition Highfield Choir (Runcorn) won. Harp solos rendered during the evening by Miss Freda Holland (Birkenhead) and pennillion singing by " Ap Elwy " (Birkenhead), added to the success of the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>MR. J. A. SEDDO'N, M.P., AND LABOUR UPHEAVALS.</head>
        <p>MR. J. A. SEDDO'N, M.P., AND LABOUR UPHEAVALS.</p>
        <p>ONE RESULT OF THE FISCAL CONTROVERSY</p>
        <p>Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P. for the Newton Division, speaking at a meeting of workmen on Sunday morning in Griffiths' Picture Theatre, St. Helens,said the trouble was not due to the work of what were called agitators ; the causes of unrest were deeper and more widespread than that. It was an upheaval that could be traced to two causes, and it was the result of the tearing raging propaganda of Tariff Reform during the past seven years, and the work of the Free Traders in reply. The arguments on both sides had been listened to by the workers and he had been thinking a lot. Then the schoolmaster had been abroad, and the statistics and facts that had been put forward on both sides had shownthe worker his position, and each individual worker had come to understand that he was worth more by two-thirds than he was getting. Out of every shilling fourpence came to them and eightpence went to the other fellow. Consequently, they had seen during the past ten years that the number of men employed had grown very largely, and, on the other band, the classes were growing in luxury and extravagance. The workingmen ought to thank the Tariff Reformer and the Free trader for the education he had given them on the questions of personal worth and wealth production. The great unwashed and the submerged tenth had received the education that had enabled them to understand their position. If the miners' question was not settled in a fortnight they would be taking a leap in the dark. He believed it would be settled within the fortnight, and settled satisfactory to the miners ; in fact, he was certain that if they would stand together there was no power on earth could beat them. They were the masters of the situation. Once they won the principle of the minimum wage the first round was to the miners, and afterwards it would be up to the whole of the industrial army to formulate their demands for a minimum wage, and the general position of labour would be improved tremendously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUM TOO WATERY.</p>
        <p>James Ind, licensee of the Wilson Hotel, Bridge- street, was summoned at the Runcorn Police Court on Monday, for having sold half a pint of rum which was thirty-nine degrees under proof and fourteen degrees more the., was allowed by the Act.—For the defence it was admitted that, owing to illness of defendant, his daughter had had to conduct the business in the mean- time, and she must have made a mistake in breaking the rum up. Ind had been a licensee for nearly thirty years, five of which referred to the Wilson Hotel, and he had never had a conviction against him, although he had been frequently vbrited by the inspectors.—rhe Chairman said it was a very serious offence, but after taking into consideration what Mr Hough had said defendant would pay the costs (Bs.6d).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>PUBLIC HOUSES REFERRED FOR COMPENSATION.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC HOUSES REFERRED FOR COMPENSATION.</p>
        <p>Major Waring and Mr. J. B. Aitken occupied the Widnes bench on Tuesday morning when the police objected to the licence of the Manchester House, Wellington-street, in the occupation of Mr. Shannon, on the ground of redundancy. Mr. Lindon Riley represented the licensee, and said that he had been in possession of the house for forty- three years. The turnover for the last three years was £1,523. The house was well kept, and the only objection the police had was that It could not be properly supervised by them. The bench decided to refer the house for compensation The objection to the licence of the Railway Inn, Sadler-street, was upheld, and the house referred for compensation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>"The Arcadians" is making a third visit to War- rington, and recording a success which must be gratifying to the management of the Hippodrome, who are staging a musical comedy of this note for the first time. The production, which is in the hands of Messrs. Hart and Moreton, compares well with its predecessors, and all the familiar and popular roles are in thoroughly capable hands. Mr. Statham Staples makes a most humorous Simplicitas, and whether in Arcadia, or at Askwood or London, he keeps the audience in roars with grotesque pleasantries. In the last ' act lie introduces a new song and chorus " Picca- dilly," which goes with a fine swing and earns repeated recalls. This number is a decided acquisition to the many musical excellences of the comedy. Much of the success of the " Arcadians " is always dependent on the role of Peter Doody, the melancholy, but mirth-provoking jockey. In the present company Mr. Gilbert Childs, though fresh from America, follows most successfully in the foot- steps of Messrs. Alfred Lester and Alfred Beers. He had a style of his own which, though reminiscent of the earlier men, finds full expression, particularly in the great song "Always merry and bright." Mr. Childs " brought the house down" in this popular item and the recalls were too numerous to be counted. Sombra, the leading Arcadian, maiden, is charmingly impersonated by Miss Kitty Peel, whose singing in "The Pipes of Pan," and "In Arcadie " charms every ear with its flute-like perfec- tion and purity of tone. Miss Dorothy Clancy is fully up to the standard of Miss Peel in the companion role of Chrysea. Mrs. Smith, the cockney " lidy " with sentimental interest in Peter Doody is always a difficult part to play without venturing too far in the realms of burlesque, and one's praise for the efforts of Miss Gillfillan is cordial and merited. She hardly equals the perfect characterisation of Miss Aylward in the rUle, but she lacks nothing on the humorous side and makes the most of the many comic interludes with Simplicitas and the Jockey. The brightness and charm of Miss Hetty Critchley as Eileen Cavanagh are an ever-pleasing feature. She scores heavily with the fascinating song "Oh my, stop your philandering," and its accompaning dance, and joins very prettily in the duets with Jack Meadows, a dashing suitor who is played in finished style by ' Mr. Harry Clarke. A word should be said for the • chorus, which in addition to youth and beauty, has a pleasing capacity for concerted singing. The augmented orchestra does capable work under the control of Mr. W. Damant, and the scenes are mounted ' with a brightness and completeness of detail which add to the pleasures of the whole performance.</p>
