        <p>WARRINGTON MALE CHORAL UNION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Forthcoming Concert in Parr Hall.</head>
        <p>Forthcoming Concert in Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>Quite recently the two Warrington Male Choral Societies—" The Apollo" and "The Male Voice "-- , decided to join forces—a step which was taken with the idea of creating a body of singers numerically and vocally strong enough to deal with the exacting diffi- culties of much of the modern music written for male voices. Both choirs have previtoisly made history in the particular branch of vocal music to which they devote their energies, and both have been well known in competitive work as societies to be reckoned with, so that, by amalgamating, the prospect is securea that a society will be built up which will compare favourably with the best choirs of the North of England—choirs which at present hold a leading position in the country. The joint society—to be known as The Warrington Male Choral Union—will give its first public performance at a concert which, as will be seen from an announcement in another column, will be held in the Parr Hall next Thursday evening. The items to be sung by the Male Choral Union include some old favourites as well as some new pieces, and amongst them opportunity should be found of testing in an all-round way the possibilities of the new combination. Three solo vocalists of known ability and standing—Miss Florence Morden, Miss Nellie Anderton and Mr. Norman Allin are to sing, and the Barton string quartet, led by our talented young townsman, Mr. Bradford Barton, will discourse some classical examples of what is known as chamber music, which should give pleasurable variety to the programme. The conductors of the Male Choral Union are those aaached to the old Male Voice Choir —Mr. S. Hassall and Mr. W. S. Nesbitt—the accom- panists, Mr. F. Atkinson and Mr. E. Bennett North being also drawn from the same source.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>HIS PAINS OF TWENTY YEARS' STANDING REMOVED IN A SINGLE NIGHT.</head>
        <p>HIS PAINS OF TWENTY YEARS' STANDING REMOVED IN A SINGLE NIGHT.</p>
        <p>FREE PRESCRIPTION DID IT.</p>
        <p>Mr. 11. Williams says: "Had anyone told me that chronic pain could be relieved so quickly I would not have believed it. I suffered from severe Sciatica and Rheumatism generally for twenty years, hardly ever being free from it. The pains were sometimes excruciating. All these were cured through a free prescription which T am pleased to give anyone. Go to the nearest chemist's shop and get GO grains of Kephaldol in tablet form. Take two tablets at once and then one every hour until the pain is relieved. My doctor tells me that it acts just as quickly in Lumbago, Neuralgia, Headache, Neuritis, etc. I saw a very severe case of Influenza cured completely in less than twenty-four hours. I understand that this marvellous remedy is used in all the best hospitals of Europe, its chief virtue being that it acts promptly and; is absolutely harmless. Advt.—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>INDE PENDENT ORDER OF RECHABITES.</head>
        <p>INDE PENDENT ORDER OF RECHABITES.</p>
        <p>Union Tent 254. The quarterly meeting of the above Tent was h eld in the Bold-street school on Monday evening last, presided over by Bro A Mansell, Chief Ruler, who was supported by a good gathering of officers and members. After the formal opening of the meeting, Bro J Hamblettk juvenile superintendent, explained that the challenge trophy, the property of the Warrington district, which adorned the table, had been won by the" Union's Pride Juvenile Tent for the largest increase during 1911, the increase being at the rate of CO per cent. After the reading of the minutes, er.c., the Chief Ruler called upon Bro J Hamblett to make a presenta- tion of a fountain pen to Bro J Charlesworth, in recognition of services rendered as treasurer to the juvenile section. Following on this interesting event, Bro B T Parker performed a similar duty in offering to Bro Geo F Rice a silver tea service as a slight recogni- tion of his services as treasurer to the adult branch for a period extending over nine years. The presentations were made in choice and felicitous terms, and the members to whom they were made, responded in similar manner. After this business had been dispensed with Bro Parkes presented his report as representative to the District Council. which showed active progress being made both in respect of the membership, financial and Temperance sides of the work of the Order. Bro. S. FERNLEY, secretary read correspondence and made some important statements in respect of the Order to the State Insurance schemes. The Order has. taken steps and applied to be recognised as an approved society and had commenced a vigorous campaign to add to its already large membership. A long discus- sion took place on the subject, in which Bro C S Fellows,P.D.C.R. and Bro T Massey, District Treasurer, took part. The Board of Directors are at present busily engaged in revising the general rules of the Order, and in amending the tables of contributions and in other ways seeking to place the Order in the most advan- tageous position possible to carry out the administration of State benefits in conjunction with its own or separate as the members may elect to have them. Their pro- posals will be submitted to a special High Moveable Conference to 65 held at Leicester on Whit Monday and Tuesday next. All total abstainers, not hitherto identified with a Friendly Society will be wisely advised if before making their selection as to the society through which they will have their State Insurance administered, they will make full inquries and give due consideration to the advantages and security offered by the Independent Order of Rechabites.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>BEECHAM'S PILLS.</head>
