      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>SHEFFIELD PICTURE FRAUDS.</head>
        <p>SHEFFIELD PICTURE FRAUDS.</p>
        <p>Warrington Councillor's Evidence in a Douglas</p>
        <p>Some intereeting local evidence was given in a case heard at Douglas last week in which Paul Wolfgang Brunt (aged 21), of Warrington, was sentenced to three months' hard labour for obtaining mousy by false pre- tences. Brunt, it was alleged, sold to a Douglas second- hand dealer and to a grocer black and white sketches of his own, which he represented to be by the late George Sheffield. .</p>
        <p>Councillor Thomas Birtles, of Warrington, said he was a retired artist and a fellow of the National Society of Photographers. For many years he carried on a fine art business in and about Warrington. George Sheffield was a personal friend of his.Re was in Warrington for ten years and then he went toManchester with his father. He frequently executed works at his (Councillor Birtles's) home. A great number of his pictures bad passed through witness's hands and he had about forty of them at present. He knew Sheffield's work as well as any one. He knew his mode of work. Sheffield made scores of pictures in his house. The picture produced was a very dreadful example of Sheffield's. No one would buy it but for the name. In his opinion, none of the pictures in the case were Sheffield's. George Sheffield was the greatest black and white artist of his day and there was none like him to-day. He had his spells of irregular work—he could never have drawn the boat pi,Aure produced. He had been on the sea. Sheffield had four daughters—Maud, Ruth, Ada and Florence, hut to witness's knowledge there was no Georgina Sheffield. By Mr Crellin (for the defence) : Brunt had a good idea of drawing but he could never have seen Sheffield's work. Sheffield would have dirtied his picture and then rubbed it out. Witness illustrated by rubbing charcoal over drawing paper and then, with a white paper, thy point working the black off. Witness was sorry to see Brunt in that position. Sheffield " sized " his pictures so as to prevent them rubbing out. Witness had " sized " scores for him when he was too lazy. The pictures produced were not " sized." Later on Councillor Birtles, in answer to a private question whether he did not know that one of George Sheffield's daughters known as Georgina turned pictures out by the score, and signed them " Geo or George Sheffield," replied" That was Florrie." Detective Woollacott, of the Warrington police, said he bad known Brunt for a number of years. He formerly worked as a student at the School of Art in Warrington It came to witness's knowledge that Brunt bad been working black and white drawings and he spoke to him about them. Witness told him he was signing them as George Sheffield and then disposing of them to various people in Warrington and district and also in Manchester That would be eighteen months or two years ago. Brunt admitted he had been doing this. He said he had sold some pictures as George Sheffield's to a War- rington dealer who sold them at a good price. Witness cautioned him and he promised not to do it again. There were no proceedings. The people who had bought the pictures were in good positions and would not come forward to prosecute. Fanny Mather, IS, Hood-lane, Warrington, stated that on January 23th, she took Brunt in and gave him a home. He worked in her house and paid her 2s. a day for his food. The three pictures produced were done in her house. They were not signed. Cross-examined : She did not say they were the same pictures, but they were like them. They were i framed in the same way. Brunt framed pictures n her I house. She had treated him as a son and had always found him a good lad. She would take him in again if he got clear. She considered he had been acting under the influence of someone else. Mr. Crellin contended that as the defendant had not warranted the pictures he was not liable. It had not been proved that they were not Sheffield's. The Magistrate : There is Mr.Birtles's evidence. His worship added that he was satisfied from the evidence of Mr.Birtles and Detective Woollacott that Brunt had been in the habit of representing a " G. Sheffields " Pictures which were his own.lle believed the detective's evidence because Brunt bad not gone into the box and contradicted it. It was stated that Brunt had been convicted of false pretences at Southport and Blackburn and had served terms of imprisonment. He was released from prison in January. Sentence of three months' hard labour was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Warrington Photographic Society.</head>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Society.</p>
        <p>One of the most gratifying things this session has been the number of evenings filled up by our own members, Tuesday evening wes spent in Lincolnshire with Mr. W. Burrell, the hon. curator and lanternist to the Society, and rarely are the pictures and material of a leeture so well blended. The towns of Boston and Grantham, and the city of Lincoln were the scenes visited, and the advantages of cultivating mutual acquaintances amongst fellow photographers is illustrated by a fact narrated by the lecturer. He had visited Lincoln many times, and thought he knew the city almost to perfection, but the secretary of the Lincoln Photigraphic Society easily convinced him that lie was only just beginning to know it, so many new and picturesque scenes did he reveal to him. Mr. Brown occupied the chair and .!'.O members were present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Evelyn-street Headmastership</head>
        <p>Evelyn-street Headmastership</p>
        <p>Sir,--Mr.- Parker evades the issue very skilfully. The only argument used in disapproving of the School Management Committee's proposal was" Throw open to unlimited competition this post because industrial and commercial firms in their own businesses secure best ' men for important positions by open com- petition through advertisement." I asserted that this is exactly what the great majority of such firms does not do. That assertion neither Mr. Parker, nor Mr. Kingdon, nor anyone else has ventured to deny. The case then in so far as it is supported by such an argu- ment must fall to the grouud. If this is not logical I don't know what is. _</p>
        <p>My statement that there is no such thing as open competition was prefatory tothe statement that teachers feared the vagaries of appointing committees and with that Mr. Parker does not deal. I could give illustra- tions which justify that fear. Mr. Parker talks of the "large free areas." The areas may be large but the schools in them as a rule are small. Penketh is a case in point. The senior assistants at the maximum salary in Warrington get £l5 a year more than the amount at which the Headmaster at Penketh commences. The voluntary schools in restricted areas are rapidly diminishing in number (Roman Catholic schools excepted) so that speaking generally the Warriugton Class Teachers are condemned to perpetual assistantship unless they can look to their own Education Authority for promotion. Mr. Paiker says managers usually appoint old scholars. I presume he means old pupil teachers,but this is not anything like so general as he supposes. Of the seven men on the Silver-street staff, only one wa; a Warrington rupil teacher. Of the women it is ' different, six out of ten being local girls And it is a perfectly natural thing that parents, after the costly training of their sons and daughters, should look for a local appointment. The parents of teachers are not millionaires and in the case of girls there is always the maternal anxiety and fear aroused when the daughters have to seek employin9nt in ether towns and amonge‘.' strangers. Mr. Parker charges the Warrington teachers with lethargy. What are his proofs? I can give many to show that the local teachers are as much awake as other people. Three years ago the Board of Education offered an additional grant for a new subject. I believe that the first schools in England to earn that grant were Warrington schools, at any rate I am sure they were among the first. During the past winter the teachers have formed an educational association and have brought at their own expense, expert lecturers from Liverpool, Huddersfield, Birkenhead to lecture upon the newest methods in the teaching of drawing, arithmetic, handiwork, and kindergarten work. A party of Wart ington teachers this year will spend the holidays at the Summer School at Scarborough where they will spend every morning and a good many evenings in further equipping themselves for their work. Where, I ask Mr. Parker, is there another body of public servants who show such enthusiasm as to give time, money and a well-earned holiday for the purpose of increasing their professional efficiency? And yet we want "Waking up"! It may surprise Mr. Parker to know that many teachers have wanted to play "Family Coach." It may also surprise him to known that the Committee of which he is a member the school Management Committee— says that teachers shall only play that interesting game under a penalty of .£lO or £l5. In other words should a teacher wish to go to another school the committee insist upon considering it a new appointment and therefore every five years of service shall only count for one year for salary purposes, with the result that the teacher mu,t begin in his new school on a salary .£lO or £l5 less than he was getting in the old. Even 111 r. Parker would be justified in " gibbing " if called upon to play such an expensive form of "Family Coach."—Yours truly JOHN UPSON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Parker and the Cycle Trade. S r,—Mr. Parker apparently asks us to believe that because we export more bicycles than we import, the policy of " Free Imports ." is vindicated and the commerce of the country is safe. Mr. Bellian, speaking with great authority, has given a smashing answer to Mr. Parker's submission, and perhaps a further reply is unnecessary. I am, however, tempted to call attention to another aspect of the case, and to ask Mr. Parker the follow- ing simple question : Why does he cite only the cycle trade, and care- fully exclude motor cars? The answer I suggest is a very simple one. The inclusion of motor cars would have smashed his case, and so with a discretion characteristic of this " old hand" he divides a trade which I submit ought not to be divided and used only those figures which suit his case, and which I further submit prove nothing. Will Mr. Parker kindly apply his mind to the follow- ing figures for the year 1910 in respect of motor cars, cycles, and parts imported from and exported to the four principal protected countries France, Germany, U.S.A. and Belgium. Imports from £4,862,443 Exports to 859,334</p>
        <p>£4,003,109 Nett Imports. Will Mr. Parker please explain :- 1. For what particular economic reason he excludes the motor industry. 2. If our superiority in the cycle trade is due to Free Trade, why our inferiority in the motor and cycle trade is not due to the same cause? 3. Why we, who have the wonderful advantage which free imports are supposed to bring, are pulverised by the foreign countries who are supposed to be ruined by a tariff ?—Yours faithfully, THE WHOLE TRUTH.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l was delighted to read Councillor Bellian's letter in your last issue. Apart from one or two sentences, which scarcely appear to belong to the main thought of the letter, it might have been written by a Free Trader. There is a wholesome invigorating, free atmosphere about it that does one good, The Councillor emphasizes important points in the develop- ment of the cycle trade. He speaks of our manufac- turers adapting themselves to altered conditions of the trade, adopting sound finance , of the improve- ments in home made cycles, and of important inven- tions devised by Britishers. Be tells us how manufacturers have retained the excellence of their goods and developed their sales abroad. These qualities of invention, the maintenance of' good quality, the intelligent development of export trade—the importance of these Free Traders' desire to emphasize. Instead of waiting for the adoption of the questionable method of retaliation by tariffs, Councillor Bellian shows us that the cycle trade had come out on top through retaliation by brains and energy. He shows us that these qualities have beaten back foreign oompetition, reduced competing imports to a negligible quantity and developed a larger export trade, all without a tariff. If the energy which a section of our manufacturers have of late years directed to reviving Protection, had been applied to their own industries as Councillor Bellian shows has been so applied to the cycle trade, it would have been well for the trade of this country. This energy—at , least in my opinion— has been misdirected, and worse than useless, because it has created the impression that national wellbeing can be promoted by allowing certain trades to acquire an advantage by State inter- ference at the expense of the community. It will be an evil day when manufacturers depend on protective statutes for their profits instead of upon their own industry and enterprise. The tendency of this depend- ence will be to undermine the reliance which manu- facturers ought to have in themselves, and in those qualities which the Councillor's letter shows have been so effective in the cycle trade. No Free Trader, certainly not the writer, has ever contended that the advantages of Free Trade extend beyond its own sphere of influence. It will never replace brains, never take the place of energy, nor do away with the necessity of business ability and sound finance. What it does is to make all these necessary qualities more effective. Councillor Bellian says "There are, I know, many trades that are being killed by Free Trade." I am interested to hear this, and ahontd like him to mention, not necessarily many, but a few of these trades, so that we might together investigate their conditions and see whether the business ability, and brain power, which the Councillor shows us has been put to good use in the cycle trade, are operative in the trades he has in mind. _ _ CHARLES E. PARKER,</p>
        <p>The Baking Trade Dispute. Sir,—ln connection with the above dispute it must not be inferred that the wages set out on the plat- form have not previously been paid in Warrington. several of our leading firms are paying, and have paid for years, wages in excess of the arrangement. The recognition of overtime is the great point achieved ; but I leave your readers to imagine what a condition of affairs must have prevailed in some of the shops when it is necessary to cAnpromise on a basis of 30s. per week of 60 hours and night work at that. The public are the greatest offenders in the matter. In the pursuit of cheap bread they have not stopped to inquire as to the conditions under which much of it is produced. Nor are our public bodies less to blame. In considering tenders, cheapness is often the deciding factor, and they overlook the f act that an article is not necessarily cheap when irs low price is brought about at the expense of another section of the community. The mO4 pleasing feature of our agitat:on has been that we have learned how much the sympathy of the public has been roused on our behalf. They have too long been apathetic in the matter, and before long we int-nd to make further appeal to them to help us to remove another great grievance of our trade. We are hoping for the total abolition of night work. There is no earthly reason why bread should be produced by night work—indeed, commonsense points to the direct opposite. The public would be assured of better bread made by men who have a normal chance of looking after their own health. And if the statements of the doctors count for anything in the matter, the health of the public would be enhanced I have sufficient confidence in the public to hope that our appeal, when it is made, will not be in Tait]. A very little thoughtfulness is all that is required, and the thing is accomplished.—Thanking you in anticipation, I remain yours truly, FRANK STRINGLR. No. 87 Warrington Branch.</p>
        <p>. lipti , , i C • - kill lli; tA P IS to the fter4.7 (3 411-izti5...,:. I f '' WI. J i I , I's EDITOR*. ' ~ \ _ _ ,---,-------,_,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Penketb, March, 7th 1912.</head>
        <p>Penketb, March, 7th 1912.</p>
        <p>NEW LIBERAL CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>[Photo</p>
        <p>Mr. Peter Peacock, the newly-elected chairman of the Executive Committee of the Warrington Liberal Association, takes a very active part in commercial, political and friendly society affairs in Warrington. A convinced,outspoken and enthusiastic supporter of the progressive cause, he has already done much useful service for Liberalism in the borough, and his succes- sion to the chairmanship of the party organisation has given great satisfaction to the rank and file. Mr. Peacock is a native of Bedfordshire and was born in 1870. He came to Warrington as a boy and was educated at Wycliffe School. At the age of fourteen years he entered the service of the Cheshire Lines Committee and remained with them, at Warrington and Liverpool, for ten years. He next became the first sub-agent for the Great Northern Railway Co., at Warrington, but two years later he relinquished the I appointment to enter into private business as a coal merchant at Warrington and Lancaster and a stone mason and contractor at Warrington. In addition he established a railway accounts checking agency. Ultimately Mr. Peacock gave up these businesses and for the past twelve years he has been traffic manager to the firm of Messrs. Joseph Croslield and Sons. Ltd. Mr. Peacock's keen interest in friendly society work is evidenced by the fact that since the age of sixteen he has been a member of the Ancient Order of Foresters, the Ancient Order of Shepherds, and the Hearts of Oak. He has served in all the principal offices arad is at present district treasurer of the Ancient Order of Foresters. For many years it has been Mr. Peacock's cusvm to devote a week of his annual holidays to attending the High Court of the Order of Foresters, and these important gatherings have taken him ti King's Lynn, Leeds, Northampton, Glasgow Birmingham and Burton-on- Trent. He is nominated for election to the Executive Council of the Order at tbe High Court to be held in I Manchester this year. Mr. Peacock is a Freemason and holds the office of steward in the Lodge of Lights (148). He is also connected with the Frederick Bonnell Lodge of the Royal Antedelnvian Order of Buffaloes. Mr. Peacock has had experience of municipal work, having served in the Town Council successively as the representative of Bewsev and Orford Wards. He was returned for Bewsey 'Ward, in succession to the late Mr. T. Brophy, in December, 1903, his opponent being Mr. James Dickenson who stood as a Non-Trader. At the next election, however, IMr. Dickenson defeated Mr. Peacock by 30 votes. Mr. Peacock resumed his connection with the Council in November 1903 when he was elected for Orford Ward, , after a three-cornered fight, by a majority of 81. The other candidates were Mr. J. A. Rusbworth (C), and Mr. James Harnpson (S). The Conservatives brought a petition in February 1909, with the result that Mr. Peacock was unseated through the acts of his agents. Mr. Peacock has long been prominently identified with the Liberal party in Warrington and after having acted as secretary of Orford Ward for eight years and vice-chairman of the Orford and 13ewsey wards for seven years, he became chairman of St. John's Ward two years ago. lie rendered willing and very useful service on the Finance and Executive Committees and General Council of the Warrington Liberal Association for twelve years and his zealous work was recognized five years ago by his election to the deputy-chairmanship of the Executive Com- mittee. On the resignation of Councillor Wilkinson last week, Mr. Peacock was unanimously and enthusi- astically elected to succeed him as chairman. Mr. Peacock is a generous supporter of the Warrington Railwaymen's Mission and the members have on many occasion been indebted to him for enjoyable entertainments and outings.</p>
        <p>4 ~, • 7.: ::::,7.,..!,..4k-f,, ~z- ' .. *ii, , /.s. _</p>
        <p>Parkinson MR. PETER PEACOCK, THE NEW CHAIRMAN OF THE LIBERAL EXECUTIVE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WARRINGTON CRICKET CLUB. ANNUAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CRICKET CLUB. ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club was held on Tuesday evening in the Lion Hotel. Mr. W. Si!cock occupied the chair, there being a large attendance of members. The balance sheet was submitted by Mr. Lowe and showed an adverse balance of £l5 7s. 2d. Since the accounts had been closed the sum of £23 Bs. had been received, thus leaving a balance in favour of the club of £8 Os. 10d. This is the first time for many years that the club has had so much in hand,</p>
        <p>_ Considerable discussion was opened by Alderman Tinnion with respect to laying a permanent service of eater to the cricket ground, bowling greens and pavilion, with the result that the committee were instructed to at once take the work in hand, the cost of which was stated to be about EGO. The Alderman proposed that a subscription be raised towards the object in view and £lB 14s was subscribed by the members present. The following is a list of officials for the ensuing year :—President, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., secretary. Mr Charles H Unsworth ; treasurer, Mr R Lowe ; assistant treasurer and collector, Mr Ii H Lowe ; match secretary. Mr 13 Silcock ; captain of the first team, Mr W Richardson, junr deputy-captain, Mr S Partridge: captain second team Mr W B Turton; bon seri Tennis Club, Mr R T Johnson; hon see Bowling Club, Mr Thomas Cooper; and captain of the Bowling Club, Mr It T Fairolcugh.</p>
        <p>'LINSEED COMPOUND for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles. 9id.,ls. lid. Of Chemists. —Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday,</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday,</p>
        <p>PREJUDICE BEFORE PRINCIPLE. LOCAL TORIES MIRE THEIR HANDS.</p>
        <p>PEACE OR PARTY CAPITAL?</p>
        <p>If you werd to ask the best or the worst of Tories if he was anxious to see the coal strike settled, you would probably get an emphatic answer in the affirmative, coupled with an expression of willingness to help the settlement along by all the means in his power. He would be sympathetic, loyal to the best national interests, and apparently as estimable an individual as you would find in a day's march. But he might be a traitor with the tongue of treason in his cheek, for all that. The fact that there are such Tories in Warrington is regrettable, but none the less a fact, as we shall prove in a moment. There are Tories—Warrington Tories—who would shake you by the hand, while they gave the wink to another to stab you in the back. It all depends whether there was that much-prized commodity, party capital, to be made out of the dark deed. Honour and principle would weigh as nothing on the other side of the scales. For proof of this party political perfidy, the , reader has only to go to the entrance of the Conservative Club and he will find displayed to the public gaze a placard bearing the following monstrous calumny on the Prime Minister: WANTED A MAN-- WE CAN DO NOTHING.</p>
        <p>That was the burden of Mr. Asquith's pathetic speech in the House of Commons, on Monday last.</p>
        <p>ONE MILLION MEN ARE ON STRIKE. At the end of this week another Million will be out of work. In a fortnight th whole business of the country will^ stop automatically. _</p>
        <p>Starv4tioz, ruin, riot, revolution are at our door, and MR. ASQUITH WRINGS HIS HANDS, throws ashes on his head, and tearfully confesses</p>
        <p>WE CAN DO NOTHING." •</p>
        <p>On public platforms the attitude of the Tory Party is one of sympathy with the Govern- ment in a desperately critical industrial crisis for which they are not responsible. The Opposition are professedly anxious to do nothing which would hamper the unsparing efforts of Mr. Asquith and his cabinet to bring the disputants into agreement.o Only yesterday the Premier said in the House : " The nation at large, and all political parties at Westminster, have shown what is needed— patience and self-restraint."</p>
        <p>In all respects the public attitude of the Tories is irreproachable. They would never dream of attempting to make political capital out of so unhappy a situation. But what is their conduct at home? Something like that of the notorious Charles Peace, who was a dignified and diligent Christian in the day- time but a thief and criminal of the worst type at night. His memory is execrated, and his life-like presentment preserved in the Chamber of Horrors as a warning to present and future generations. There ought to be a niche in the same pace for Tories of the type illustrated. We hope they are proud of their handiwork, and we only regret the modesty--or cunning—which prevailed upon them to give the placard a limited publicity. Mr. Harold Smith's opinion on the party tactics at home would be useful. Or he might favour us with a suggestion for finding "the man," and ending the strike in true statesmanlike fashion. The gratitude of the nation and of the Premier would neither be grudged nor withheld.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>MR. W. G. C. GLADSTONE'S VISIT.</head>
        <p>MR. W. G. C. GLADSTONE'S VISIT.</p>
        <p>Our readers are reminded that the Junior Liberal Association will hold a public meeting in the Co- operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Friday evening next when the principal speaker will be Mr. W. G. C., Gladstone, ALP, grandson of the "grand old man ' and one of the most promising of the younger members of the House of Commons. Mr. E Lloyd Jones, (chairman of the Association) will preside aud the other speakers will include Mr. P M Oliver prospective Liberal candidate for the Knutsford division, and Councillor Henry Roberts.</p>
      </div>
