      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>March, 9, 1912.</head>
        <p>March, 9, 1912.</p>
        <p>LICENSING SESSIONS. UNSUCCESSFUL OBJECTION TO A LICENCE.</p>
        <p>REMOVAL OF THE" BOWLING GREEN " LICENCE REFUSED.</p>
        <p>There was some interesting business ttt the adj mrned licensing sessions for the County Borough of Warrington which were held at the Police Court yesterday morning. The magistrates on the Bench were: The Mayor(in the chair),Councillors II Roberts and C Jackson Holmes, Messrs A J Jolley, G W Roberts, J W Hayward,B Wright, W Furness, and T Lccker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>BREW ER I' OFF-LICENCE.</head>
        <p>BREW ER I' OFF-LICENCE.</p>
        <p>On the application of Mr. J. Longland, the magis- trates confirmed the granting of an off (beer) licence to Alderman W. Bolton, in respect of the brewery in Mersey-street. A LICENCE OBJECTED TO BUT RENEWED. Mr. A. Browne applied for the renewal of the licence i.f the King's Arms, Buttermarket-street, which was objected to by the police. He stated that at the annual licensing meeting he told the Bench that Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., Ltd., bad under consideration a scheme for re-building and re-arrang- ing licensed houses in I3uttermarket-street in what was known as the Scotland-road area. It was, how- ever, impossible for them to carry out the project under the present circumstances. The King Arms was owned by a well-known Liverpool solicitor who was a bachelor. He had sold his , adjoining property to the Corporation and it was an open secret that part of the consideration was that, on his death, .4-2-'l,OOO should go to the Infirmary. He was the owner, along with another person, of the public-house, and Messrs. Greenali, Whitley and Co , were only the lessees. They there- fore had no tight under their lease to offer to give up the place or to re-arrange the houses in the district, without instituting proceedings in a London Court at a cost of 5.;400 or £5OO. Were the justices, in face of of what Messrs. Greenall had done in the past, going to take aay the licence for the act of a tenant com- mitted without their consent and absolutely contrary to their wishes? Were they going to at )p a benefactor of the town ? If they did, Warrington might suffer COrsirler;ll:, There had only been one conviction ngaii.st the house. The Magistrates Clerk : Two co_:victions in one day:</p>
        <p>Mr Browne : Nominally there were two, but not within the meaning of the section. It was practically one offence.</p>
        <p>The Chief Cunstab!e : Two different offcncea on two dlerent dates.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne :Two c)nvictions on one day, according to a case wllich I could quote, only count us one con- viction.</p>
        <p>Councillor H. Roberts : But they occurred on two different dates. Mr. Bnwne : That does not matter. Councillor Roberts : You must not ask us to accept that.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne : Of course lam a lawyer and you are not. but N. ou know bettor than 1.</p>
        <p>Councillor Roberts: You may as well argue thst for a dozen offences on different dates, if they happened to be heaid on one date, there is only one conviction. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne: I say one conviction for certain pur- poses under the Licensing Act. I don't care who contradicts rue, I again assert it. CouncilLor Roberts: And I don't care, either.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne, continuing, said he could quote a case in the King's Bench Division. As soon as ever the firm were in a position to re-arrange the houses in Butterruarket-street by the purchase of the King's Arms they would do so and thus diminish the number of houses in that street.</p>
        <p>The Chief Canstable said that, in addition to the conviction, it was only fair to say that on February `27th Chief Inspector Williamson and he were accom- panying a number of magistrates who wet() visiting licensed houses. On entering the King's Arms, they found a man in a maudlin state of drunkenness. He followed the magistrates about and persisted holding a conversation with them. The attention of the licencee's wife, son and daughter was drawn to the case and they promised to get the man out of the house. Since the conviction the house had been con- ducted quite satisfactorily. No complaint could be made against the new tenant. The Mayor asked if Mr. Browne would quote the case he had referred to, with reference to two offences heard on one date counting as one conviction. Mr. Browne : I nave not brought it with me this morning. I had it here at the last hearing. The magistrates decided to renew the licence, but the Mayor said if it had not been for the proposition brought before the licensing committee by the brewers, they might have acted differently. They considered it; perfectly correct for the police to object to the licence. Mr. Browne : I have never taken the slightest objection to that course. THE BOWLING GREEN INN. REMOVAL OF LICENCE REFUSED. Mr. Browne applied on behalf of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., for the removal of the licence of the Bowling Green Inn, Liverpool-road to a new house to be erected at the corner of Powys-street, and Liverpool-road. He con- tended that the house was in a unique position as the inn stood on one side of the road and the bowling green, which was included in the licence, was on the other side. Messrs. Greenall had done everything they possibly could to reduce the number of licences in this totem Continuing, he said the transference of the Bowling Green licence to the new house would remove one of the most danzerous corners on the Manchester to Liverpool road. The matter had been under con- sideration for years but the firm were in the awkward position that they could not acquire the property on the opposite side of the road, through the stupidity of a certain lady. Tney had at last succeeded in com- pleting the purchase at a most exorbitant figure—more ' than double the value. Mr. Browne pointed out that so long ago as 1903 an agreement was entered into between the Corporation, Messrs Joseph Crosfield and Sons Lta, and Messrs Greenall, Whitley and Co Ltd, with reference te acquiring the land to do away with the dangerous corner. The new house, he continued, would cost about £3,500 but the firm would get abso- lutely nothing more out of it than they were getting from the present house When Mr. Browne was beginning his address, the Chief Constable went up to the bench and was apparently explaining the plans to the magistrates when Mr. Browne interjected in a deprecatory tone: " Mr. Chief."</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: Do you think the magis- trates would listen to anything improper I had to say ? Mr. Browne: No, but lam conducting the case at the present moment. Councillor Roberts asked if the firm were prepared to give up anything if the application were granted? Mr. Browne: Nothing. We have given up so much in times gone by and we are not doing this with any object. Councillor Roberts; You are getting a new house for a very old one. Mr. Browne: For a very good old house with which the owners are perfectly satisfied. Mr. W. Roberts: You have made very good bargains Councillor Roberts asked if the firm could not give up the licence of the Railway Tavern which was no longer required and could very easily be objected to on the ground of redundancy. It was not doing a local trade, all the business being brought to it. Mr. Browne : That we decline. It is a good house.</p>
        <p>Councillor Roberts : It is a good house and it is doing a business which possibly may be the subject of comment later. Mr. Browne We perhaps know why. Councillor Roberts: And I know why, too. Con- tinuing Councillor Roberts pointed out that the directors of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons. Ltd. did not wish the removal of the house for the purpose of acquiring the land. Mr. Browne : I did not suggest that. After the magistrates had consulted in private, the Mayor said : The Bench have given a very full and serious consideration to the application ana I regret to say it is refused.</p>
        <p>Temperance Guild. The Guild which has for ita object the promotion of temperance principles amongst the adult members of Bands of Hope and others, has held its meetings in Cairo street schools during the winter months. The programmes have been of a varied character and addresses have been given by the following: Mr I Ellson, Manchester ; Mr J J Firth, Warrington ; Mr P Taylor, Manchester ; Mr T D Leslie, Wigan and Mr Bailey, Manchester. 'Coss:Wu:fa,' Transparent Cement for Glass, China, &amp;a. 6d. and Is., poet free.—Adirt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>COAL WAR.</head>
        <p>THE COAL WAR.</p>
        <p>EFFORTS FOR PEACE STILL UNSUCCESSFUL.</p>
        <p>DISLOCATION OF INDUSTRY.</p>
        <p>The effort for peace in the coal-fields is being continued, but there is still no definite result.</p>
        <p>The Industrial Council has adjourned till next week, which means that the Prime Minister and the Cabinet Committee are again conducting the conversations direct.</p>
        <p>In the comae of a speech at Covent Garden yester- day afternoon Mr. Asquith said : Those of us who are responsible for the govern- ment of the country have for the time imposed on our shoulders a weighty addition to the load of responsi- bility, which is at all times hard enough to sustain The nation at large, and all political parties at West- minster, have shown what is needed—patience and self-restraint. (Hear, hear.) We of the Government on our part have not spared, and are not sparing, any effort in our power to bring about peace. (Hear, hear:) But, speaking for myself, I still entertain that the controversy may be settled, as it certainly ought to be settled, by an agreement based upon the principles of equity, and honourable to both sides. (Hear, hear.) The disastrous situation due to the strike in various parts of the country is intensified from day to day. From all parts come reports of the closing of works and in all the great industr.al centres thousands of workpeople have been thrown out of employment. Should the strike last much longer, great distress will prevail. The shortage of fuel has already brought about a serious interference with the various railway services. The London and North-Western Railway Company have curtailed their service about 25 per cent. ' and after Monday the reduction will amount to a total extent of between 40 and 50 per cent. The Great-Western Railway Conpany consider it desirable to further curtail the passenger and goods train services as from to-morrow (Sunday) and full particulars of the trains running can be obtained upon application at any of the Company's offices and stations. All excursions and cheap bookings will be cancelled.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire Lines Committee have considerably cm tailed their service through Central Station. During the week only half the usual number of expresses have been sunning, and half the local service has been dispensed with. The reduction will be considerably greater next week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>BLACK OUTLOOK IN WARRINGTON,</head>
        <p>BLACK OUTLOOK IN WARRINGTON,</p>
        <p>Although the effect of the coal strike has not been so immediately tragic in Warrington as in some industrial centres, the outlook is sufficiently black to give rise to grave alarm, and employers and workers alike are fervently hoping or a settlement of the trouble. Some exaggerated reports as to the extent of unemployment in the town have gained circulation and, although it is somewhat difficult to arrive at the exact position, the number of the workless fortunately does not as yet leach the 6,000 estimated in one outside quarter. About 4,000 would be much nearer the mark. At the same time the prospect is serious enough, for several of the largest works are partially closed and coal supplies in some of the others are rapidly running down, Many chimneys have ceased to belch forth their volumes of smoke and the town cannot at present be fairly described as "smoky Warrington "—some of the workers are beginning to wish that it could. Distress has not yet manifested itself in an acute form but in numerous poor homes the pinch will soon be felt, for the resourc-s of the household are not often proof against the loss of even a week's wages. Inquiries at the varioas works last evening did not disclose any very striking change in the situation, with regard to the effect on the labour world, as compared with last week-end The forges of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., at Dallam and Bewsey, closed down on Saturday, throwing about 1,300 men out of employment, but the engineering section has been . kept going. At Messrs Rviands Bros., Ltd, Church-street, between 600 and 700 men have been suspended, but it is not likely that the number will be augmented in the next fortnight. About 800 of the workmen of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Company Limited have been placed on the unemployed list through the closing of the forge, mills and part of the tube works. The Whitecross Co., Ltd., have partially closed their works, the number of men temporarily dispensed with being '250, but every effort is being made to keep the remainder of the concern open as long as possible. Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd.,have so far succeeded in keep- ing the great bulk of their hands employed, but notice has been given to all the employees that they are on daily contract. Messrs. W. D. Houghton, Ltd., Sankey Wire Mills, have been working for the greater part of the week,but last night almost the whole of the works, involving 350 men, was closed down. Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co., Ltd., the Longford Wire Co., Ltd., and the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., report that they are working as usual. The price of coal is still mounting steadily upwards, and from 255. to 30s. per ton is now being asked for fuel which a short time ago was selling at 16s. Bd. to 18s. -Id. per ton. The most unfortunate feature is that the advance tells most heavily on the poorest section of the community who had not the where- withal to lay in supplies before the crisis came to a head.. There is one cause for thankfulness, the mild weather of the past week having made it possible to exercise considerable economy in the use of fuel without undue hardship.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Strikers' Expensive Frolic.</head>
        <p>Strikers' Expensive Frolic.</p>
        <p>About half-past five on Monday morning it was noticed that a small lock-up shop near Winwick ClArch, rented by H. Talbot, an attendant at the Asylum, bad been broken into. P.C. Woof was informed, and the depredation was traced to a gang of strikers from Abram, young fellows about 18 to 25 years of age. Four of them,the ones who had actually broken into the premises, were tried at Newton on Wednesday and fined 40s. and costs each. About £2 worth of goods, chiefly cigarettes and sweets, were taken, and the tap of the vinegar barrel had been left running.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Mayor and the Catastrophe.</head>
        <p>The Mayor and the Catastrophe.</p>
        <p>Before enterinq upon the ordinary business at the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening the Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph) referred to his attend- ance ate meeting called by the Lord Mayor of London at which a strong resolution was passed, that a strike should be avoided at all hazards. He could not help thinking that such a disastrous event, affecting the national trade and the welfare andcomfort of the inhabitasts,should not he brought about by any dispute, however important it might be regarded by the parties concerned. He regretted that the efforts exerted had so far failed to avert the catastrophe but he sincerely hoped that during the next few days the outlook would become brighter. (Hear, hear).</p>
        <p>Warrington Gas and Water Supplies. In moving the confirmation of the minutes of the Gas Committee at the monthly meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening. Alderman JOHN EVANS (chairman), said the com- mittee had been anxious for several weeks to make the reserve of coal as high as possible, but for several weeks before the strike took place the deliveries were not up to the normal. However, in spite of that difficulty, when the strike took place there was a reserve at Longford of about 5,000 tons, and that roughly speaking, would enable the work of the com- mittee to be carried on for five weeks. (Hear? hear). In face of the possibility of the strike continuing, be had been instructed by his committee to ask the con- sumers of the town to be as careful as they could with the use of gas, and in order to set an example to them he had been deputed to ask the Council for economy in the lighting of the public streets. Mr. Haddock, the gas engineer, had Informed the committee that it would be possible to economise by extinguishing one burner, where two were now in use. A considerable saving would thus take place. Connelllor DICKENSON asked why the committee bad raised the price of coke from 12s to 15s per ton, as this would seriously affect the poor people. Alderman EVANS said the reason was that the com- mittee was under contract with several Isrge consumers, and if the strike continued long, they feared they would not be able to supply them. Councillor PEMBERTON Surely there is a strike clause in the contracts. This is a serious matter to poor people who use coke regularly. At a late stage of the meeting, Alderman JoIIN EVANS, in moving the confirmation of the minutes of the Water Committee, said be was also pleased to be able to report that they had sufficient coal at their disposal to carry on pumping operations for three months. The committee bad, however, under con- sideration the advisability of closing down the Winwick pumping station, and working night and day at Dolph- lane. If that course was adopted they would be able to carry on for four months instead of three. (Hear, hear). The minutes of each committee were approved.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' (Aniseed, Senesa, Squill, Tolu &amp;o.) For Coughs and Colds, Influenza, U.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>PULPIT REFERENCES.</head>
