        <p>The Duty of the Church. cb The Rev. W. T. Jaye, preaching at Emmanuel ChOro on Sunday evening from St. Matthew xi, 28-30„050 that the utterance of the words, "Come unto Me all .00 that labour and are heavy laden and I will give 90., rest," marked the pivotal point in our Lord's minis'it'f It was His new message to such as were consciousbe need. It was still His message, and to-day, when toe could think only of the great incipstrial unrest rie country, he felt that that unrest of man could fin"the real satisfaction apart from Christ. While it WO ,re duty of the Church to take sides with all who lit-be oppressed, her first responsibility was preaching Word of God. Let every man but do his duty to uvicl whether he be employer or employe, and there wcwil be an end to labour disputes. To masters St. sod said," Give unto your servants that which is just rvo equal," and his injunction to employes was to Seopri their masters "in singleness of heart . • A. rtieo whatsoever ye do, do it heartily." Masters arJ," -607 alike were responsible to God, with whom there respect of persons." He (the preacher) prayori tlti this great and catastrophic struggle there might bota speedy settlement which should be equitable to parties.</p>
        <p>The Worker's Ideals. The Bev. W. C mper was the preacher at b,-)th morning and evening services at the Legh-st_r 01) Primitive Methodist Church on Sunday. At service special petitions were offered that the Pres:, commercial and labour unrest might be brought "tbe speedy and honourable conclusion. Preaching evening Mr. Cooper spoke on the world-wida clateulje for ideals both in history and the present day.ti pointed out that all real progress had invariablY ";or, attended by strife, persecution' misunderstanding! the and bloodshed, and adduced the present position Odof coal crisis as evidence of that fact. Democracy Woe had becon.e conscious of its powers coupled with 6000 of its own worth. It had discovered that its contribugere in labour was of equal value with capital and thereitoy was now demanding fairer recognition for its coats:go tion to the public weal. He and his congregation 10:01 have different views upon the present issue, but 'ors were all agreed that it was high time that the w°,r.orit of to-day should live under more favourable coodlliiee and have fair opportunities of rearing their fs(liiste' under conditions which were not a disgrace to cis,l tile tion. The worker might make a few mistakes i° hie beat of agitation in his upward movement toward: the ideals, but that was one of the healthiest signs o` present day and of the present crisis in particular. Rev. H Ogden Supports the Miners' Deitanilos: "The coal crisis" was the subject on which the ite;.pel Ogden preached at the Golborne-street Baptist ti“ 000 on Sunday evening. At the outset the rev gentleios° said the present crisis probably roused peculiar que6tbitge in one's mind. Only the other day he had passecl,ll„jv stack of coal almost unprotected, there being ~,"-„poo` thin gateway to separate it from the public. nosde„ ; the strike continued and March came with the te' that winter was not out, and there were empty pteh, ll'' and empty stomachs, would it be robbery t° ed down the fenc