        <p>State subsidy, inevitably accompanied by State super- vision, be found necessary, it would not be without an instructive precedent. Though a sum of nearly £16,000 is voted annually by Parliament for hospitals the fact is not widely known, still less the result of the method of administration adopted under statutary sanction and now in operation for over half a century. The State-aided hospitals are in Dublin, and they are eleven in number. How they came to be in this position is an interesting story, but it is much too lengthy for reproduction here. The great point and conclusion of the whole matter is Viat the success of the Dublin scheme is beyond doubt.</p>
